-"4","sppilot_19190319","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1919-03-19,1,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19190319.2.4&srpos=33&e=-------en--20-SPNP-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""W. W. Beach, now of San Diego, was here today renewing old acquaintances. For the past year he has been connected with the Neptune Sea Food Co. in the canning of turtles. The meat is put up in various forms, including turtle soup, turtle broth and escalloped green turtle. \n The amount of meat handled amounted from one and one-half to two tons per day, some of the turtles weighing over 500 pounds each. Mr. Beach states that the product finds a ready sale in the market and that another company will be organized soon to carry on the business on a still larger scale. \n Mr. Beach lived for a number of years and at one time was engaged in the canning of lobsters at Terminal.""","2","""W. W. Beach Canning Turtles at San Diego""","cargo","green","reported","inferred",500,NA,"not_reported","""This is connected to article sppilot_19171210. Neither of them are about individual turtle observations/sightings, but both indicate the abundance and frequent capture of turtle cargo that is being shipped—specifically for W.W. Beach— for canning in the LA area. Weight given is a general maximum they give, but it also says half a ton to 2 tons per day are shipped. Additionally, no specific location for capture but given that it is being shipped to the same person as sppilot_19171210 we can likely assume baja_california and/or turtle bay/magdalena bay.""","1919",7,"high",57.46875,226.8,NA,1910,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
