-"112","spdaily_19190508","""San Pedro Daily News""",1919-05-08,1,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPDN19190508.2.6&srpos=20&e=-------en--20-SPDN-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Carl O. Nielsen and A. F. Kittle, owners of the Nielsen-Kittle company’s fish cannery at East San Pedro, have returned home from a voyage to Mexican waters that they made in the company’s craft, Necko No. 1. The packers brought back 48 turtles that they captured at Turtle bay. The largest turtle weighed 350 pounds. The turtles are to be experimented with in the making of canned soup.""","2","""CAPTURE AND BRING HOME BIG TURTLES""","48","green","inferred","known",350,NA,"not_reported","""the reported size is that of the largest turtle captured""","1919",8,"high",48,158.76,NA,1910,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
-"113","sppilot_19190506","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1919-05-06,1,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19190506.2.16&srpos=12&e=-------en--20-SPNP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""The Nielsen & Kittle cannery may begin manufacturing turtle soup in the near future as the result of a number of experiments which have been made with a catch of turtles recently made in Mexican waters. \n Carl Nielsen, A. F. Kittle, Herman Norden and E. H. Hansen of the Nielsen & Kittle Canning company have just returned on the Nicko No. 1 with a load of fifty large turtles, caught in Turtle Bay and other Mexican waters. The largest weighed 500 pounds. The boat was out twelve days. \n If the experiments being carried out prove satisfactory, the cannery may begin manufacturing turtle soup on a large scale.""","2","""Local Cannery May Put Up Turtle Soup""","50","green","inferred","inferred",500,NA,"not_reported","""the location of turtle bay is given as exact, but it also specifies that they are found 'other Mexican waters', therefore the location is inferred. The 500-lb weight given is the largest turtle""","1919",7,"high",50,226.8,NA,1910,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
-"114","stinson_sdunion_19320825","""San Diego Union""",1932-08-25,NA,"57","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""On her second visit here with a similar cargo in the last few months, the vessel Mexico, Peter Koralish, skipper, was in port yesterday with a quantity of live turtles for the local market. \n Weighing anywhere from 85-150 pounds each, the turtles numbering about 60, will bring if sold here, about six or seven cents per pound 'on the hoof' according to reports along the waterfront. The heavy shell, it was said, causes a loss of between 60 and 70 percent in the net value of the turtles to the buyer. \n No market for the turtles had been found here yesterday, according to an official of one of the large fish companies, and in the event that the turtles cannot be disposed of either here or in San Pedro, the opinion was expressed that the cargo may be taken south and dried.""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","60","green","inferred","not_reported",150,NA,"not_reported","""the location is not reported but based on context, it is likely in or around baja_california""","1932",6,"medium",60,68.04,NA,1930,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
-"115","latimes_19170329","""Los Angeles Times""",1917-03-29,2,"na","""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160361911/590BBC08B3D34453PQ/65?accountid=14026""","""Competition Established in Lobster Importing. \n New Deal Below Line Giving Hope of Lower Cost. \n Big Fines for Trout and Quail Law Violators—Turtle. \n Competition in Lower California fisheries, so earnestly advocated by the Fish and Game Commission as a means of increasing supply and thereby lowering prices to fish consumers, set in with a most business-like rush yesterday. \n Two boats came into San Diego yesterday with load of lobsters, and the three concerns now operating below the line expect to average five tons each per week once they get into action. This will mean fifteen or more tons of lobsters coming into market in place of less than five, and local dealers believe the price cannot be longer monopolized by anyone so there is a chance hereafter of the general public getting a taste of this delicacy which heretofore has been the special prerogative of the rich. \n Sunday night Deputy Pritchard took two inspectors, to San Diego, who are commissioned to go south with the boats of the importing concerns, who hold permits from the Carranza government. These officers are paid by the importers as required by law, and their duties are to certify that the provisions of the California law governing lobster importation are respected. A third inspector has been asked for by the 10th of April. \n In addition to granting permits overriding the concessionaires operating for the de facto government of Lower California, President Caranza has taken the California fish and game laws for his guidance in conserving the West Mexican fisheries, and has appointed his own inspectors to accompany the boats of his concessionaires to see that they take no young fish, use no trawlnets or other gear destructive of the future supply. Carranza has taken the ground that much of the ablest fisheries thought and study available has been spent on the California fisheries conservation problem, which is very similar, and instead of merely patterning after it, has adopted it in toto. \n Work of the concessionaires under this new competitive arrangement fostered by the simplifying of importation under rulings of the Fish and Game Commission, will extend to the fresh-fish trade as rapidly as they can get organized for it. Owing to the distances to be traveled, fast boats with refrigeration are essential to bring up the halibut, which so far have been caught mostly by San Pedro and San Diego fishermen on a 2 cents ??? [scanned newspaper is illegible] ???  … cents more has been added, so the Los Angeles trade have had to pay an average of 8 cents, and the freight. By the new arrangement the Carranza concessionaires will combine several of these profits, and prices to the public can be materially reduced. The supply is said to be greater than California ever had, and the feeding banks are better. \n The territory is divided among the Carranza concessionaires, and the floating cannery John C. Heston is now in Magdalena Bay working on turtle and oversize lobsters until the albacore began to run, when she will go to putting up tuna. Lobsters of legal size will be inspected, boiled and shipped to California in cold storage, as the distance is too great to bring them in alive: but live shipments from closer points are now being made. \n The former controllers of the Lower California fisheries had an eighty-foot yacht bought presumably for patrol purposes, but she is tied up in San Diego under the Federal order denying clearance to any transferred vessel for foreign ports because of international complications. The former patrol boat Ensenada is tied up without a crew since the memorable “battle of the halibut banks” wherein some poachers decoved her close aboard then rammed her, chasing her ashore. \n This has been a week of much action and big fines in field patrol circles, trout “sooners,” six of them, falling victims to Deputy James II. Gvger on San Jacinto reservoir for snagging “spawners”. They had fifteen fish in their car weighing forty-five pounds and a Riverside justice imposed $25 fine on each, or $150 for the day’s fishing. \n At the same time, Deputy Tipton Mathews served a warrant on a last year’s quail case, wherein two men now in Taft were arrested for exceeding the quail limit in Monterey county and placed under $100 bond each. \n Late advices from San Diego are that three boats have come in with lobsters from Lower California this week and another is due the 4th of April. Besides lobsters, these boats brought in seventy turtles from 100 to 200 pounds each, which will swell at 8 cents a pound in Los Angeles. This is not as cheap as it seems owing to the great waste; only about one-third is usable, but the meat is rich and delicious.""","2","""MEXICAN FISH COME IN FAST""","70","green","inferred","inferred",200,NA,"not_reported","""location is a little vague but likely magdalena bay""","1917",7,"high",70,90.72,NA,1910,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
-"116","sdunion_19190422","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-04-22,5,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190422.2.50&srpos=2&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""National City Concern Packs 20 Tons of Meat and Hundreds of Cases Daily. \n The turtle soup canning plant of the National City Commercial company at the upper end of the bay is packing 20 tons of turtle meat and several hundred cases of turtle soup daily, according to members of the sea-products packing concern. \n The ocean-going launch Lion entered port from Scammell's lagoon yesterday with 96 turtles. The auxiliary schooner Catarina will sail today for the Lower California coast for another turtle cargo. \n During the last month the Catarina and other craft have brought more than 270 turtles here to be cooked and pecked at the National City plant. \n San Diego packed turtle steak and soups is in great demand among the fashionable cafes and restaurants of the Pacific coast.""","2","""BUSINESS BRISK AT TURTLE PLANT""","96","green","inferred","known",NA,NA,"not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's, National City Commercial company""","1919",8,"high",96,NA,NA,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
