-"120","sdunion_19170423","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1917-04-23,5,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19170423.2.77&srpos=55&e=-------en--20-SDDU-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","""Seventy-Four Turtles Thrown Overboard to Lighten Craft Awaiting Aid.  \n Laden with a cargo of seventy-five turtles, the fishing schooner Apex arrived in port yesterday in tow of the fishing launch Beaver. \n The Apex left Turtle bay with a cargo of 150 turtles, but was compelled to heave seventy-four overboard at a point about 130 miles south of San Diego when the propeller dropped off and the craft became unmanageable in the high seas. \n A jury rig enabled the Apex to maintain steerageway until sighted and picked up by the Beaver. Some of the turtles which were thrown back Into the sea weighed, according to the skipper of the Apex, nearly 300 pounds. \n The cargo of the Apex was the largest single shipment of turtles that has arrived here from the lower coast in many months. Chicago, San Francisco and Los Angeles cafes, which make a specialty of turtle steaks and turtle soup, will obtain the greater part of the shipment. \n Receipts of lumber by sea yesterday measured nearly 1,000,000 feet. The steam schooner Washington, after discharging her lumber cargo, loaded 400 tons of citrus powder for delivery at San Francisco and Seattle.""","2","""FISHING SCHOONER TOWED INTO PORT""","150","green","inferred","known",300,NA,"not_reported","""the weight is what was given in the article as the weight for 'some of the turtles', but not necessarily the average or exact value""","1917",8,"high",150,136.08,NA,1910,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
-"121","stinson_sdherald_19830907","""San Diego Herald""",1983-09-07,NA,"48","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Speaking of turtles, however, we are indebted to Captain Bogart for the supply our bay now contains. In August 1856 [sic 1857], he returned from about three hundred and fifty miles down the coast with about fifty turtles. They were the kind known as the green turtle and rawbill. The largest weighed about three hundred and fifty pounds. They were all caught with a seine. Captain Bogart built a turtle pen in the bay, intending to send them to San Francisco, but they escaped from him and their children's children are often seen in our bay.""","2","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","150","green","reported","inferred",350,NA,"not_reported","""same turtles/event as stinson_sdherald_18571003 (redundant)""","1983",8,"high",150,158.76,NA,1980,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
-"122","stinson_sdherald_19830907","""San Diego Herald""",1983-09-07,NA,"48","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Speaking of turtles, however, we are indebted to Captain Bogart for the supply our bay now contains. In August 1856 [sic 1857], he returned from about three hundred and fifty miles down the coast with about fifty turtles. They were the kind known as the green turtle and rawbill. The largest weighed about three hundred and fifty pounds. They were all caught with a seine. Captain Bogart built a turtle pen in the bay, intending to send them to San Francisco, but they escaped from him and their children's children are often seen in our bay.""","2","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","150","hawksbill","inferred","inferred",350,NA,"not_reported","""same turtles/event as stinson_sdherald_18571003 (redundant), and same as row above but with a different species, because both are reported. The species is reported as ""rawbill"", which can be assumed as hawksbill""","1983",7,"high",150,158.76,NA,1980,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
-"123","stinson_sdunion_18890509","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1889-05-09,NA,"52","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Wharf and Waves. A Large Catch of Turtles. Executive Officer Harris, of the United States Survey Steamer Ranger, states that while off the Lower California coast, and bound for San Diego, they caught 177 turtles in one haul, the smallest weighing 50 pounds and the twenty largest weighing over 300 pounds""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","177","green","inferred","inferred",300,NA,"not_reported","na","1889",7,"high",177,136.08,NA,1880,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
-"124","sdunion_19191013","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-10-13,7,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19191013.2.94&srpos=12&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------"" ","""Two hundred and fifty turtles formed the cargo of the power schooner Catarina arriving from Scammon's lagoon yesterday morning. The turtles were consigned to the turtle soup factory at the upper end of the bay. \n The purse seiner St. Nicholas, bound from San Pedro for the lower coast, put into port yesterday with a leaking gasoline tank. A quick, reply from the S. O. S. call for plumbers enabled the St. Nicholas to leave for the fishing banks at dusk.""","2","""TURTLE CARGO HERE FOR SOUP FACTORY""","250","green","inferred","known",NA,NA,"not_reported","na","1919",8,"high",250,NA,NA,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
-"125","sdunion_19190505","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-05-05,6,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190505.2.87&srpos=1&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""Loaded with 260 turtles, the power schooner Catarina arrived from Scammell’s lagoon, Lower California, late Saturday. \n The turtles are consigned to the turtle packing plant of the National City Commercial company at the upper end of the bay. \n It Is estimated that Catarina's cargo will make about 5000 gallons of turtle soup and fully 1000 cases of turtle meat. \n The local turtle soup and turtle meat packing plant is the only one of Its kind on the Pacific coast. Since the plant opened several weeks ago it has handled nearly 1000 turtles.""","2","""TURTLE CARGO ARRIVES FOR PACKING COMPANY""","260","green","inferred","known",NA,NA,"not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's""","1919",8,"high",260,NA,NA,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
-"126","latimes_18890909","""Los Angeles Times""",1889-09-09,5,"na","""https://www.proquest.com/docview/163507545/fulltextPDF/C8D12580C68F44A0PQ/1?accountid=14026""","“The bird in his native Catalinian lair. \n A Turtle Turn—The Delights of Turtle Eggs—Catalina Rowers Surprised—The Yellow Tails Still to the Fore. \n Avalon (Santa Catalina Island), Sept. 6.—[Special correspondence of The Times.] A party from the Hotel Metropole were rowing around the point south of the hotel near Pebble Beach yesterday, when a curious round head popped out of the water, and a low hiss broke the stillness. One of the party was sure that it was a sea serpent; another thought it was something no one had ever seen before; but the head belonged to a fine green turtle, a big fellow, that would have tipped the scales at 500 pounds at least, and if turtle soup is not served at the hotel this week, it will not be the fault of the fishermen, who are camping on its trail night and day. \n Several of these reptiles have been seen about Catalina this summer, and it is supposed that they have been ashore, certain curious marks on the beach that puzzled the finders being laid at their door. If this is so there was a nest, and as out of any batch of turtle eggs laid a goodly per cent fail to change into young turtles, there must be some eggs there yet. \n Several parties have been out scouring the sandy beaches, and all sorts of trails have been followed up and worked in vain. The green turtle, in the experience of the writer, is fond of strolling on the sands on moonlight nights, and if by any chance the big fellows decide to come in there will be some great sport, and perhaps the would-be turtles would like some pointers from an old hand at the sport, who has “turned” a good many, and been laid out if not knocked out. \n On the last green turtle party it was my good fortune to attend, there were four or five ladies, so that six or seven couples were made up, and by 9 o’clock, just as the full moon was rising we were stationed all along the sandy beach of an island about one-fourth as large as Catalina. \n Every few moments the gentlemen of each party would start out and patrol the space between each other, walking rapidly along near the water watching for tracks. We did this for half an hour, when I noticed two tracks leading up from the water. \n Following them up on a run I almost stumbled on a big green turtle, busily engaged in throwing the sand out of a hole. The moment she saw me she turned, and shouting for help I seized her by the shell, and lifted. I raised her about a foot, then her big flipper came swinging around, filling my eyes full of san and almost knocking me over, while she went rapidly toward the water. Three of us caught her again about half way down and with a piece of board, tried to turn her, but not until the fourth man came, did we succeed in turning the largest green turtle I ever saw. They do not bite, but for throwing sand and scraping gravel, they beat the world. \n The turtle season to the south of here is in May or June, and what the big fellows are doing in shore now, when they should be in the high seas, is difficult to determine. \n La Paloma, Hancock Banning’s yacht, picked one up on her trip down from San Diego, and probably the one that has put in an appearance around Acalon will be caught. \n The fishing for the past week has been the best of the season, and is being enjoyed by large numbers of Los Angeles people. The summer rush is over, and ample accommodations are to be had by those who do not like the crowds of the mid-summer time. The Metropole has a delightful society of its own, about a hundred guests being there, many families having taken rooms for the month of September, which promises to be the gayest of the season. Admission day is to be celebrated here in fine style, a yacht race, tub race, pig hunt, swimming match and various diversions being arranged for. \n The Hermosa makes a grand excursion around the island with a band, and the Wanda and Hartie have arranged for special excursions to the various points of interest. \n The yellowtail fishing which started in so furiously the first of the week has kept up, and every day good catches have been made. \n Mr. Sumner is arranging for a grand goat hunt at Middle ranch, in which a number of Los Angeles sportsmen will participate.”","2","""GREEN TURTLE.""","4-5","green","reported","known",500,NA,"not_reported","""This reports multiple different encounters, at different times, with the green turtles--therefore the num_turtles is slightly uncertain""","1889",9,"high",NA,226.8,NA,1880,"catalina_island","los_angeles","california","usa",33.3889849,-118.4549433
-"127","sdunion_19190525","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-05-25,10,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190525.2.115&srpos=3&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","“Turtles on the way to San Diego to be transformed into turtle soup, turtle meat and turtle oil. Top picture shows one section of confining pen at Scammon’s lagoon, with a number of the prisoners; middle, schooner Catarina, which brings the turtles here, aground on the shore of the lagoon. Below, loading the turtles on the Catarina. \n Fishing for turtles is the interesting work of George Naylor and his brother William, sons of E. H. Naylor, 1904 Atlantic street, and Richard Johnson, all of this city. While interesting, the pursuit of turtles from 100 to 400 pounds is the hardest kind of work, as well as being extremely dangerous. \n The Naylor boys and Johnson are under contract with the Lower California Canning company of National City to supply turtles, and they make their catches In Scammon’s lagoon, 400 miles south of San Diego. \n Methods used in turtling differ, but the local boys use a net strung over about 1200 feet of water, the lower part anchored to the bottom and the other on the surface, buoyed by corks. This net is strung across the channel used by the turtles in going to and from their feeding grounds in shallow water. The turtles are approached while feeding and are frightened towards the net, where they become entangled In the meshes, which are about a foot in width. \n Wherever a turtle becomes caught, the corks on the surface are pulled under, and the turtles, in rowboats, pull in the net and land the turtle in the boat. Considerable skill is necessary to accomplish this part of the work, for in many cases the turtles are much heavier than the man in the boat. \n From the rowboats the catch is transferred to a motorcraft which lands the turtles on shore. A specially constructed pen, capable of confining hundreds, next receives them, and there they remain until the schooner Catarina arrives. \n The Catarina is chartered by the turtles and makes from two to three round trips a month between San Diego and Scammon's lagoon. Upon the Catarina's arrival at the lagoon the turtles are brought from the pen to the motor boat and thence to the school. Great care is necessary during the transfer, and, despite all precautions, many of the turtles escape. \n Two of the three partners remain on the turling grounds all the time, one of them making the semi-monthly journey on the Catarina. On arrival of the catch here the work of canning the meat and soup and extracting oil from the shell and fat of the turtles begins. The size of the Catarina’s cargo varies from 15 to 30 tons, and, while the craft is away on the trip to the cannery, the work of refilling the confining pens at Scammon's lagoon goes on unceasingly. \n The turtle canning Industry is a comparatively young one here, as the Naylor boys and Johnson started only three months ago. shortly after their return from the army. The investment of the partners amounts to more than $10,000. The cannery also represents a considerable investment.”","2","""Turtle Fishing in Mexican Waters for Cannery Is Work of 3 Former Soldiers""","cargo","green","inferred","known",400,NA,"not_reported","""the reported weight was 100 to 400 lbs, here we reported the maximum. Further estimates of weight and total cargo were given that could be used to calculate an estimate of num_turtles, but none given""","1919",7,"high",114.9375,181.44,NA,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
-"128","sdunion_19190402","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-04-02,9,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190402.2.119&srpos=6&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","“Steaks and Soup Put Up by Packing Concern; Supplied By Three Boats. \n San Diego has a new industry. It is the canning of green turtle steak and turtle soup. The plant is located near the foot of Thirty-second street in the buildings formerly occupied by the Lower California Chemical company for the manufacture of aniline dyes. \n The National City Commercial company, headed by J. K. Blackman. has taken over the Lower California Chemical company’s property and converted it into a turtle canning plant. A number of retorts for cooking turtles have been installed. Apparatus for making 800 gallons of turtle soup at a time also has been placed in the plant. \n The turtle cannery is supplied by a fleet of three boats. Two ocean-going launches are employed in capturing turtles in Scammell’s lagoon, just south of Ensenada. The power schooner Catarina brings the turtles to San Diego, making three voyages a month. The Catarina arrived from the south last week with 45 tons of live turtles and is now at Scamell’s lagoon loading another cargo. \n The products of the turtle cannery will be distributed throughout the United Slates.”","2","""TURTLE CANNING PLANT IS CITY’S NEWEST INDUSTRY""","cargo","green","reported","known",NA,NA,"not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's, National City Commercial company, general estimates of weight/cargo were given to calculate. Perhaps talking about the same hunting trips as sdunion_19190422 but unsure""","1919",8,"high",114.9375,NA,NA,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
-"129","sdunion_19190305","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-03-05,4,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190305.2.35&srpos=101&e=-------en--20-SDDU-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","“SAN DIEGO soon will have the novel distinction of being the world's first turtle-oil exporting port. \n According to word received here yesterday from the lower coast, a gang of Mexicans is employed in Scammell’s lagoon killing turtles from which an excellent grade of soap oil is obtained. \n The lagoon is said to be fairly alive with turtles ranging from 75 to 200 pounds in weight. At one period recently the Mexicans had turtles piled up like cordwood waiting to be cut up and rendered for the oil. \n The first shipment of turtle oil is expected here from Ensenada this month. The oil sells wholesale at $1 a gallon. \n Shark oil also is imported here from the Lower California coast. This oil is utilized in the manufacture of cosmetics.”","1","""Turtle Oil New Import From Lower California to San Diego""","cargo","green","inferred","known",200,NA,"not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's""","1919",6,"medium",114.9375,90.72,NA,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
-"130","spdaily_19251224","""San Pedro Daily News""",1925-12-24,1,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPDN19251224.2.9&srpos=71&e=-------en--20-SPDN-61--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Total Receipts for November Totaled 13,830,379 Pounds, Report \n Total receipts of fish for the canneries and wholesale markets at San Pedro harbor for the month of November amounted to 13,830,579 pounds, according to the official report of the state fish and game commission, compiled under the direction of H. L. Williamson, in charge of the commercial fisheries department. \n Sardines led the list with 11,837,080 pounds. Yellowfin tuna came next with 781,403 pounds. Skipjack were third, with 495,038 pounds. The catch of barracuda totaled 129,923 pounds, while the mackerel catch was 173,222 pounds. \n The lobster catch was considerable larger than in former seasons. with a total catch of 63,248 pounds. Turtle, with 21 pounds; anchovies, 60 pounds; pompano, 8 pounds; flounders, 54 pounds, and clams, 75 pounds, formed the smallest in the last of 39 varieties of sea food caught.""","2","""SARDINES HEAD LIST OF FISH BROUGHT HERE""","cargo","green","inferred","not_reported",NA,NA,"not_reported","""The total number of turtles was not reported but the total weight of the cargo was just 21 pounds; additionally, no exact location was reported, but perhaps we can infer san pedro area, because that is where the newspaper is from""","1925",5,"medium",114.9375,NA,NA,1920,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
-"131","spdaily_19200225","""San Pedro Daily News""",1920-02-25,1,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPDN19200225.2.14&srpos=112&e=-------en--20-SPDN-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Sixty Per Cent of Investment in Southern California and Forty-four Per Cent of Total Values Created in Local Harbor. \n Fisheries students long have marveled at the phenomenal growth of California’s sea food industries during the war; but figures just issued by the State Fish and Game Commission have an equally compelling interest for the general public. \n Aided by the various enactments of the last legislature rendering all facts accessible concerning catches at their origin, and reinforcing them by requiring exact reports as to the pack, the fisheries statisticians have found that the preserving or fish alone has now gone considerably beyond the “Twenty Million a Year” class. Not counting the enormously growing fresh fish demand, now that Californians really have begun to take their fish more seriously as a food supply, the export fisheries business alone takes rank as a capital industry of the first magnitude on its showing during 1919. \n The compilation shows that nearly eight million was invested in canning, drying, salting, smoking and pickling sea products giving employment to no less than 8242 persons. This harmonious combination of capital and labor swelled the wealth of the state by the splendid total of $21,417,743. \n Southern California again led the state, and took rank as foremost center of production by contributions to this total of, respectively 60 per cent or $12,294,064 and 44 per cent or $9,694,482. The spreading sphere of the truly excellent California sardine in the world’s markets is attested by its pre-eminence in the total pack, surpassing even the ""sea chicken” already so well set up on a gastronomic pedestal of its own by the albacore tuna canners. \n Many species none too generally known in their fresh state, have been packed in varying quantities, some experimentally, others on such substantial basis as Monterey’s 3538 cases of the toothsome and delicate squid, only properly appreciated by the Mediterranean peoples. The epicurean green turtle, celebrated of bou-vivants the world over, figures to the extent of 7407 cases, the “raw material” having been imported from the sandy strands of Lower California bays where the turtles come ashore to deposit their eggs. \n Evidences of constant effort to add something to the food of the world are abundant in the fisheries compilation of 1919, suggesting general appreciation among the canners of the great truth that because it can, eventually the sea must feed the land. Packers of fish are embracing new products at a rate that speaks well for the building of an immense direct export business of canned sea foods from the ever-widening harbors of the south where produced, to those foreign peoples who already have learned to laugh at the meat barons through mastery of preparation, and appreciation of the food value of fish. \n When the figures on last year’s fresh fish business are compiled by the Fish and Game Commission, it is predicted upon the information already tabulated, that some more and bigger surprises are in store. While it is true that Californians as yet have not fully learned to eat fish, the figures make it apparent that they are rapidly learning. High meat prices, war premium upon every nature of food supply, and the wide advertisement given sea products by state conservation authorities played their part, but the genuine excellence of the Pacific Coast fish themselves when properly prepared is really doing it. Housewives are gradually learning that a fine fish is worth as much culinary attention, and as loving preparation as they would lavish upon a choice roast or fowl, or a piece of game. Foreign cooks learned that lesson long ago. The ready cooked products of the cannery, scientifically and deliciously prepared, have created considerable taste for the fresh product also, and the combination bids fair to go towards solving the present high cost of living by diversifying the present top-heavy demand upon meat, potatoes and a few other over-exploited staples. \n Despite the enormous tonnages represented by the figures, and notwithstanding the multiplication of canneries with capacities of 150 tons per day, the State fisheries experts insist that the possibilities of California’s marine resources are but scarcely scratched, as yet. Aliens make constant use of dozens of species little understood by Americans, fully equal to many in daily use, and superior to some. At present rates of increase, the astonishing totals of today’s fisheries business in another decade will seem trivial. Intensive methods, scientific preservation, elimination of some present wastes, are all along the trend of present times.""","2","""SAN PEDRO LEADS PORTS OF COAST IN PACKING FISH""","cargo","green","reported","known",NA,NA,"not_reported","""part of the article is also continued on page 6; the article states that the location was 'lower california' aka Baja California. It also states a number of 'cases' of green turtle, but I am unsure if this means the number of individual turtles, or the number of cases of meat: 'The epicurean green turtle, celebrated of bou-vivants the world over, figures to the extent of 7407 cases'""","1920",8,"high",114.9375,NA,NA,1920,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
-"132","vfp_18910623","""Ventura Free Press""",1891-06-23,4,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MFP18910623.2.11&srpos=5&e=-------en--20-MFP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""The crop of monster turtles at Magdalena Bay, Lower California has induced a company to experiment with canning the extract for exportation. The first lot has gone to England through La Paz. The Lower Californian of Ensenada reports also that fishing for hair seals is a growing industry at the head of the Gulf of California, miners buying the output at 50 cents a gallon.""","1","""Canned Turtle.""","cargo","green","inferred","known",NA,NA,"not_reported","""This article does not explicitly name the capture of turtles at a certain time/date, but does mention that there is a 'crop' of turtles in Magdalena bay that can be used for canning.""","1891",6,"medium",114.9375,NA,NA,1890,"magdalena_bay","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
-"133","sppilot_19190327","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1919-03-27,1,"na","""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19190327.2.16&srpos=190&e=-------en--20-SPNP-181--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Three fishing vessels are now being fitted with proper equipment for catching Mexican turtles, and it is reported that several canning concerns are preparing to handle their catches. \n The turtles will be brought from Mexican waters, about a day and a half run from the harbor. Last year several tons of turtles were cared for, but the industry did not become established. \n Soup and canned turtle meat have heretofore come mostly from San Diego.""","1","""Going to Mexico for Turtles for Canning""","cargo","green","inferred","not_reported",NA,NA,"not_reported","""This article is very ambiguously written/worded, and location of capture is unclear whether it is san_diego or baja_california""","1919",4,"medium",114.9375,NA,NA,1910,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
-"134","latimes_18990622","""Los Angeles Times""",1899-06-22,13,"na","""https://www.proquest.com/docview/163891894/D4971430CE2047A5PQ/268?accountid=14026""","“SAN PEDRO, June 21. — [Regular Correspondence.] The prospect of establishing a cannery here for the packing of the sea products of the Lower California waters is favorable, according to S. W. Waring, late manager of the Catalina Conserving Company’s lobster-packing plant in this city. Mr. Waring returned Tuesday from a trip down the coast about one hundred and twenty miles beyond the Mexican line. Turtles of a kind very like the much-prized green turtle of commerce are there in abundance, and the transportation of them for packing at this port seems to be practicable. The turtles, which average about one hundred and twenty-five pounds in weight, and some of which weigh as much as 200 pounds, may be caught in shallow water. The natives capture them with the use of spears, but that method would not do for turtles to be brought here. They may be taken unawares by men wading in the water and approaching them when asleep. A quick little motion by hands grasping the lower edge of the shell on one side turns the turtle on its back, and it is then practically helpless. The turtle is made ready for transportation by tying its fins and placing a block of wood so that the head may not fall too far down and cause strangulation. Waring says he has shipped live turtles treated in that manner from Galveston, Tex., to New York city without injury to them. If the number of turtles brought to a cannery hero should be greater than could be used for a few days, they may doubtless be kept alive as long as desired in an in-closure in the bay. \n Eight kinds of meat may be obtained from the turtles, and from one of them weighing 125 pounds the edible parts weigh nearly one hundred pounds. In the same waters lobsters are plentiful and barracuda are abundant during the season when they are not found in such large numbers in the waters near here. There is no import duty on live or fresh fish taken in Mexican waters and brought into the United States, provided they are brought in boats of not less than thirty tons’ net register. If the fish bo in any manner preserved there is a duty of ¼ of a cent a pound imposed.”","2","""Prospective Sea-food Cannery""","cargo","green","reported","inferred",200,NA,"""shallow water""","""says that average weight is 125, but max weight is 200. This article is about the prospect of establishing a cannery near LA and therefore would be fishing the turtles out of 'Lower California', and while it hadn't yet started, they were reportedly 'in abundance' for fishing at the time, and packing adn fishing is practical. Exact location just says 120 miles south of the US-Mexican border""","1899",8,"high",114.9375,90.72,NA,1890,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
-"135","stinson_sdherald_18720104","""San Diego Herald""",1872-01-04,NA,"51","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""In giving the shipments of merchandise by the steamer Orizaba on Tuesday we neglected to make a note of a small lot of green turtle which went up. The shipment in point of numbers was light, but in pounds it looks well. One of the last lot shipped, weighed 103 pounds. During the remainder of the winter, we are told, regular shipments of the green turtle will be made to San Francisco.""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","cargo","green","reported","not_reported",103,NA,"not_reported","""no location is given, but likely can presume baja_california, base don context within the thesis.""","1872",6,"medium",114.9375,46.72,NA,1870,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
-"136","stinson_sdunion_18910615","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1891-06-15,NA,"52","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Extract of Turtle. A New Use for the Monsters of Magdalena-Ensenada. The crop of monster turtles in Magdalena Bay, Lower California, has induced a company to experiment with canning the extract for exportation. The first lot has gone to England through La Paz. The Lower Californian of Ensenada reports also that fishing for hair seals is a growing industry at the head of the Gulf of California, miners buying the output at 50 cents a gallon.""","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","cargo","green","inferred","known",NA,NA,"not_reported","na","1891",6,"medium",114.9375,NA,NA,1890,"magdalena_bay","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
-"137","latimes_19200902","""Los Angeles Times""",1920-09-02,1,"na","""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160850108/fulltextPDF/FE617DBE9A774635PQ/1?accountid=14026""","“Father and Son Testify They Were Ill-Fed on Trip. \n Get Judgment Against Owner of Coasting Vessel. \n Dollar-a-month Contract is Branded as ”Doctored.” \n The brine of the Pacific, beans and boiled turtle meat, the last named procured in the hot sands of the Magdalena Islands, formed the principal ingredients in the dish set before U. S. Commissioner Long yesterday, in the trial of the admiralty case involving the claims of Albert G. Stebernaler and his son. A. G., Jr., against A. H. Moore, owner and master of the gas schooner Newark. \n Slebernaler, Sr, sued for $318.50. said to be due as wages as second cook on the vessel, and the commissioner gave him a judgment for $198 50. Theeboy asked for $326 50, and he was awarded $206. The commissioner figured that the defendant Moore should be allowed two months to make the trip from the Magdalena Islands to Acapulco, Mex., and back to San Diego, and that he should pay the plaintiffs for the balance of the time, the voyage requiring from April to November, 1919. \n Slebernaler and the boy went to the Magdalena Islands to make assays for an amalgam corporation. Involving the deposits of manganese. It was expected that the Newark should call for them and bring them back to the United States, as the vessel had been utilized to carry provisions to the islands. But the vessel was delayed. \n When the two got ready to come back to this country they found themselves without funds and the testimony showed that they went before the American Consul at Manzaniello. Mex., Harry L Walsh, and signed ship’s papers binding them to serve on the Newark, that later on touched at the island, until it returned to San Diego or Los Angeles Harbor. The Siebernalers found it necessary to work their way back \n SUM PUT IN LATER. \n The ship’s papers show that the two men, aged 46 and 20 years, respectively, agreed to work on the boat for $1 a month, but both witnesses said that figure had been inserted after they had signed the agreement in the office of the Consul That was where the argument started. \n In the bill of complaint the elder Slebernaler sued for 165 days’ pay at $2 a day, as second cook on the Newark and the boy asked for the same rates as engineer for the same period. \n The father gave his testimony some months ago, and yesterday the son was on the stand. He said that both had been compelled to work hard, and that the most of the time they were compelled to subsist on beans and turtle meat, but the witness admitted that Capt. Moore ate at the same table and had the same food. Both testified that they were ill-fed, were compelled to go without shoes, and the son said that he suffered from sore feet for a long time after the Newark reached her home port at San Diego. \n Young Slebernaler stated that it was one of his duties to get up at 3 o’clock in the morning and go to the Mexican villages to get milk for Capt. Moore, and in addition he had to do the ordinary duties of a seaman, wash down the deck, etc He testified that Capt. Moore told him that his services were very satisfactory. Both the father and son thought a dollar a month was not enough for such work. But both denied that they had agreed to work for that amount, although the ship’s papers showed their signature to the agreement calling for the payment of the sum. \n The judgment rendered by the commissioner will act as a libel on the Newark”","2","""OBJECT TO DIET OF TURTLE MEAT""","not_reported","green","inferred","inferred",NA,NA,"not_reported","""magdalena_islands = magdalena_bay? This article is about these people eating turtle meat captured from the area so the exact number and location is unclear""","1920",5,"medium",NA,NA,NA,1920,"magdalena_islands","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
