+"5","stinson_sdherald_18710615","""San Diego Herald""",1871-06-15,NA,"51","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""A firm in this city is in receipt of a letter from a large house in San Francisco making inquiries in relation to the possibility of receiving a steady supply of turtle through this port. The San Diego firm has replied offering to contract to supply any stated quantity, from one up to ten thousand pounds, by each steamer, or even more if desired. Turtles are found in abundance at Turtle Bay... or at Scammon's Lagoon... and can be supplied to the San Francisco dealers very cheaply.""","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","cargo","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""no precise number given, just says that they are found 'in abundance', and seemingly enough to support a fishery/industry.""","1871",6,"medium",NA,57.46875,NA,1870,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
+"6","stinson_sdherald_18710615","""San Diego Herald""",1871-06-15,NA,"51","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""A firm in this city is in receipt of a letter from a large house in San Francisco making inquiries in relation to the possibility of receiving a steady supply of turtle through this port. The San Diego firm has replied offering to contract to supply any stated quantity, from one up to ten thousand pounds, by each steamer, or even more if desired. Turtles are found in abundance at Turtle Bay... or at Scammon's Lagoon... and can be supplied to the San Francisco dealers very cheaply.""","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","cargo","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""no precise number given, just says that they are found 'in abundance', and seemingly enough to support a fishery/industry.""","1871",6,"medium",NA,57.46875,NA,1870,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
+"7","sdunion_19170402","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1917-04-02,5,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19170402.2.68&srpos=32&e=-------en--20-SDDU-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+automobile-------""","“New Waters Near La Paz Promise Prolific Supply of Sea Food. \n Competition In the Lower California fisheries trade, advocated by the California state game and fish commission as a means of crating a supply greater than the demand, and therefore resulting in the lowering of the high cost of living, is beginning to bear fruit. \n For years fishermen operating out of this port have been compelled to pay tribute to certain concessional holders of fishing rights along the southern littoral for the privilege of operating in those waters. \n Independent fishermen, headed by M. Kondo, have now started fishing in waters south of the twenty eighth parallel under permits granted by the governor of La Paz. These waters form a prolific source of supply of halibut, barracuda, lobsters and other kinds of shellfish. \n Two California inspectors have been commissioned to go south with the lobster craft operating under permits issued by the governor of La Paz. These officers are being paid by the importers, as required by law. Their duties are to certify that the provisions of the California law governing lobster importation are respected. A third inspector will arrive here April 10. \n In addition to granting permits overriding the concessionaires operating for the Cantu government, the Mexican de facto government as represented in the La Paz regime has taken the California fish and game laws for its guidance in conserving the west Mexican fisheries and has appointed its own inspectors to accompany the boats to which it has issued permits, to see that the fishing craft take no young fish, use no trawl nets or other gear destructive of the future supply. \n Work of the concessionaires under this new competitive arrangement, fostered by the simplifying of importation under rulings of the fish and game commission, will extend to the fresh fish trade within a short time. Owing to the distance traveled refrigerating apparatus will have to be installed on boats operating to the new fishing grounds in the vicinity of Turtle and Magdalena bays. \n The auxiliary schooners None Such and Telemaches are now operating exclusively between this port and Turtle bay in the lobster and turtle carrying trade. Turtle meat now is being sold at 8 cents a pound In the California markets, and the demand is said to be far greater than the supply. \n Captain Robert Israel, who, with Kondo, is establishing an independent lobster and turtle trade at this port, and who is operating the None Such and Telemaches, plans to put another vessel in service between here and Turtle bay within a few weeks. \n The floating cannery John A. Heston is operating at Magdalena bay, engaged in the canning of turtle soup, sardines and other fish products. The Heston, during the tuna season, is expected to pack thousands of cases of this delectable sea food for shipment to San Diego, thence to be distributed to the Southern California, Western and Eastern markets. \n Five thousand pounds of halibut and barracuda arrived in port yesterday from the halibut banks north of Cedros on the fishing schooner Josephine Z.”","2","""PUBLIC TO BENEFIT FROM OPENING OF FISHING GROUNDS""","cargo","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""location is not specified to an individual fishing site, but instead clarifies that fishing is 'south of the 28th parallel' and includes Magdalena bay, turtle bay, and La Paz""","1917",6,"medium",NA,57.46875,NA,1910,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
+"8","stinson_sdherald_18870326","""San Diego Herald""",1887-03-26,NA,"54","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""It is a singular fact that turtle is appreciated in California neither as an article of food nor an element of commerce. Californians can hardly be persuaded to eat turtle. In New York, and the East generally, and over the whole world, turtle is regarded as a great delicacy. [Captain Bogart] informed me that turtle exist in wonderful quantities on the Lower California coast. It may be impossible to make a market for the dainty in California, but the meat can be packed and exported. Captain Bogart informs us that Scammon’s Lagoon, several lagoons to the north of it, and the bay at Cerros [Cedros] Island are crammed with turtle. Many sloughs open into the lagoons, and the practice of the turtle is to float up them at flood-tide and feed. Captain Bogart, by stretching a seine over the mouth of these sloughs, has captured eight tons at a single haul. He could have taken more but had no room for them.”","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","cargo","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1887",6,"medium",NA,57.46875,NA,1880,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
+"9","stinson_sdherald_18870326","""San Diego Herald""",1887-03-26,NA,"54","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""It is a singular fact that turtle is appreciated in California neither as an article of food nor an element of commerce. Californians can hardly be persuaded to eat turtle. In New York, and the East generally, and over the whole world, turtle is regarded as a great delicacy. [Captain Bogart] informed me that turtle exist in wonderful quantities on the Lower California coast. It may be impossible to make a market for the dainty in California, but the meat can be packed and exported. Captain Bogart informs us that Scammon’s Lagoon, several lagoons to the north of it, and the bay at Cerros [Cedros] Island are crammed with turtle. Many sloughs open into the lagoons, and the practice of the turtle is to float up them at flood-tide and feed. Captain Bogart, by stretching a seine over the mouth of these sloughs, has captured eight tons at a single haul. He could have taken more but had no room for them.”","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","cargo","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1887",6,"medium",NA,57.46875,NA,1880,"cedros_island","ensenada","baja_california","mexico",28.2012034,-115.2522841
+"10","latimes_19190306","""Los Angeles Times""",1919-03-06,7,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160705637/2796BCF9F7524B0FPQ/39?accountid=14026""","“Inscription Cut in Shell,' “E. J. W., El Dorado, 1858,” Creates Interest. \n [LOCAL CORRESPONDENCE] \n VENICE, March 6. — An aged sea turtle was caught off the Venice breakwater today. On its back was the following inscription: ""E.J.W., El Dorado, 1858.” \n The lettering was etched deep into the tortoise shell and must have been done many years ago. What the name “El Dorado” signified can be left to anyone’s imagination. It may be the name of some ship that sailed the southern seas in the days of the ‘49‘ers, or it might mean merely a name carved by some sailor without any special purpose. \n The shell was badly damaged in getting the turtle ashore. so after some vain speculation by the fishermen, the creature was tossed back into the ocean.”","1","""AGED SEA TURTLE HAULED ASHORE""","1","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1919",7,"high",NA,1,NA,1910,"venice","los_angeles","california","usa",33.9874998,-118.4617152
+"11","sdunion_19170423","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1917-04-23,5,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19170423.2.77&srpos=55&e=-------en--20-SDDU-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","""Seventy-Four Turtles Thrown Overboard to Lighten Craft Awaiting Aid.  \n Laden with a cargo of seventy-five turtles, the fishing schooner Apex arrived in port yesterday in tow of the fishing launch Beaver. \n The Apex left Turtle bay with a cargo of 150 turtles, but was compelled to heave seventy-four overboard at a point about 130 miles south of San Diego when the propeller dropped off and the craft became unmanageable in the high seas. \n A jury rig enabled the Apex to maintain steerageway until sighted and picked up by the Beaver. Some of the turtles which were thrown back Into the sea weighed, according to the skipper of the Apex, nearly 300 pounds. \n The cargo of the Apex was the largest single shipment of turtles that has arrived here from the lower coast in many months. Chicago, San Francisco and Los Angeles cafes, which make a specialty of turtle steaks and turtle soup, will obtain the greater part of the shipment. \n Receipts of lumber by sea yesterday measured nearly 1,000,000 feet. The steam schooner Washington, after discharging her lumber cargo, loaded 400 tons of citrus powder for delivery at San Francisco and Seattle.""","2","""FISHING SCHOONER TOWED INTO PORT""","150","green","inferred","known","300","not_reported","not_reported","""the weight is what was given in the article as the weight for 'some of the turtles', but not necessarily the average or exact value""","1917",8,"high",NA,150,136.08,1910,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
+"12","sppilot_19190506","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1919-05-06,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19190506.2.16&srpos=12&e=-------en--20-SPNP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""The Nielsen & Kittle cannery may begin manufacturing turtle soup in the near future as the result of a number of experiments which have been made with a catch of turtles recently made in Mexican waters. \n Carl Nielsen, A. F. Kittle, Herman Norden and E. H. Hansen of the Nielsen & Kittle Canning company have just returned on the Nicko No. 1 with a load of fifty large turtles, caught in Turtle Bay and other Mexican waters. The largest weighed 500 pounds. The boat was out twelve days. \n If the experiments being carried out prove satisfactory, the cannery may begin manufacturing turtle soup on a large scale.""","2","""Local Cannery May Put Up Turtle Soup""","50","green","inferred","inferred","500","not_reported","not_reported","""the location of turtle bay is given as exact, but it also specifies that they are found 'other Mexican waters', therefore the location is inferred. The 500-lb weight given is the largest turtle""","1919",7,"high",NA,50,226.8,1910,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
+"13","spdaily_19190508","""San Pedro Daily News""",1919-05-08,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPDN19190508.2.6&srpos=20&e=-------en--20-SPDN-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Carl O. Nielsen and A. F. Kittle, owners of the Nielsen-Kittle company’s fish cannery at East San Pedro, have returned home from a voyage to Mexican waters that they made in the company’s craft, Necko No. 1. The packers brought back 48 turtles that they captured at Turtle bay. The largest turtle weighed 350 pounds. The turtles are to be experimented with in the making of canned soup.""","2","""CAPTURE AND BRING HOME BIG TURTLES""","48","green","inferred","known","350","not_reported","not_reported","""the reported size is that of the largest turtle captured""","1919",8,"high",NA,48,158.76,1910,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
+"14","cejournal_19170331","""Coronado Eagle and Journal""",1917-03-31,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=CJ19170331.2.4&srpos=21&e=--1846---1973--en--20-CJ-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Live turtles and lobsters were plentiful around the municipal pier Wednesday morning when two boats arrived from Turtle bay on the Lower California coast. \n The auxiliary schooner Nonesuch brought its first load from the camps recently established by the Israel brothers, the delivery being 21 turtles and 58 crates of lobsters. The Telemachus, Captain Olson, brought 20 turtles and 49 crates of lobsters. \n Turtle steaks and soup will be on the menu at del Coronado for a day or two, as five big turtles were brought over for the delectation of the palates of guests. One of the turtles weighed 275 pounds, and the other four were almost as large, except a little one that Manager Hernan may decide to use as a mascot. Turtles cost 6c a pound as they come from the water, and there is lots of bone in their shell.""
+","2","""BRING TURTLES AND LOBSTERS""","21","green","inferred","known","275","not_reported","not_reported","""The given weight is of just one turtle, but seemed to be a typical size for the given shipment""","1917",8,"high",NA,21,124.74,1910,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
+"15","spdaily_19170608","""San Pedro Daily News""",1917-06-08,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPDN19170608.2.14&srpos=54&e=-------en--20-SPDN-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""In the warehouse of the Avalon Fish company is a large turtle weighing 160 pounds. It was captured at Turtle Bay, two hundred miles down the coast from San Diego, from which place it was brought to the southern city, whence it was shipped by rail to San Pedro.""","2","""West Shore Notes""","1","green","inferred","known","160","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1917",8,"high",NA,1,72.57,1910,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
+"16","sppilot_19290122","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1929-01-22,13,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19290122.2.197&srpos=71&e=-------en--20-SPNP-61--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""The 102-foot yawl rigged yacht Talayha, owned by Lazard Lippman, San Pedro merchant, was back in the harbor yesterday, following a successful two weeks’ cruise down the Mexican coast. On the trip Lippman and his party of friends spent the time fishing and hunting. They secured several deer in the mountains near San Quentin Bay, Lower California. Off Cedros Island the party took red abalones for dinner and at Turtle Bay they captured a 300-pound green sea turtle.""","2","""Lippman’s Yacht Talahya Returns From Long Cruise""","1","green","reported","known","300","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1929",9,"high",NA,1,136.08,1920,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
+"17","sppilot_19290905","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1929-09-05,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19290905.2.15&srpos=1&e=-------en--20-SPNP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""TURTLE BAY TURTLE DISAPPEARS Speaking of Turtle bay, the shelled animal from which it gained its name because of the great numbers of turtles there in the early days, is now to be found there but rarely. The advancing front line of civilization and appetites for soup have driven the turtle to his last stronghold, farther down the Mexican coast. San Pedro fishermen cruising down the coast occasionally capture one of the large creatures. One such turtle brought back to the municipal fish wharf alive some time ago had laid a peck of eggs on the trip. Capture of one of the turtles is always an occasion for celebration by the fishermen. The heavy fishing in Turtle bay is believed to have caused the animals to leave.""","1","""FISH PACKERS MARKING TIME""","0","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""While this article mentions the capture of one turtle, it is predominantly about the ABSENCE of many turtles in Turtle bay as they once were. The single turtle captured was a marked rarity. This demonstrates a change in the cargo-yielding capacity of Baja California.""","1929",7,"high",NA,0,NA,1920,"turtle_bay","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.6943941,-114.8965209
+"18","sdunion_19071029","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1907-10-29,14,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19071029.2.147&srpos=71&e=-------en--20-SDDU-61--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------"" ","“W. J. Forbes Hooks Huge Turtle Which Carries Boat to Sea; Capsizes \n Word has been received from Lower California that William J. Forbes, prominent in the affairs of Wall street while thousands of miles removed from the financial disturbance of the eastern metropolis, had a close call the other day from drowning in the waters of Todos Santos bay, thirty or forty miles south of Ensenada. \n Forbes, who has a number of warm friends in San Diego, is the guest of Walter Nordhoff, who owns a splendid country home on the shores of the bay, and who is famous as an entertainer. The Wall street financier was out fishing from a small boat, accompanied by a boatman, when a huge turtle was hooked. Forbes was unable to land the fish, and within a short time they were off for a cruise, the destination of which they knew not. It was worse than sailing under sealed orders in that there was no certainty that the destination would ever be known. \n However, after the turtle had towed them for an hour or two the men in the boat, becoming exasperated, resolved to either land the fish or overturn the boat in the attempt. The effort resulted in the capsizing of the craft. \n In the advices received here nothing is said about the boatman, and it is supposed that he was more fortunate than Forbes, who was in the water nearly two hours before the rescue. \n Local friends are awaiting the full details of the accident.”","2","""NEW YORK BANKER NEARLY DROWNS""","1","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""seems like it could be a different species other than green, even though we assumed that, because green seem a little bit too small too tow a boat for that long, this also appears to be the same turtle event as laherald_19071030 and sbmp_19071030""","1907",8,"high",NA,1,NA,1900,"todos_santos_bay","ensenada","baja_california","mexico",31.7890284,-116.6749348
+"19","laherald_19071030","""Los Angeles Herald""",1907-10-30,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19071030.2.12&srpos=31&e=-------en--20-LAH-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""William J. Forbes' Boat Upset Near Ensenada and He Struggles for Two Hours In the Water \n By Associated Press. \n SAN DIEGO. Oct. 29.—Word has just been received from Ensenada of the narrow escape from death of William J. Forbes, prominent in the affairs of Wall street, who is a guest of Walter Nordhoff, at his home at Puenta Banda. Mr. Forbes was fishing on the bay in a small boat, accompanied by a boatman, when their line was taken by a sea turtle. The line held and the turtle towed the boat for some distance. \n The fishermen determined to land the turtle if possible, and in their efforts they overturned the boat. The turtle got away with the tackle and the fishermen were in the water for two hours before they were rescued.""","2","""BIG TURTLE ENDANGERS LIFE OF CAPITALIST""","1","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""this appears to be the same event as sdunion_19071029, from the SD union, and sbmp_19071030. location specifically given as Puenta Banda but assumed to be todos santos bay because of connection to sdunion article""","1907",8,"high",NA,1,NA,1900,"todos_santos_bay","ensenada","baja_california","mexico",31.7890284,-116.6749348
+"20","stinson_sdherald_18870902","""San Diego Herald""",1887-09-02,NA,"53","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""A 600 pound turtle will form a novel feature of the St. Louis Grand Army Encampment. It will be in the San Diego exhibit, having been caught in the bay of Ensenada de Todos Santos, in Lower California.""","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","1","green","inferred","known","600","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1887",7,"high",NA,1,272.16,1880,"todos_santos_bay","ensenada","baja_california","mexico",31.7890284,-116.6749348
+"21","sbmp_18871014","""Morning Press""",1887-10-14,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP18871014.2.3&srpos=5&e=-------en--20-MP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","“Sights for the Curious at the Santa Cruz Fair. \n Santa Cruz, Oct. 13. —The second day of the County Fair has proved to be a success. The display of live stock is good and many fine thoroughbreds are on exhibition. An Italian fisherman at Soquel yesterday caught in his net a monster turtle which measured eight feet in length and over seven feet in width. It weighs about one thousand pounds. It is now on exhibition at the Fair. It is said to be the largest turtle ever caught on this coast.”","1","""A MONSTROUS TURTLE""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","1000","8'","not_reported","""possible leatherback based on size, including 7 feet in width""","1887",7,"high",243.84,1,453.59,1880,"soquel","santa_cruz","california","usa",37.0019613,-121.9471344
+"22","latimes_18871014","""Los Angeles Times""",1887-10-14,5,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/163445030/D4971430CE2047A5PQ/285?accountid=14026""","“Santa Cruz, Oct. 13.—An Italian fisherman at Soquel yesterday caught in his net a monster turtle, which measures eight feet in length and over seven in width. It weighs about 1000 pounds. It is now on exhibition at the fair. It is said to be the largest turtle ever caught on the Coast.”","1","""Something of a Turtle.""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","1000","8'","""caught in net""","""width is 7' Species unclear--could be either leatherback based on size, or green based on inference. Says it is largest turtle ever caught on the coast.""","1887",8,"high",243.84,1,453.59,1880,"soquel","santa_cruz","california","usa",37.0019613,-121.9471344
+"23","sdunion_19190525","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-05-25,10,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190525.2.115&srpos=3&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","“Turtles on the way to San Diego to be transformed into turtle soup, turtle meat and turtle oil. Top picture shows one section of confining pen at Scammon’s lagoon, with a number of the prisoners; middle, schooner Catarina, which brings the turtles here, aground on the shore of the lagoon. Below, loading the turtles on the Catarina. \n Fishing for turtles is the interesting work of George Naylor and his brother William, sons of E. H. Naylor, 1904 Atlantic street, and Richard Johnson, all of this city. While interesting, the pursuit of turtles from 100 to 400 pounds is the hardest kind of work, as well as being extremely dangerous. \n The Naylor boys and Johnson are under contract with the Lower California Canning company of National City to supply turtles, and they make their catches In Scammon’s lagoon, 400 miles south of San Diego. \n Methods used in turtling differ, but the local boys use a net strung over about 1200 feet of water, the lower part anchored to the bottom and the other on the surface, buoyed by corks. This net is strung across the channel used by the turtles in going to and from their feeding grounds in shallow water. The turtles are approached while feeding and are frightened towards the net, where they become entangled In the meshes, which are about a foot in width. \n Wherever a turtle becomes caught, the corks on the surface are pulled under, and the turtles, in rowboats, pull in the net and land the turtle in the boat. Considerable skill is necessary to accomplish this part of the work, for in many cases the turtles are much heavier than the man in the boat. \n From the rowboats the catch is transferred to a motorcraft which lands the turtles on shore. A specially constructed pen, capable of confining hundreds, next receives them, and there they remain until the schooner Catarina arrives. \n The Catarina is chartered by the turtles and makes from two to three round trips a month between San Diego and Scammon's lagoon. Upon the Catarina's arrival at the lagoon the turtles are brought from the pen to the motor boat and thence to the school. Great care is necessary during the transfer, and, despite all precautions, many of the turtles escape. \n Two of the three partners remain on the turling grounds all the time, one of them making the semi-monthly journey on the Catarina. On arrival of the catch here the work of canning the meat and soup and extracting oil from the shell and fat of the turtles begins. The size of the Catarina’s cargo varies from 15 to 30 tons, and, while the craft is away on the trip to the cannery, the work of refilling the confining pens at Scammon's lagoon goes on unceasingly. \n The turtle canning Industry is a comparatively young one here, as the Naylor boys and Johnson started only three months ago. shortly after their return from the army. The investment of the partners amounts to more than $10,000. The cannery also represents a considerable investment.”","2","""Turtle Fishing in Mexican Waters for Cannery Is Work of 3 Former Soldiers""","cargo","green","inferred","known","400","not_reported","not_reported","""the reported weight was 100 to 400 lbs, here we reported the maximum. Further estimates of weight and total cargo were given that could be used to calculate an estimate of num_turtles, but none given""","1919",7,"high",NA,114.9375,181.44,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
+"24","sdunion_19190402","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-04-02,9,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190402.2.119&srpos=6&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","“Steaks and Soup Put Up by Packing Concern; Supplied By Three Boats. \n San Diego has a new industry. It is the canning of green turtle steak and turtle soup. The plant is located near the foot of Thirty-second street in the buildings formerly occupied by the Lower California Chemical company for the manufacture of aniline dyes. \n The National City Commercial company, headed by J. K. Blackman. has taken over the Lower California Chemical company’s property and converted it into a turtle canning plant. A number of retorts for cooking turtles have been installed. Apparatus for making 800 gallons of turtle soup at a time also has been placed in the plant. \n The turtle cannery is supplied by a fleet of three boats. Two ocean-going launches are employed in capturing turtles in Scammell’s lagoon, just south of Ensenada. The power schooner Catarina brings the turtles to San Diego, making three voyages a month. The Catarina arrived from the south last week with 45 tons of live turtles and is now at Scamell’s lagoon loading another cargo. \n The products of the turtle cannery will be distributed throughout the United Slates.”","2","""TURTLE CANNING PLANT IS CITY’S NEWEST INDUSTRY""","cargo","green","reported","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's, National City Commercial company, general estimates of weight/cargo were given to calculate. Perhaps talking about the same hunting trips as sdunion_19190422 but unsure""","1919",8,"high",NA,114.9375,NA,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
+"25","sdunion_19190305","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-03-05,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190305.2.35&srpos=101&e=-------en--20-SDDU-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","“SAN DIEGO soon will have the novel distinction of being the world's first turtle-oil exporting port. \n According to word received here yesterday from the lower coast, a gang of Mexicans is employed in Scammell’s lagoon killing turtles from which an excellent grade of soap oil is obtained. \n The lagoon is said to be fairly alive with turtles ranging from 75 to 200 pounds in weight. At one period recently the Mexicans had turtles piled up like cordwood waiting to be cut up and rendered for the oil. \n The first shipment of turtle oil is expected here from Ensenada this month. The oil sells wholesale at $1 a gallon. \n Shark oil also is imported here from the Lower California coast. This oil is utilized in the manufacture of cosmetics.”","1","""Turtle Oil New Import From Lower California to San Diego""","cargo","green","inferred","known","200","not_reported","not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's""","1919",6,"medium",NA,114.9375,90.72,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
+"26","sdunion_19190505","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-05-05,6,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190505.2.87&srpos=1&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""Loaded with 260 turtles, the power schooner Catarina arrived from Scammell’s lagoon, Lower California, late Saturday. \n The turtles are consigned to the turtle packing plant of the National City Commercial company at the upper end of the bay. \n It Is estimated that Catarina's cargo will make about 5000 gallons of turtle soup and fully 1000 cases of turtle meat. \n The local turtle soup and turtle meat packing plant is the only one of Its kind on the Pacific coast. Since the plant opened several weeks ago it has handled nearly 1000 turtles.""","2","""TURTLE CARGO ARRIVES FOR PACKING COMPANY""","260","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's""","1919",8,"high",NA,260,NA,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
+"27","sdunion_19191013","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-10-13,7,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19191013.2.94&srpos=12&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------"" ","""Two hundred and fifty turtles formed the cargo of the power schooner Catarina arriving from Scammon's lagoon yesterday morning. The turtles were consigned to the turtle soup factory at the upper end of the bay. \n The purse seiner St. Nicholas, bound from San Pedro for the lower coast, put into port yesterday with a leaking gasoline tank. A quick, reply from the S. O. S. call for plumbers enabled the St. Nicholas to leave for the fishing banks at dusk.""","2","""TURTLE CARGO HERE FOR SOUP FACTORY""","250","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1919",8,"high",NA,250,NA,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
+"28","stinson_altaca_18710210","""Alta California""",1871-02-10,NA,"51","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""San Diego, February 10th. —Arrived, schooner Cygnet, from Scammon's Lagoon, with 100 turtle; forty of them will be shipped direct to Chicago.""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","100","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1871",8,"high",NA,100,NA,1870,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
+"29","sdunion_19190422","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-04-22,5,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190422.2.50&srpos=2&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""National City Concern Packs 20 Tons of Meat and Hundreds of Cases Daily. \n The turtle soup canning plant of the National City Commercial company at the upper end of the bay is packing 20 tons of turtle meat and several hundred cases of turtle soup daily, according to members of the sea-products packing concern. \n The ocean-going launch Lion entered port from Scammell's lagoon yesterday with 96 turtles. The auxiliary schooner Catarina will sail today for the Lower California coast for another turtle cargo. \n During the last month the Catarina and other craft have brought more than 270 turtles here to be cooked and pecked at the National City plant. \n San Diego packed turtle steak and soups is in great demand among the fashionable cafes and restaurants of the Pacific coast.""","2","""BUSINESS BRISK AT TURTLE PLANT""","96","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's, National City Commercial company""","1919",8,"high",NA,96,NA,1910,"scammons_lagoon","mulege","baja_california_sur","mexico",27.7499996,-114.25
+"30","sbweekly_18770721","""Santa Barbara Weekly Press""",1877-07-21,8,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SBWP18770721.2.105&srpos=1&e=-------en--20-SBWP-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""A 700-pounder. \n This morning, as the U. S. Coast Survey steamer McArthur was steaming from her late anchorage in Beecher’s Bay, Santa Rosa Island, toward the coast, a large turtle was descried just astern of the ship, lying in the water breast upwards. It was apparently disabled, as was then supposed, by the ship’s propeller. Those who were below decks were surprised by the sudden order of “hard-a-port!” and as they rushed on deck, to inquire why, were soon shown the immense specimen swimming off, showing its head every now and then as it came to the surface to breathe. The ship was heeded directly for it, and the dingy made ready to lower, one of the men, who professed to be a great turtle-catcher, in the bows ready for the attack upon the monster. The ship having approached to within a short distance of the object of its travels, the headway was checked and the boat was lowered. A few strokes with the oars brought the dingy to close range, and when the turtle came up to breathe, over went the man, landing astride the turtle's back. No doubt the reptile was surprised, for its surprise was evinced by its diving under the boat, carrying with it its unwelcome rider. It was a question whether to sink or to dismount, so our friend chose the latter alternative, coming to the surface blowing like a porpoise, while the turtle made off with its utmost speed. The boat followed in its wake, which could easily be distinguished by the broken water. A second dash was made, and again a third, but without avail. Meanwhile, whenever the opportunity offered, rifle shots were fired from the ship, all missing the mark. The chase became very exciting, especially as upon the fourth attempt the turtle showed signs of fatigue, diving under the boats without attempting to make off. Finally the ship approached and as the turtle rose to the surface to breathe, a rifle shot from the forecastle ended its career. The boat then ran alongside the turtle, and making a line fast to its flippers, towed it to the ship. The task of preparing it to hoist on board was no small one, as the turtle was immense, and not being quite dead was continually opening and closing its huge jaws. Eventually it was hoisted on board by the fall of one of the boats, and laid on its back on deck. It was the largest specimen ever seen by any one on board, weighing probably in the neighborhood of seven hundred pounds. One of the rear flippers was gone, although the accident had not been of late occurrence. This probably accounts for its inability to make better speed through the water. It was a female of the hawk-bill species. It had evidently been stunned by contact with the ship or the ship's propeller, as the monster’s head was bruised. The measurements taken were as follows: From the tip of the nose to that of the tail 6 feet 6 inches; from the tip of one fore flipper to that of the other 8 feet; around the breast 7 feet 10 inches. The McArthur came into port this afternoon with her immense prize.""","2","""A MONSTER TURTLE""","1","leatherback","reported","known","700","6'6""","not_reported","""the location could also be categorized more broadly as 'channel_islands; other measurements included were: 'from the tip of one fore flipper to that of the other 8 feet; around the breast 7 feet 10 inches', and the reported weight is said to be an estimate ('in the neighborhood'. Species could be mis-identified as a green""","1877",9,"high",198.12,1,317.51,1870,"santa_rosa_island","santa_barbara","california","usa",33.9659616,-120.1104458
+"31","latimes_19040807","""Los Angeles Times""",1904-08-07,11,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164260036/C8D12580C68F44A0PQ/3?accountid=14026""","""James Kennedy of Sawtelle captured the carcass of a rare turtle in the surf near the long wharf today. It weighs 400 pounds and is at least seven feet long and four feet across, with peculiar ridge lengthwise across its back""","2","""RARE TURTLE""","1","leatehrback","inferred","known","400","7'","""carcass in the surf""","""Leatherback inferred based on size, and description of ridge on back. Additional size measurements include a 4-foot width""","1904",9,"high",213.36,1,181.44,1900,"santa_monica","los_angeles","california","usa",34.0218605,-118.4807555
+"32","latimes_19151105","""Los Angeles Times""",1915-11-05,10,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160160767/590BBC08B3D34453PQ/128?accountid=14026""","“SANTA MONICA, Nov. —A 200-pound turtle, the largest brought to the long wharf in two years, was part of the catch of S. Kutonoff, a fisherman in the Japanese village, who brought it here today in a boat. It was caught in a net near Santa Cruz Islands.”","2","""CATCH BIG TURTLE""","1","green","inferred","known","200","not_reported","""caught in net""","""caught near santa cruz islands, but brought into the santa monica wharf""","1915",9,"high",NA,1,90.72,1910,"santa_cruz_islands","santa_barbara","california","usa",34.0184307,-119.7250486
+"33","latimes_19030121","""Los Angeles Times""",1903-01-21,7,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164143422/C8D12580C68F44A0PQ/14?accountid=14026""","""Avalon, Jan. 20.—A very unusual catch was made this morning. Mr. and Mrs. F. J. Young of San Francisco, who were out fishing with R. S. Compton, spied two objects on the water near them, which proved to be sea turtles. Mr. Compton steered his launch quietly up along side of them and thrust a gaff hook into one and landed it in the boat. The other took alarm and escaped. The one captured was brought in and placed in the aquarium, where it is keeping company with “Path,” the big sheepshead, which has heretofore had a big tank to himself. The turtle is estimated to weigh forty pounds, and is two feet in length in its normal state, but when “rubbering” adds about six inches to the length of its neck. \n Island Pick-Ups. \n Thomas McD. Potter, who last summer proved himself the most indefatigable, as well as the most successful angler that ever struck Catalina has had a boat built for his special use, and is now here enjoying himself. The boast is of the usual fishing type, a little more roomy than most of them, equipped with a five-horse-power Union engine. Mr. Potter was out this morning and caught eight sheepshead and a tom cod, the latter a very rare fish about Catalina. The new boat was christened the “Alleen.” \n H. B. Hurd of Chicago had one of the largest catches of albicore yesterday that has been made here for some time. It numbered eighteen , and included a record fish, a huge fellow weighting forty-five pounds, with side fins like wings. Mr. Hurd was out with George Johnson, in the Kathryn. \n Gilmour Sharp has bought the Spray from Capt. McDonald, and will use it as a private fishing and pleasure boat. \n Hon. A. T. Gray and wife and Miss Elizabeth M. Gray of Vermont, Mrs. Brainerd Avery of New York City, Mrs. Ethel Gray Buxton and her two daughters, Misses Mae and Corinne, form a party who have been at Hotel Metropole for a few days. \n Superintendent R. H. Ingram of the Southern Pacific with his wife and daughter, and Mrs. W. D. Woolwine and daughter of Los Angeles, were among the arrivals at the Metropole today. \n W. T. Green and wife of Salt Lake, frequent visitors to Catalina, are again at the Grand View.""","2","""Boatman Lands Sea Turtle""","2","green","inferred","inferred","40","2'","not_reported",NA,"1903",7,"high",60.96,2,18.14,1900,"santa_catalina_island","los_angeles","california","usa",33.3889849,-118.4549433
+"34","latimes_19001211","""Los Angeles Times""",1900-12-11,7,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/163972934/441C656BF472400FPQ/328?accountid=14026""","“A sea turtle weighing about seventy-five pounds was captured at Cherry Valley last week by Richard Markel, who is in charge of the pumping station for the new town at the Isthmus at Howlands. Markel was returning from the Isthmus in a row boat when he observed something creating a commotion in a bed of kelp. which he soon ascertained to be a turtle, one of whose flippers had become entangled in the kelp and held him a prisoner Markel had a shotgun and a charge in the head finished the animal. It contained more than 200 eggs. \n Cyrus Bellows of Humboldt, Iowa, Horace Bellows and Frank L Wingard of Long Beach this morning in a short time caught two skipjacks, a yellowtail and an albicore. \n N. Swenson entered one of his moonlight scenes in a photographic contest engineered by the Grit Publishing Company of Williamsport, Pa., and was second in the list of honorable mention. \n A. A Carraher, who has been at San Jacinto Hot Springs for three weeks, is home again. \n A. P. Sawyer of the Post-Intelligencer of Seattle, is enjoying a rest at Hotel Metropole. \n Dr. Mortimer Ayers of Pasadena and Ralph M. Weaver have been spending a few days on the island. \n Alfred Reed, wife and maid, Mrs. Mary B Reed and Masters Alfred, Nelson and Charles Reed of Pittsburgh are at the Metropole for a two weeks’ stay. \n Mr. and Mrs. Compton Lundle of Oceanside, golf devotees, are enjoying themselves on the Catalina links \n Mr. and Mrs. G. M. Hobbs, Chicago; James T. Grau and wife. Santa Rosa; John T. Dickson and Miss Belle Dixon, Houston, Tex, and Mr. and Mrs J. Fleming, Trinidad, Colo, were among the arrivals at Hotel Metropole today.”","2","""ISLAND BREVITIES""","1","green","inferred","known","75","not_reported","""entangled in kelp""","""The location is given more specifically as Cherry Valley on Santa Catalina island""","1900",9,"high",NA,1,34.02,1900,"santa_catalina_island","los_angeles","california","usa",33.3889849,-118.4549433
+"35","laherald_19010825","""Los Angeles Herald""",1901-08-25,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19010825.2.58&srpos=167&e=-------en--20-LAH-161--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""(Special Correspondence.) \n SANTA BARBARA. Aug 24 —J. W. Nier of this city has classified the large sea turtle caught on Wednesday in this channel as a native of the Mediterranean sea. It is Spargis coriacia, and its being on this coast is the greatest of wonders. The thing is not edible. \n It was killed Thursday afternoon because it seemed to be dying from being so long out of water. It had been placed in a large tank house, where it was kept wet down, but in this it was apparently not happy, for with its large and powerful flippers it smashed out one of the sides of the house and got into the yard of the Larco fish market, where it was kept. It was then placed in a tank that was so small that it could not move in any way. Putting it in this cramped position was probably the cause of its death. \n How the turtle got into these waters would be an interesting study in ocean currents. It must have come from the Mediterranean. The skin and shell have been saved.""","2","""SANTA BARBARA'S BIG TURTLE COMES FROM FAR OFF WATERS""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""The article reports the species to be 'Spargis coriacia' which seems to be some kind of leatherback or similar based on googling. It also notes that Santa Barbara is an unusual place (for the time) to find a turtle of this species.""","1901",8,"high",NA,1,NA,1900,"santa_barbara_channel","na","california","usa",34.2419435,-119.89
+"36","sbweekly_19010822","""Santa Barbara Weekly Press""",1901-08-22,5,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SBWP19010822.2.42&srpos=3&e=-------en--20-SBWP-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""Measures Over Six Feet in Length and Will Weigh More Than Six Hundred Pounds. \n Landed on the Beach After a Terrible Struggle—Is Considered of Great Value—Will be Put on Exhibition—Exceptionally Good Eating. \n Sebastian Larco is in possession of a large turtle caught by his men yesterday afternoon, which is considered to be worth hundreds of dollars to a museum or institute. \n Every afternoon about 4:30 o’clock the fishermen in his employ pull in their nets, which are set in the channel about a mile and a half out. Yesterday, while they were returning, they noticed several large splashes resembling a whale in distress, located in the center of the kelp. They immediately turned their boat in that direction, and on arriving at the spot noticed that the cause of the disturbance was a monster turtle, caught in the kelp and unable to extricate itself. After considerable hard work a rope was fastened to it and an effort made to bring it into the boat. Finally the monster was dragged in,  but scarcely had it been landed before it struck one of the men with its wing, or fin, sending him headlong into the ocean, and then crawled back into the water. \n The fisherman succeeded in getting back into the boat again, and, regardless of his wet condition, set about to securely fasten the rope to their prize. A terrible struggle ensued, the monster of the ocean fighting viciously for freedom, and the two men working to prevent him from doing so. \n At last the work was accomplished, but it was considered better not to again attempt to land him in the boat. The rope was securely fastened and the turtle towed to the beach and landed. \n A wagon was called and their capture was safely landed at Larco’s residence. Then another struggle began. The turtle objected to being taken into the fish house, and three powerful men were unable to master him. They were thrown aside by the turtle, carried upon his back and occasionally thrown down. More assistance was needed, and not until six men with ropes attached to the monster’s body were they able to conquer him. \n Captain Larco was interviewed by a representative of The Press and his account of the capture was as follows: \n “The boys have secured a great prize. It is a pure green turtle, and of great value, not only as a curiosity, but its meat is of the finest quality. During all my experience of almost half a century, I never heard of but one other being captured on the Pacific coast. It was taken by government officials aboard the survey boat McCarty, near San Francisco, many years ago. I should judge that the one captured by the boys weighed about 1000 pounds. They do not as a rule enter shallow water, but live thousands of miles at sea. It was a great surprise to me when I saw it.” \n The exact weigth of the turtle is not known, yet it will weigh over 600 pounds. IT measures almost seven feet in length by four feet across the back, and from tip to tip of the front fins over twelve feet. \n Mr. Larco will exhibit it, beginning tomorrow. An effort will be made to have it put in the bath house, where thousands of people could see it. By throwing salt water over it daily, it can be kept alive indefinitely, and no doubt a large price will be offered for it by some museum. \n Captain Alexander of the Santa Rosa was at the dock when it was brought in, and will ascertain its value when he reaches San Francisco. \n Plano Larco and Pietro Marfolli were the fishermen who captured it.”","2","""MONSTER GREEN TURTLE CAUGHT BY FISHERMEN IN THE CHANNEL""","1","green","reported","known","1000","7'","""caught in the kelp""","""specific location not given, just says 'in the channel', which I assume to be the santa barbara channel. Further measurements given as: 'It measures almost seven feet in length by four feet across the back, and from tip to tip of the front fins over twelve feet.'""","1901",10,"high",213.36,1,453.59,1900,"santa_barbara_channel","na","california","usa",34.2419435,-119.89
+"37","sbweekly_18770804","""Santa Barbara Weekly Press""",1877-08-04,8,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SBWP18770804.2.61&srpos=4&e=-------en--20-SBWP-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","“A deep-sea turtle of extraordinary size, weighing nearly 400 pounds, was exhibited in Newport, Rhode Island, on Thursday. It was caught in the vicinity. — [Boston Weekly Transcript. \n We can beat that. A turtle weighing nearly 1200 pounds was caught in the Santa Barbara channel week before last.”","1","""A Bigger One.""","1","green","inferred","known","1200","not_reported","not_reported","""possible other species given the size, however, no notes on species are given""","1877",7,"high",NA,1,544.31,1870,"santa_barbara_channel","na","california","usa",34.2419435,-119.89
+"38","sbmp_19010925","""Morning Press""",1901-09-25,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP19010925.2.11&srpos=7&e=-------en--20-MP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------"" ","“Dr. L. G. Yates has Charge of the Interesting Work. \n THIRTY-FIVE FEET OF STITCHING \n Will be on Exhibition in Some up Town store in a Short Time—Monster Curiosity. \n Unless something happens mighty quick, the big turtle which was captured by Larco in the channel last month, will not know himself at the resurrection. For the past four days Dr. L. G. Yates has been engaged in mounting the monster. A representative of The Press called on the doctor yesterday. Aproned and bloused, he was stitching away, closing the last seam in the turtle’s hide. There is something like 35 feet of stitching on the monster’s body. \n In a few days the work will be completed, the eyes put in to give it a more lifelike appearance, and the former inhabitant of the briny deep will look complacently on the moving crowd from some up-town window. He has already been treated to a most remarkable diet, one that will last him for some time to come. Dr. Yates was just ramming home the last charge of dessert yesterday. It consisted of a generous stepmother supply of excelsior. Previous to that course he had been treated to a full meal of blocks, hay, straw, boards and several pieces of wagon-tire steel. There is nothing delicate now about the appetite of the turtle. \n The skin has yet to be treated with stains in order that it may retain its natural color. Then it will be varnished, and the head poised at the proper angle. The specimen will be one of the most interesting caught in the waters of the western coast.”","2","""LARCO'S TURTLE BEING STUFFED""","1","leatherback","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Perhaps given size could be a leatherback""","1901",7,"high",NA,1,NA,1900,"santa_barbara_channel","na","california","usa",34.2419435,-119.89
+"39","laherald_19050105","""Los Angeles Herald""",1905-01-05,12,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19050105.2.130&srpos=4&e=-------en--20-LAH-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------#""","“CAPTURED IN WATERS NEAR SANTA BARBARA \n Creature Measures Seven Feet From Head to Tall and Its Weight Is Eight Hundred Pounds \n Special to The Herald. \n SANTA BARBARA, Jan. 4.— Captain Gourley of the pleasure fleet and one of his assistants, Albert Stafford, captured a huge sea turtle yesterday afternoon which proved to be the largest specimen ever caught in these waters. When hauled onto the pleasure pier and carefully anchored down with stout ropes to prevent trouble it was estimated that the turtle weighed at least 800 pounds, and several competent judges placed its weight at considerably above this figure. \n The turtle was first discovered by Stafford, who came across it while rowing alone in a small skiff. The reptile was entangled in some kelp, and was asleep at the time Stafford sighted it. Not liking to venture too near in so small a boat, Stafford returned and was joined by Gourley. The pair went after the turtle in a sail boat and succeeded in gaffing it through one of the flippers, after which they managed to throw ropes over it and tow it to the pier. The turtle put up a hard fight all the way and several times attempted to clamber aboard the boat. It was finally hoisted to the pier, after a few exciting moments for those who were engaged in the work. \n The picture was taken within a few minutes of the time the big turtle was finally made fast to the pier. The turtle measured seven feet from head to tail.”","2","""MONSTER TURTLE TAKEN PRISONER""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","inferred","800","7'","""entangled in some kelp""","""I thought it would be a leatherback based on the image and the descriptions of size, but unsure. Location just generally says 'near Santa Barbara""","1905",8,"high",213.36,1,362.87,1900,"santa_barbara","santa_barbara","california","usa",34.398587,-119.7498556
+"40","laherald_19010822","""Los Angeles Herald""",1901-08-22,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19010822.2.43&srpos=82&e=-------en--20-LAH-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","“(Special Dispatch to The Herald.) \n SANTA BARBARA, Aug. 21.—The largest and strongest turtle ever caught, an all around record breaker, was captured this afternoon after a long and terrible struggle in the channel here. The captor was the fiteen-year-old son of a fisherman named A. Larco. The trutle is a 700-pounder, and is of a species unknown to the host of Mexican coast and South seas sailors. \n Young Larco found the turtle caught in the kelp that fills the water for about one mile off this coast. The gigantic creature was thrashing about to get itself untangled. Larco tried to get it into his boat, but the thing fought viciously. It struck Pietro Marfolli, who was with Larco, with one of its flippers and knocked the man the whole length of the boat. He was bruised and stunned. \n Finally the lad lassoed the turtle and towed it ashore. It struggled all of the way and it took two hours to make the mile. On shore Captain Alexander of the steamer Santa Rosa and his sailors saw the creature but they said they had never seen its like. Down its back there run seven high ridges. It is very vicious and strikes at all who get near it. The weight and size are the astounding features as well as the fact that the turtle was caught by a boy. It will be exhibited here and may be sold to the coming circus.”","2","""GIGANTIC TURTLE IS CAPTURED OFF THE SANTA BARBARA COAST""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","inferred","700","not_reported","""caught in the kelp""","""given the size and description (having seven high ridges), this is likely a leatherback, and therefore this identification would be inferred""","1901",8,"high",NA,1,317.51,1900,"santa_barbara","santa_barbara","california","usa",34.398587,-119.7498556
+"41","sbweekly_19010829","""Santa Barbara Weekly Press""",1901-08-29,6,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SBWP19010829.2.38&srpos=2&e=-------en--20-SBWP-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""The monster turtle that was captured by the Larco boys Wednesday afternoon died about 1 o’clock yesterday afternoon. It is supposed that It was Injured while being brought to the beach, and alto when being placed in the fish house. Many people were at the Larco residence during the morning to view the curiosity, and various scientists were searching for the proper species to which it belonged. There is some talk of having it stufl,ed. The idea is a good one, and were it carried out would make a valuable addition to the collection of curios now at the chamber of commerce. THE TURTLE. Dr. Yates contributes the following account of the ocean turtle, classifying the Santa Barbara find: The curious reptile known by the name of turtles, or tortoises, are remarkable for the peculiarity of being a vertebrate animals Wi.a their bones worn on the outside of the lody. They are divided into several general classes with different habits and characteristics. Some are confined to the land, others to the fresh water, and still others inhabit the ocean, which they rarely leave. . Some of these animals furnish the tortoise-shell of commerce. The kind most valued for this product is the ""hawkbill turtle,” which is found on portions of the west coast of Mexico; while the flesh of this turtle is not considered edible, its eggs are thought to be a great delicacy; the female fs very prolific, laying a large number of eggs at intervals of three weeks, which is also the period required to hatch the young. They are laid In the sand and hatched by the heat of the sun. When the hatched young have to run the gauntlet of many enemies before they reach the sea, toward which they instinctively rush. The writer has a specimen of the above species. The “green turtle” is the best known of the sea turtles. Its flesh is highly prized by epicures. The specimen captured by Mr. Larco’s fishermen is the “Luth,” or leathery turtle (Sphargis coriacia), so called from the fact of its having no horny plates, the bones being covered with a strong leathery skin. The eyelids open vertically Instead of horizontally, giving it a comical expression. About the back are seven longitudinal ridges, sharp and slightly toothed. The Jaw has two curved sharply pointed teeth, and the extremity of the lower Jaw is strongly hooped. The flesh is not good for food, as it causes many symptoms of poisoning in those who eat it It feeds on fish, and other marine animals, and lays about three hundred and fifty , eggs at a time. One of its best known hatching places is the Turtle Islands of Florida. It Inhabits the warm regions of the Atlantic, Pacific and Indian oceans, and grows to a great size, often weighing more than 1600 pounds and attains a length of 8 feet From the great development of its fore legs, it ib a good long-distance swimmer, and often ventures far out to sea, and is sometimes driven to strange countries. In a specimen eight feet long, the fore legs, which are devoid of claws, are about thfce feet long. Us general color Is a dark blackish olive, spotted and blotched with light yellow or white.""","2","""Monster Turtle.""","1","leatherback","reported","inferred","not_reported","8'","not_reported","""specific location not given, just mentions Santa Barbara. Exact measurements of size and weight are also not given, but general measurements for the species as a whole is provided""","1901",8,"high",243.84,1,NA,1900,"santa_barbara","santa_barbara","california","usa",34.398587,-119.7498556
+"42","slomt_19050324","""Morning Tribune"" ",1905-03-24,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SLOMT19050324.2.5&srpos=4&e=-------en--20-SLOMT-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""E.S. Hoyt Has Negative of a Rare Curiosity—Photographs Furnished to Academy of Science \n E. S. Hoyt is the owner of a kodak negative of rare value. Prof. F. M. Anderson of the Academy of Science of San Francisco has sent here to have some photographs developed from this negative from which wood cuts will be made to be used in embellishing a write-up soon to be printed. The subject of the photog[r]aph is a mammoth turtle captured in Santa Barbara in 1901 and seen by Mr. Hoyt at that time when he secured the negative while the huge animal was on a pair of trucks. \n It will be remembered that a monster turtle was captured in the waters off the coast of Santa Barbara last summer. The two turtles were alike, the latter being somewhat the larger, and are the only two of the kind ever captured in California waters. Neither lived and it happens that Mr. Hoyt has the best negative known to exits of the first turtle. \n Some of the pictures were finished yesterday at the Lewis gallery and sent to the Academy of Science. The matter being prepared for the writeup will doubtless be of considerable value and interest as the turtles were monsters and of a very rare species.""","2","""PICTURE OF BIG TURTLE""","1","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Though the year of publishing is 1905, this turtle was captured in 1901. The species is unspecified and no actual size is given, so it is inferred to be green, however, given the rarity and how it is labelled as a 'mammoth', it is possibly a leatherback. The article references another turtle of the same species being captured in a nearby area the prior year and says: 'The two turtles were alike, the latter being somewhat the larger, and are the only two of the kind ever captured in California waters.'""","1905",8,"high",NA,1,NA,1900,"santa_barbara","santa_barbara","california","usa",34.398587,-119.7498556
+"43","sbmp_18930721","""Morning Press""",1893-07-21,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP18930721.2.32&srpos=106&e=-------en--20-MP-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Mr. N. Gutierrez, in company with Jennie Larco, caught a big sea turtle yesterday weighing 60 pounds. This is the first one caught at Santa Barbara in the past four or five years.""","2","""CITY AND VICINITY""","1","green","inferred","inferred","60","not_reported","not_reported","""Included text is not the entire article, only the relevant text of the story. Article mentions that it is rather rare to find a turtle in Santa Barbara at this time (first one in the past 4-5 years)""","1893",7,"high",NA,1,27.22,1890,"santa_barbara","santa_barbara","california","usa",34.398587,-119.7498556
+"44","latimes_19050108","""Los Angeles Times""",1905-01-08,4,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164264960/D4971430CE2047A5PQ/301?accountid=14026""","“Large Santa Barbara Turtle and Its Captors. \n Capt. George Gourley and Albert Stafford of Santa Barbara caught a monster sea turtle in the ocean near the pleasure wharf of that city Tuesday. Still alive the big animal specimen was hauled to the pleasure wharf and made fast with ropes. Its estimated weight is 800 pounds. \n Stafford first saw the turtle while rowing in a small skiff. It was entangled in seaweed and seemed to be asleep. Stafford went ashore and found Gourley and the two put out in a sailboat. The managed to gaff it in one of the flippers and throw ropes about it so they could bring it to the pier. The turtle made fierce resistance. \n According to a classification of Prof. Dodge of the High School of Santa Barbara the monster specimen is a Sphargis coriacea, or leather-backed turtle. When it was hauled to the wharf there were a number of small fish known as remoras attached to it.”","2","""Huge Leather-back Taken from the Sea""","1","leatherback","reported","known","800","not_reported","""asleep entangled in seaweed""","""article lists species as leatherback, and also mentions the historic latin name, sphargis coriacea""","1905",10,"high",NA,1,362.87,1900,"santa_barbara","santa_barbara","california","usa",34.398587,-119.7498556
+"45","sdunion_19110922","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1911-09-22,10,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19110922.2.111&srpos=1&e=-------en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle+AND+alejandro+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+doves+NOT+house-------""","""Legends of Peninsula Say Old Tortoise Alejandro Was Tamed by Selkirk. \n Old Alejandro has been captured alive, away down south of San Quintin, and now lives ignominiously upon the floor of the San Diego Fish company’s market at the foot of D street. \n Alejandro, the pet turtle of Robinson Crusoe, according to the legends of the Mexicans of Lower California, has been the terror of all fishermen since the whaling days, and so tradition says, in the days of the whalers fought the whaling boats. Antedating the days of the whaling ships that sailed hence from the coast of Maine, the old turtle wrested for his domain with the Spanish galleons, gold laden, and Manila bound. \n Many are the tales that the Mexicans tell their little children of the prowess of Old Alejandro, the turtle that knew no fear and that could sink a whale-boat with one stroke of his powerful flipper. Like the bugaboo of the northland, he would “get the little children if they did not watch out.” \n They say that he was gentle once, several hundred years ago, when he followed Robinson Crusoe around the shores of Magdalena bay, although he is not mentioned in the historical accounts that tell of the presence there of Alexander Selkirk (Robinson Crusoe. It is a matter of history that Selkirk was at that time the second-mate of a British warship. \n Legend following history, the folklore of the peninsula, is full of incidents in the life of old Alejandro. \n He was captured two weeks ago, speared by an Indian fisherman many miles below San Quintin. \n In company with two others of his kind, the famous old tortoise is awaiting the headsman’s knife and will soon round out his semi-millenium in a pot of soup on the range of a restauranteur.""","1","""Robinson Crusoe's Pet Turtle Speared Near Port of San Quintin""","1","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""much of this report appears to be fiction or exaggerated for entertainment, but some aspects may be true.""","1911",6,"medium",NA,1,NA,1910,"san_quintin","san_quintin","baja_california","mexico",30.5711336,-115.9413448
+"46","laherald_19040507","""Los Angeles Herald""",1904-05-07,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19040507.2.58&srpos=17&e=-------en--20-LAH-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","“1200-Pounder Is Named for President \n (Special to The Herald.) \n SAN PEDRO. May 6.—""Teddy Roosevelt,"" a 1200-pound turtle, which was caught yesterday by Capt. George Evitch twelve miles off the coast, is to go to the St. Louis exposition. Hundreds of people took a look at the monster today. As soon as arrangements can be made it will be sent east by rail. \n The turtle measures seven feet in length and is four feet wide. From tip to tip of his flippers the distance is about ten feet. It has the appearance of being several hundred years old. Three ridges run lengthwise on the shell. \n Several bullet holes are records of previous ineffectual attempts at his capture. The shell is of a greenish hue and very rough. The turtle Is classified scientifically as ""festudo lyra."" \n When angered the turtle spouts like a whale and threshes the water into foam. It is believed that a school of these turtles has been carried here in some ocean current. The steamer Santa Cruz, which came into port today, captured nearly a dozen smaller specimens in the channel this morning.”","1","""HUGE TURTLE IS TO GO TO FAIR""","1","leatherback","reported","inferred","1200","7'","not_reported","""'festudo lyra' = testudo lyra, a historic name for leatherbacks; four feet in width and from tip to tip of flippers is about 10 feet; article also notes almost a dozen more smaller leatherbacks were caught a few days later in the same area""","1904",7,"high",213.36,1,544.31,1900,"san_pedro","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7395016,-118.28357
+"47","laherald_19040509","""Los Angeles Herald""",1904-05-09,10,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19040509.2.146&srpos=88&e=-------en--20-LAH-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""SAN PEDRO, May 8—lt is not a certainty that the big turtle caught by George Evitch will be sent to St. Louis. The managers of the Chutes in Los Angeles have offered Capt. Evitch $500 for the turtle. The price set upon his head is just double that amount.""","2","""Chutes Want Monster Turtle""","1","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""could potentially be another species, given that the size is noted to be so large. Cannot confirm without further desriptions""","1904",7,"high",NA,1,NA,1900,"san_pedro","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7395016,-118.28357
+"48","sppilot_19390310","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1939-03-10,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19390310.2.5&srpos=60&e=-------en--20-SPNP-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------:","""A replica of the big leatherback turtle which was captured off San Pedro several weeks ago is crated at Cabrillo beach marine museum and awaiting shipment to the San Francisco exposition. With the specimen is a porpoise and several rare fish which will go on display as San Pedro’s contribution to the exposition.""","1","""Museum to Ship Turtle to Fair""","1","leatherback","reported","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be reporting the same turtle observation as sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390830, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390529 (redundant). While the location is reported in this article as san_pedro, it is likely more specifically point_vincente, assuming that it is indeed the same specimen as the other four articles, though admittedly the connection is a little weaker for this article. Timing is a few months off.""","1939",8,"high",NA,1,NA,1930,"san_pedro","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7395016,-118.28357
+"49","sppilot_19390529","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1939-05-29,22,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19390519.2.249&srpos=110&e=-------en--20-SPNP-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""It was started as a hobby enterprise several years ago. Today it’s an institution and so great is its lure to thousands of visitors from all over the country that plans are being laid to build an elaborate building especially for it. \n This briefly is the history of the Cabrillo Beach Marine museum, which provides an attractive point of interest for visitors to the harbor area. \n Last year when a 1,100-pound giant turtle, caught in Mexican waters, was on display, thousands of visitors’ came especially to see the giant specimen. And hundreds of letters arrived from various sections of the country seeking information. \n A plaster cast life-size replica of the turtle in now on display at the San Francisco Golden Gate Exposition. \n The museum was started as a hobby in order to provide a place where persons interested in the nature lore of the Pacific coast could learn about the flora and fauna of the sea and shore, and also bring their own private collections of birds, fish, shells and other specimens for identification. \n From this beginning the museum has grown until it now contains hundreds of fascinating specimens of the under sea and shore denizens of the Pacific. Growth of the museum has been assisted through donations to it by various expeditions to Mexico, Central and South America, including those of Captain G. Allen Hancock, well known sportsman-explorer. The museum is under the direction of Dr. William L. Lloyd, former University of Pennsylvania naturalist.""","2","""Permanent Building Planned for Cabrillo Beach Museum""","1","leatherback","reported","inferred","1100","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be reporting the same turtle/observation as sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390830, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390310. This article is reporting in retrospect and therefore timing of this article is off from the time of capture by about a year. The location of capture is not given, so I reported it as San Pedro, however based on the other sources it seems point vincente would be more accurate of a location. Additionally, the species is noted as reported, even though not explictly stated, based on previous reports.""","1939",8,"high",NA,1,498.95,1930,"san_pedro","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7395016,-118.28357
+"50","sppilot_19130920","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1913-09-20,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19130920.2.23&srpos=94&e=-------en--20-SPNP-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Capt. Dan Kain's launch Tillikum stumbled onto a Japanese razor-back turtle sunning of the surface of the deep about four miles off the end of the breakwater. By dexterous manipulation the quarry was potted with a harpoon and towed to the Long Beach municipal dock alive Saturday. He measured about 6x4 feet and weight undressed approximately 1200. \n Old timers hereabouts classify the speciman as a member of a Japanese family of razor-backs never before seen in these waters and probably carried hither by the Japanese current. Veteran fishermen say it’s the choicest table product of all deep sea turtles. \n Capt. Kain will have the shell and skin mounted and will in a double sense try out the oil for its virtues as a rheumatism cure. The skeleton is still on exhibition at the end of the municipal pier. The meat was sold at a local fish market.""","2","""CAPTURED JAPANESE TURTLE WEIGHING 1200 POUNDS""","1","leatherback","inferred","inferred","1200","6'","""sunning of the surface of the deep""","""The article notes the dimensions to be about 6x4, and the location is inferred as san_pedro, because that is where the newspaper is from, but it was said to be 'about four miles off the end of the breakwater.' and then was brought to the Long Beach dock. The species is named as a 'Japanese razor-back' but given the size, it's most likely a leatherback; it is noted as being rare in this area""","1913",8,"high",182.88,1,544.31,1910,"san_pedro","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7395016,-118.28357
+"51","latimes_19090718","""Los Angeles Times""",1909-07-18,10,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/159327339/fulltextPDF/831523F0C774447FPQ/1?accountid=14026""","“FISHERMAN TRIES VAINLY TO GET IT IN PORT ALIVE. \n Monster Seven Feet Long and Believed to Be Thousand Years Old. Second One of Species Ever Captured Off Coast Kills Itself in Struggle for Freedom. \n SAN PEDRO. July 17.—After trying for two days to tow into port alive a big turtle of rare species, Steve Williams, an East San Pedro fisherman, arrived home this morning, but the monster was dead. The turtle became entangled in a buoy rope attached to his nets, and ropes were made fast to its flippers. Had not the engine of Williams's launch become disabled, the turtle would not have killed itself in its efforts to regain its freedom. \n It is the second of its kind ever captured in these waters and weighs about 1000 pounds. It is estimated that it was 1000 years old. It was more than seven feet in length, and measured nearly nine feet between the extremities of its front fllþpers. \n Unlike the land turtle, It has two long front flippers and two short hind ones, like those of a seal. The shell is soft, with five ridges down the back. Fishermen occasionally see turtles of this species in the fishing banks, but say they are very timid, and that a boat cannot get close enough to capture them. This one was caught in twenty fathoms of water. \n Williams will have his prize mounted and sell it to a museum.”","2","""THOUSAND-POUND TURTLE CAUGHT.""","1","leatherback","inferred","inferred","1000","7'","not_reported","""species is inferred based on description of a soft shell and five ridges on the back""","1909",7,"high",213.36,1,453.59,1900,"san_pedro","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7395016,-118.28357
+"52","sdut_19610707","""San Diego Union Tribune""",1961-07-07,NA,NA,"""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-13C1722552DF9770%402437488-13C16EB6A122C338%402-13C16EB6A122C338%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=6&pdate=1961-07-07"" ","""A 1,000-pound turtle, described as the largest ever seen in Southern California waters, lies on dock at Long Beach. A fishing boat saw the turtle about two miles off San PEdro break-water and used grappling hooks to make the catch. Turtle was towed into Long Beach harbor.""","1","""Record Catch In Coast Waters""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","1000","not_reported","not_reported","""although the species is inferred, there is a fairly clear photo with a leatherback. This, in addition to size, makes it a fairly clear identification. Location specifically is given as 'two miles off San Pedro breakwater'""","1961",7,"high",NA,1,453.59,1960,"san_pedro","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7395016,-118.28357
+"53","latimes_19050102","""Los Angeles Times""",1905-01-02,13,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164327510/17A6E603F61A4B73PQ/416?accountid=14026""","“MAMMOTH TURTLE. \n On the return from the whistling buoy to San Pedro breakwater, a huge turtle was closely passed. It was clumsily flapping its way to windward, the huge forward flippers seeming to cause but little progress. The commodore got the pikepole ready as the yacht was swung around for the encounter, but the huge beast did not wait for the tourney and disappeared. This turtle was caught the same night in the nets of the Italian fishermen and brought into San Pedro the next morning, where it turned the scales at 1600 pounds and was sent to the St. Louis Exposition as a California product. It probably came, however, from more southerly islands and seemed to be of the same species which Darwin found!\ in the Galapagos Islands, and which he described in his ""Naturalist's Trip Round the World.” This species attains great age and size, is possessed of much sugacity and makes long ocean passages from one island to another. How this one got so far off his course is a mystery.”","1","""PARADISE FOR YACHTSMEN""","1","leatherback","inferred","inferred","1600","not_reported","not_reported","""the species is not specified, however, it claims to be the same one that Darwin found in the Galapagos, and makes long ocean passages from one island to another. Also note the size.""","1905",6,"medium",NA,1,725.75,1900,"san_pedro","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7395016,-118.28357
+"54","latimes_19020812","""Los Angeles Times""",1902-08-12,5,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164090597/17A6E603F61A4B73PQ/420?accountid=14026""","“E. W. Scripps of San Diego, who, with his family and party of friends, in two yachts and a power launch, were in the bay here a week ago, are now sojourning at the Isthmus, and will probably remain there for the next two months. Mr. Scripps has just chartered two more boats here, the yacht Avalon, and the fishing launch Mero, and they went up to the Isthmus this morning. The outing is taken for the benefit of the health of one of Mr. Scripps’s boys, and the family is accompanied by a tutor, a physician, a secretary and a storekeeper, besides servants. They will use the Avalon for making trips to San Nicholas and perhaps other islands of the channel group. They are out for a good time, and expense cuts no figure. The party is well pleased with the Isthmus, and they are negotiating for a lease of the Barracks and the cottages near by. \n D. J. Alberga, with the Pacific Coast Biscuit Company, had the honor of bringing in the only big fish today. He got a 195-pound black sea bass, which kept him out of mischief for an hour and a half. A picturesque feature of his catch was when his boatman, “Shorty” Bencke, skipper of the Catalina, finding the fish was about to carry the line into his propeller, jumped overboard without divesting himself of his clothing and diving under the boat prevented the fouling of the wheel and saved the fish! With Mr. Alberga were M. E. Conboy and F. J. Matthie of Tucson. \n The launch Idlewild took a party to the Isthmus. The trip was arranged by Mrs. L. J. Merritt for the members of the Moscos and Cosmos Clubs of Pasadena and their friends on the island. The party consisted of Mrs. L. J. Merritt, Mrs. George Kelly and Mrs. J. H. Farley of Stanford; Mr. and Mrs. Ammerman of Duluth, Mr. and Mrs. Earl Barnhart of Chicago; Misses Alice Ammerman, Daisy White, Evelyn Merritt, Bertha Merritt, Daisy White: Messrs. Horace White, John Newkirk of Berkeley, W. Arthur McNally and Mr. Newberry. \n F. L. Hober, W. H. and Harry Bollyn ran up against a fighting shark this morning. They were out for sand dabs, of which they made a good catch, and were coming home, trolling a line for yellowtail, when a white shark about four feet in length took the bait. He fought hard till they got him up near the boat, and then he made a lunge for them, and came near landing in the boat. He made several more tries at it seeming determined to get at them, when one of the men clubbed an ore and dispatched the vicious brute. \n H. T. Kendall of Pasadena, who now has the honor of holding the word’s record for biggest fish, has method in having the monster 419-pound fish mounted. He is vice-president of the Potter Hotel Company at Santa Barbara, and proposes to give the big bass a place of special honor in the hall or lobby of the new hotel, which is the largest lobby contained in any hotel in the United States, which makes it appropriate that it should contain the largest fish. \n Capt. Trefethen of the Hermosa reports having seen an enormous sea turtle yesterday on his trip over from San Pedro. It was not more than a hundred feet away from the track of the vessel, and he had a good view of the creature, which he asserts was from eight to ten feet across the back. The captain says this is the largest turtle he has ever seen in his experience of more than twenty years in these waters. \n The Mascot took out a fishing party yesterday, composed of Miss Carrie Connell of Phoenix, K. C. Kitt of Tucson, Misses Corinne Frances, Celia Knowles, Leota Latimer, G. Campbell, W. Culver and A. Cooper of Los Angeles, who caught seven yellowtail and three barracuda. \n James Burton Adams, editor of the Denver Post, with his wife, is again visiting Santa Catalina. Mr. Adams says they design taking up their residence in Los Angeles at no distant day. \n Mrs. Helen Safford, who has been here the guest of Mrs. Ed Stanton for a fortnight, left for her home in Los Angeles this morning regretted by a large circle of friends. \n J. A. Simpson, master mechanic of the Southern Pacific Railway at Los Angeles, accompanied by his wife, is taking an outing on the island for a week. \n Miss Sylvia Bowman of Pasadena and Miss Mabel Leaverton of Hollywood, are guests of Miss G. Myrtle Stotts at Beach Haven cottage. \n Maj. H. C. Eggers of Fresno joined a number of his townsmen and friends at the Grand View for a few days.”","2","""ISLAND BREVITIES""","1","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","10'","not_reported","""specifically size is given as 8 to 10 feet across the back""","1902",7,"high",304.8,1,NA,1900,"san_pedro","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7395016,-118.28357
+"55","stinson_sdunion_18900606","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1890-06-06,NA,"47","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""National [City]. 'Turtles of large dimensions are reported numerous around the wharves, and are quite annoying to anglers.'""","1","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","not_reported","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""said to be large, no precise number or weight""","1890",4,"medium",NA,NA,NA,1890,"san_diego","san_diego","california","usa",32.8245525,-117.1089785
+"56","stinson_sdunion_18900606","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1890-06-06,NA,"47","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""National [City]. 'Turtles of large dimensions are reported numerous around the wharves, and are quite annoying to anglers.'""","1","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","not_reported","hawksbill","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""said to be large, no precise number or weight.  And same observation as row above but with a different species, because both are inferred to be present based on other articles""","1890",4,"medium",NA,NA,NA,1890,"san_diego","san_diego","california","usa",32.8245525,-117.1089785
+"57","stinson_sdunion_19030524","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1903-05-24,NA,"48","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Peter Stanovitch brought in fourteen large turtles which he had caught during the night, the largest one weighing 500 pounds [230 kg]. The turtles were captured in the shallow waters of the mud flats where they breed at this time of the year.""","2","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","14","green","inferred","inferred","500","not_reported","""mud flats""","""fishermen supposedly believed that these turtles were the children of the children of the turtles from baja california that escaped from their corral in the san diego bay in 1857, but evidence is unclear""","1903",8,"high",NA,14,226.8,1900,"san_diego","san_diego","california","usa",32.8245525,-117.1089785
+"58","latimes_18990204","""Los Angeles Times""",1899-02-04,15,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/163853622/590BBC08B3D34453PQ/70?accountid=14026""","“Among the fishermen outside yesterday in the choppy sea were two young men from San Diego, who had great sport catching a school of sea turtles into which their smack happened to run. The turtles were upon the surface of the sea, sunning themselves, and floating lazily along. The boys rigged up strong lines with nooses, and stole silently alongside the biggest fellow, who seemed to be asleep. With a quick jerk one of his flippers was caught, and the rope jerked taut. \n There was a lively sequel, and the big turtle pulled himself together and tried to dive. He could not, and reappearing, he started on a fast gait to the southward. The toys gradually hauled him to the gunwale, where with another line they got him into the boat. He weighed 276 pounds. In this manner they caught thirteen big turtles, running from eighty to 245 pounds. It is not often that the turtles are found off this part of the coast, as they prefer the warm water of the Gulf of California. \n The fish catch yesterday was: Halibut, 100; lobsters, 150 pounds, trout, 100, croakers, 175; silver smelt. 135 pounds.”","1","""FUN WITH SEA TURTLES.""","13","green","inferred","inferred","276","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1899",6,"medium",NA,13,125.19,1890,"san_diego","san_diego","california","usa",32.8245525,-117.1089785
+"59","sdunion_19100905","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1910-09-05,15,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19100905.2.130&srpos=14&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""Turtle Caught by Steve Zolezzie While He Was Fishing in Ocean South of San Diego. \n Ocean-Going Monster Weighing 487 Pounds Becomes Entangled in Net \n A giant green turtle which became entangled in nets of Steve Zolezzi, a local fisherman, was captured by him and a companion in the kelp near the boundary line yesterday morning. \n The big chelonian was found about 5 o'clock, when the fishermen went out to pay their usual morning visit to their nets. It had completely enmeshed itself and its captor worked hard for several hours before it was finally freed and the nets were hauled away from its vicious back-shaped jaws. One section of the nets was practically destroyed, involving a loss of about$50 to the owners. \n Despite the enormous size of the turtle it was finally dragged into Zolezzi’s boat by the fisherman and his companion and brought to San Diego. It was landed at the wharf of the San Diego fish market, where it was the cynosure of curious eyes during the entire day. It will not be killed until Monday, when, according to the statements of the market management last night, the edible portions of its huge hulk will be sold to Harry Rudder of the Union cafe. \n The turtle tipped the scales at 487 pounds.""","2","""GIANT TURTLE ENTRAPPED; SOON TO BE IN THE SOUP""","1","green","reported","inferred","487","not_reported","""in the kelp near the boundary line""","""note that the name of the man who caught turtle is spelled two different ways (typo). While 487 lbs seems perhaps unrealistically heavy, it is an exact weight reported to be measured by scales, not an estimate.""","1910",9,"high",NA,1,220.9,1910,"san_diego","san_diego","california","usa",32.8245525,-117.1089785
+"60","sdunion_19070621","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1907-06-21,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19070621.2.27&srpos=26&e=-------en--20-SDDU-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","""Monster Found By Fisherman Off Coast Evidently In Toils Before \n HAS NAME AND DATE BURNED IN SHELL \n Evidently Picked Up By British Ship Sea Bride in August 1881 \n Of all the peculiar and mysterious things given up by the sea, one of the most puzzling ever found by San Diego fishermen was discovered yesterday in the shape of a gigantic turtle captured in a fish net by Julius Solissa, who is considered the cleverest fisherman in the city, despite his youth. \n Solissa, who is employed by Charles Holmquist, president of the San Diego Fish company, went out in his big gasoline fishing launch to the cod banks on his regular trip last Wednesday morning at 1:30 o'clock. Nothing out of the ordinary routine of fishing occurred, as the running was slow and poor for hours. \n Young Solissa's trained eye soon discovered that something was wrong with the nets and the disturbance suddenly grew, so that it appeared as though some monster were churning the water under the boat. \n Solissa believed at first that some of the seals which patrol the nets in big herds had become entangled in the nets and nettled back to wait until they had been drowned by being held under the water for a long time. \n But the row continued and Solissa finally concluding that a large fish must be the cause of the trouble proceeded to drag in the nets to look them over. \n He was greatly surprised to find an enormous turtle, which struggled so violently that it wound the strings of the net around its flippers and cut them until the blood ran and the flesh was torn to shreds. \n Solissa lowered the net and made off to find his mates in the other launches. Returning, three other fishermen aided Julius to' make the turtle fast to the boat, but it was all they could do to handle it, as the turtle pulled them around like mere children. They were finally compelled to give up the task of pulling the turtle into the boat, and were satisfied to tow it in with them on Thursday noon. \n When the turtle was finally landed in the shipping room of the San Diego Fish company, the big surprise came. \n Although the monster's back shell was covered with slime and barnacles, when these were scraped off and the shell fairly cleaned, numerous characters were plainly visible. It appeared that they had been either burned or cut deep into the shell years ago. \n After some study, the following inscription was deciphered: \n “Br. S. Sea Bride —Aug. 12, 1881—3S.—86 W."" was on the left side of the turtle's shell, looking toward the head, while these words were on the right side: \n ""If Found Please Notify— Thomas Fletcher—Brawley Road. Rivington, Eng.” \n It would appear from an Interpretation of the inscription that a sailor or officer of the British ship Sea Bride, caught this turtle in Pacific waters off the coast of South America, August 12, 1881, in latitude 3 South and longitude 86 west, branded or cut the characters indicated on the turtle's shell and threw it back into the ocean again. \n The turtle must, therefore, he at least 26 years old, and it is probable that it is much older, as it tipped the scales at 1,902 1-2 pounds—nearly a ton— while it measured 5 feet 2 inches over the shell from front to rear. \n It was wrinkled about the neck like a clown's collar and was a pathetic sight as it lay on the shipping room floor, helpless as a baby, its great flippers all torn to pieces by its own futile struggles in the fishing net. \n The turtle was immediately sold by evidently quite wary and wise to have escaped the wiles of man and its fellow monsters of the deep for over 26 years, but its feminine curiosity for the fishing net probably caused its capture an ddeath. \n The turtle was immediately sold by telegraphic order to a big fish market in Los Angeles and by this time is probably nothing but fine French soup and steaks on the fashionable tables of that city.”
+","2","""HUGE TURTLE CAPTURED IN NET BEARS MESSAGE""","1","green","inferred","inferred","1902","5'2""","not_reported","""this turtle was captured twice, this being the second time. The first was off the coast of South America 26 years prior. this also seems to be reporting the same turtle observation/capture as laherald_19070622, latimes_19070622, and sdut_19070621. This exact article can also be found in the sd union tribune archives at ""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-13910D6796D5FE01%402417748-13910B4FBB102280%402-13910B4FBB102280%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=3&pdate=1907-06-21""""","1907",7,"high",157.48,1,862.73,1900,"san_diego","san_diego","california","usa",32.8245525,-117.1089785
+"61","laherald_19070622","""Los Angeles Herald""",1907-06-22,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19070622.2.11&srpos=133&e=-------en--20-LAH-121--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""By Associated Press. \n SAN DIEGO, June 21.— One of the puzzles of the sea came to the surface yesterday when a gigantic sea turtle was captured by Julius Solissa, a fisherman in whose net it had become entangled. The monster weighs 1902 1/2 pounds, almost a ton, and its shell is five feet two inches from tip to tip. On Its back plainly is burned or carved this inscription: ""British ship Sea Bride, .August 12, 1881, 2 south, 86 wset. If found please notify Thomas Fletcher, Brawley road, Rivington, England."" \n It would appear from this that the turtle was captured twenty-six years ago in the South Pacific, and that it was released after the lettering had been burned in its shell.""","2","""TURTLE FIRST CAUGHT IN SOUTH PACIFIC IS SAN DIEGO PUZZLE""","1","green","inferred","inferred","1902.5","5'2""","not_reported","""this appears to be the same turtle that is reported about in sdunion_19070621, latimes_19070622, and sdut_19070621; additionally, wset is a typo of west in the article itself""","1907",7,"high",157.48,1,862.96,1900,"san_diego","san_diego","california","usa",32.8245525,-117.1089785
+"62","laherald_19120828","""Los Angeles Herald""",1912-08-28,13,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19120828.2.111&srpos=134&e=-------en--20-LAH-121--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""The 200-pound shell of the largest turtle in the world, captured yesterday near San Diego after a terrific battle of seven hours on the sea, will serve as a bulletin board for the San Diego 1915 fair in the exhibit rooms of the Los Angeles chamber of commerce, if James A. Jasper, special representative of the southern city's chamber here, is successful in his efforts to secure the shell. \n The turtle measured nearly eight feet in length, six feet in width and was two feet thick. It weighed 2500 pounds. \n ""I am exerting every effort to secure the shell for the exhibit rooms of the chamber of commerce here,” said Mr. Jasper. “I propose to advertise the San Diego fair with a sketch of the exposition grounds on its shell.”""","2","""World’s Biggest Turtle Shell to Boost 1915 Fair""","1","leatherback","inferred","inferred","2500","8'","not_reported",NA,"1912",7,"high",243.84,1,1133.98,1910,"san_diego","san_diego","california","usa",32.8245525,-117.1089785
+"63","natcitysn_19660529","""National City Star-News""",1966-05-29,NA,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=NCSN19660529.2.27&srpos=1&e=-------en--20-NCSN-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""A National City man, Ludwick Egger, could hardly believe his eyes when a giant sea turtle surfaced near his boat while he was fishing in San Diego Bay this week. \n “I never heard of sea turtles around San Diego,” he said, “but there he was.” The turtle surfaced about 50 feet from the boat the first time he was spotted and later surfaced about 35 feet away, Egger said. \n Egger said he had a fishing companion in the boat, “and he can vouch for the turtle.” Egger, who lives with his wife at 43 L Ave., said he is taking a ribbing from friends who insist he saw the turtle because he was intoxicated. \n Egger said he saw the “big flippers” of the turtle “and I got so hungry. I dearly love turtle. Those big flippers make wonderful roasts.” \n Egger said he is concerned about the turtle causing a speedboat accident “My grandson has a speedboat, and if he hit that big fellow it would wipe out the boat” \n A member of the San Diego Zoo’s Reptile division staff said, “Although it is certainly not an everyday occurrence, sea turtles do get up this far north. I have heard of four or five instances where turtles were sighted in the bay.” \n The turtles weigh from 200 to 800 pounds and abound in the southern waters.""","2","""It's a Sea Turtle—In San Diego Bay?""","1","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""The article notes that there have been 4-5 other sightings in the recent past, and 'although it is certainly not an everyday occurance [to see sea turtles in san diego bay], sea turtles do get up this far north'. The location says San Diego bay and the article doesn't note the weight of this exact turtle, but says that generally the turtles are 200-800 lbs.""","1966",8,"high",NA,1,NA,1960,"san_diego","san_diego","california","usa",32.8245525,-117.1089785
+"64","latimes_19070622","""Los Angeles Times""",1907-06-22,11,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/159114704/590BBC08B3D34453PQ/86?accountid=14026""","“SEA MONSTER WEIGHING ALMOST TON IS CAPTURED \n On Its Shell Is Discovered Inscription Showing That the Animal Had Been Captured by British Sailor in Southern Seas Twenty-six Years Ago. [BY THE ASSOCIATED PRESS— P.M.] \n SAN DIEGO, June 21.—One of the puzzles of the sea came to the surface yesterday, when a gigantic turtle was captured by Julius Solissa, a fisherman in whose net it had become entangled. The monster weighs 1902½ pounds, or almost a ton, and its shell is five feet two inches from tip to tip. On its back, somewhat plainly, is burned or carved this inscription: \n “British ship Sea Bride, August 12, 1881. 8 south, 86 west. If found please notify Thomas Fletcher, Brawley Road, Rivington, England."" \n It would appear from this that the turtle was captured twenty-six years ago in the South Pacific and that he was released after the lettering had been burned in its shell.”","2","""GIANT TURTLE BEARS MESSAGE""","1","green","inferred","inferred","1902.5","5'2""","not_reported","""same observation (redundant) as laherald_19070622, sdut_19070621, sdunion_19070621. could possibly be a leatherback based on size""","1907",7,"high",157.48,1,862.96,1900,"san_diego","san_diego","california","usa",32.8245525,-117.1089785
+"65","sdut_19550927","""San Diego Union Tribune""",1955-09-27,NA,NA,"""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-13C1810E91B2E52E%402435378-13C13C52EC1C0E73%4014-13C13C52EC1C0E73%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=2&pdate=1955-09-27""","“[image included] Crowd inspects captured 750-pound turtle on freight elevator. Charles Sweeney, at left, and Billy Cromer, second from right, are among seven sailors who caught it. At right is Arvid Roper, U. S. Grant Hotel chef who gave it temporary home. \n LARGEST IN CAPTIVITY \n By JOE BROOKS \n A 750-pound turtle last night reached the comparative tranquility of a commercial aquarium near Palos Verdes after some harrowing experiences in the San Diego area. \n It is the largest turtle on exhibit anywhere. And if it remains alive, it will be the first Leatherback turtle that ever did so in captivity. \n If not, it'll make a mammoth pot of turtle soup. \n The turtle's bad luck started Sunday when it ran afoul of a boatload of seven U. S. sailors while loafing in a kelp bed off Rosarito Beach. \n TWO-HOUR STRUGGLE \n The sailors were imbued with that natural human instinct of catching anything big that one encounters. They lassoed the critter with the anchor line and after a two-hour struggle succeeded in getting it ashore and hoisting it into their car trunk. \n On the trip to San Diego the weight of the huge reptile broke the car springs. Then on arrival, no one could be found to take custody of the creature. The zoo couldn't. The city pound wouldn't. \n The sailors finally left the turtle in the basement of the U.S. Grant Hotel. Sam Hinton, curator of the aquarium at Scripps Institution of Oceanography, placed it [t]emporarily in the quarantine tank at Scripps. \n ""There were four of us and we nearly killed ourselves getting that turtle moved,"" Hinton said. \n TOOK 10 MEN \n Three men from Marineland Aquarium arrived in a pickup truck late yesterday afternoon to take the turtle there. \n Ten men lifted it out of the tank and placed it on its back on a mattress on the truck bed. \n A cargo net was used to truss it for the trip north. \n Leatherbacks are the largest species of turtle, Hinton said. But none has ever lived in captivity, ""although this one looks like it has a good chance,"" he said. ""It is in good shape."" \n ""We'll put it in the porpoise tank,"" said Frank Calanbrino, assistant collector at Marineland. ""People love to see those big lumbering things swimming around.""”","2","""Monster Turtle Survives Ordeal""","1","leatherback","reported","known","750","not_reported","""loafing in kelp bed""","""article gives accompanying image""","1955",10,"high",NA,1,340.19,1950,"rosarito_beach","playas_de_rosarito","baja_california","mexico",32.3594973,-117.0574025
+"66","latimes_19210511","""Los Angeles Times""",1921-05-11,6,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160939571/pageviewPDF/6F8781D478ED49A8PQ/1?accountid=14026""","“[LOCAL CORRESPONDENCE] \n REDONDO BEACH, May 10.— Hearing that Redondo Beach has something unusual in the way of a harbor, a big spotted turtle, a kind often seen off the coast of Mexico and the West Indies, made port here, Tuesday. A sixty-foot mackerel line in the hands of Ed Shortridge and Johnny Greenback, local fishermen, towed the turtle to shore. It weighs at least 1000 pounds, it being impossible for six men with timbers to lift it. Fishermen here say that they have never seen this species in these waters before. Those who are familiar with the kind- estimate the age of this .one as between 1500 and 2000 years. This specimen will be on exhibition here for several days.”","2","""LOTS OF SOUP.""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","1000","not_reported","not_reported","""the specific species is not reported, the article just claims it to be a 'big spotted turtle' and is rare for this area. this is perhaps a leatherback, not a green? especially given the size?""","1921",8,"high",NA,1,453.59,1920,"redondo_beach","los_angeles","california","usa",33.854634,-118.3771421
+"67","sdbee_18870920","""San Diego Daily Bee""",1887-09-20,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDB18870920.2.12&srpos=6&e=-------en--20-SDB-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Two car loads of San Diego county products left for St. Louis yesterday, consisting of fruits, vegetables, minerals and grain of as fine a quality as can be shown from any county in the State. Ensenada sent her quota from Baja California, among which was an immense living sea turtle, with which the Bay of Punta Banda abounds. \n At Fallbrook the cars will be reinforced with two living full-sized ostriches in full feather, grown without irrigation. While many of the fruits may not stand transportation, and arrive in as good condition as when sent, there will be sufficient to base a proper opinion on what this heretofore neglected country can do comparatively with others, whose boom commenced and died down to a legitimate condition long before ours was generally known to exist. \n Our Grand Army Corps, who have taken this portion of the St. Louis exhibit in charge, know our important responsibility as representative men. \n Mr. Wm. Lyons accompanies the procession, and what he lacks in exhibit can be made up with his, “literary specials’’ and placer gold samples, with which he is well provided. \n Several representatives of our local press will be in St. Louis, and many of our other citizens, all of which will have a tendency to give San Diego a satisfactory representation, and prove that such men as Lyons, Large, Hartzell, and others, were not propagated and brought to their present condition on climate alone; and after exhibiting themselves they can exhibit their rations. When the late Professor Louis Agagssiz remarked at a public assembly that that the climate of San Diego would produce a fortune to each of its then 2500 inhabitants there were but few that then appreciated the prophecy. Some 20,000 people have come to this city alone within fifteen months, and come to stay with us, while the county at large has been filled up in proportion. \n R. W. Dana in his phenomenal experiences with the Wide Drayers in 1834 and 5 reports San Diego as then the only port of safety on the “northwest coast.” characterizing about all others as the hell holds of California. \n Mrs. Helen Jackson in her “ramona,” written from personal observation here, pays a continual tribute to the climate and its productions, and speaks of a special suburb as “Oneonta,” or the place of rest. It’s a mottled question whether the “Oneonta” known to the trade and now sold in 25x140 lots has been proselyted to that purpose. Mr. Peck, of “Peck’s Sun,” even excels Mrs. Jackson in proving up the resting qualities of our climate. He relates that he came here to hunt and fish, but while enjoying a day’s rest previous to this hunting, he found resting was the very thing to do, and kept at it while here. In his last letter from Riverside to his paper he opens it with, “The old gentleman is still resting.” Mrs. Maria Burton, widow of General Burton, U.S.A., in her narration of the “Don and Squatter” from 1872 up to a recent date, confirms all previous statements as to climate that the radical element of emigration failed to interrupt. Our real estate agents of to-day keep up the refrain and in many instances seem to emulate the historic “Silas Megg” and “drop into poetry” without meaning to plagiarize. At the same time there is nothing abnormal in the condition of San Diego.""","1","""SAN DIEGO BEGINNING TO BOOM""","1","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""In the article it only mentions the sure existence of one sea turtle, however, it says that the Bay of Punta Banda 'abounds' with many sea turtles""","1887",7,"high",NA,1,NA,1880,"punta_banda","ensenada","baja_california","mexico",31.7196805,-116.662891
+"68","sbmp_19071030","""Morning Press""",1907-10-30,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP19071030.2.30&srpos=1&e=-------en--20-MP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","“SAN DIEGO, Oct. 29.—Word has just been received from Ensenada of the narrow escape from death of William J. Forbes, prominent in the affairs of Wall street, who is a guest of Walter Nordhoff, at his home at Puenta Banda. Mr. Forbes was fishing on the bay in a small boat accompanied by a boatman, when their line was taken by a sea turtle. The line held and the turtle towed the boat for some distance. The fishermen determined to land the turtle if possible, and in their efforts they overturned the boat. The turtle got away with the tackle and the fishermen were in the water for two hours before they were rescued and not one would have been able to stand it much longer.”","2","""TURTLE LEADS BOATMEN TO NARROW QUARTERS""","1","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Appears to be the same sdunion_19071029 and laherald_19071030. Species is unclear; either green or leatherback; location in article says puenta banda, but assumed to be punta banda""","1907",8,"high",NA,1,NA,1900,"punta_banda","ensenada","baja_california","mexico",31.7196805,-116.662891
+"69","slotelegram_19201229","""San Luis Obispo Daily Telegram""",1920-12-29,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SLODT19201229.2.28&srpos=28&e=-------en--20-SLODT-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""There was an enormous turtle in the bay at Port Harford yesterday.""","1","""TWENTY-ONE YEARS AGO""","1","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Though the year of publishing is 1920, this turtle was captured 21 years prior (1899). The species is unspecified and no actual size is given, so it is inferred to be green, however, given that it is said to be 'enormous', it could possibly be a leatherback""","1920",7,"high",NA,1,NA,1920,"port_harford_bay","san_luis_obispo","california","usa",35.1705927,-120.755477
+"70","pvnews_19470828","""Palos Verdes Peninsula News""",1947-08-28,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=PVPN19470828.2.37&srpos=86&e=-------en--20-PVPN-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks+NOT+velvet-------""","""J. Adams \n Dr. William Lloyd, Director of the Cabrillo Reach Marine Museum, addressed members of the Friday Morning Study Club when they were conducted through the museum following their potluck picnic at the beach. Dr. Lloyd called attention to the fact that all wild creatures have the same problems as humans, that is, home, food and self-protection and their body is so developed to meet these problems. Of particular interest was the pelican, which has a seven or eight foot wing expansion. The wide wing spread compared to the body length enables the pelican to fly very slowly in its search for fish in the water. The bill is distinctive in its narrow top and scoop-net lower hill which spreads to hold a large quantity of fish. \n The huge leatherback turtle on display was harpooned off Point Vincente and weighed 1100 pounds and was estimated to have been between 300 and 1000 years old. \n During luncheon announcement was made by Mrs. Elmer Higgs, Club President, of the Overseas Relief Drive sponsored by church and civic groups as well as the Philharmonics and Artists concert series. Club members were invited to attend the Community Sing at Anderson Memorial 7:30 p. m., September 2nd. when they would be special guests. \n The first regular program of the year was announced for October 10th and memberships are now being received by Mrs. John Langer, Membership Chairman, 702 Ellery Drive. The luncheon was arranged by Mrs. Violette Boone, hospitality chairman. Also introduced were Mrs. W. H. Leisk, the club’s first president; Mrs. Fred Wright, charter member; and Mrs. L. R. Tarr, honorary life member.""","2","""Study Club Holds Potluck Picnic at Cabrillo Beach""","1","leatherback","reported","known","1100","not_reported","not_reported","""The location can be labeled more broadly as la_county""","1947",9,"high",NA,1,498.95,1940,"point_vincente","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7418706,-118.4107343
+"71","sppilot_19381102","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1938-11-02,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19381102.2.12&srpos=14&e=-------en--20-SPNP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""The 1100-pound giant sea turtle caught offshore early in September by Capt. Abe Bolton and crew of the bait boat Izu will be preserved for Cabrillo beach marine museum. \n Bolton and his men, according to Playground Director Ernest Ehrke, have given the specimen over to the playground department. A Wilmington cold storage firm, which kept the huge amphibian frozen solid for nearly six weeks, co-operated by writing off all charges, Ehrke said. It was to be moved by truck today. \n The turtle will be prepared for exhibit by Dr. William Lloyd, museum curator. Authorities are puzzled over the appearance of this species in California waters, declaring it is an equatorial turtle.""","2","""Place 1100-Pound Sea Turtle In Cabrillo Beach Museum""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","inferred","1100","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be the same observation and turtle as sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390830, sppilot_19390310, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390529. Therefore, although no exact location is given, simply 'offshore', as well as no reported species, the information is inferred from those articles.""","1938",7,"high",NA,1,498.95,1930,"point_vincente","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7418706,-118.4107343
+"72","sppilot_19380912","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1938-09-12,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19380912.2.32&srpos=16&e=-------en--20-SPNP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Soup meat and museum curio. That probably will be the fate of a giant 1100-pound turtle, a record catch, brought in over the weekend by Capt. Abe Bolton and his crew of the bait boat Izu. \n Now resting on ice at the Wilmington Ice company, the behemoth of the turtle family, he measures more than three feet across and seven feet long, puzzled the skipper and the ice company today as to what will become of it. Some of the meat is usable but to stuff the giant turtle would cost money and neither Ernest Ehrke of the Cabrillo beach marine museum nor Capt. G. Allan Hancock, leading marine curiosity seeker, appeared interested. \n Meanwhile, Jimmie Comer and other members of the bait boat crew were checking records which show, so far, that theirs is the largest turtle ever caught. \n The 1100-pound monster was surface swimming when first sighted near Point Vicente but led the boat on an hour chase before Bill Tagami hurled a harpoon through his leather-covered back. The turtle’s appearance so far north puzzled authorities who say this species is native to equatorial waters. Sharing credit for the catch with Capt. Bolton, Tagami' and Comer are Frank Bottles, Fred Thomas and I. Furukawa, all of San Pedro.""","2","""San Pedrans’ 1100-lb. Turtle Believed Largest Ever Caught""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","1100","7'","""surface swimming""","""This appears to be reporting the same observation and event as sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390830, sppilot_19390310, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390529, making this clipping redundant, but with a bit more detail. The size is reported to be 3 feet across, and the authors note that it is unusual at this time to see this species (possible leatherback) in these waters. The article is on page 1 and continued on page 2.""","1938",9,"high",213.36,1,498.95,1930,"point_vincente","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7418706,-118.4107343
+"73","sppilot_19470818","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1947-08-18,6,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19470818.2.104&srpos=89&e=-------en--20-SPNP-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""The leatherback turtle on display was harpooned off Point Vincente and weighed 1100 pounds, Dr. Lloyd said. It is estimated to have been between 300 and 1000 years old at the time of its capture. It is the largest known sea turtle to have been captured. \n Dr. Lloyd, in showing a Gila Monster, told the group that it is the only poisonous lizard in this country, and stated that the lizards found in this area are beneficial to gardens for they kill harmful insects. \n The museum is operated by the Los Angeles Recreation and Parks department, and during the past year has been visited by 300,000 persons. Dr. Lloyd has addressed 253 groups in that time. The museum is distinctive in that it is the only one in the country which exhibits marine life exclusively.""","2","""Aged Turtle""","1","leatherback","reported","known","1100","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be reporting the same turtle as sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19390830, sppilot_19390310, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390529 based on size, location, species, and mention that it was displayed in a museum with dr. Lloyd for years. Though the time of this article's publishing is nearly a decade later, all the details connect to the other articles, though perhaps the connection is not certain as for the others. The article states the turtle is estimated to be between 300 and 1000 years old. Note that the timing of capture is not accurate to when this article specifically was published""","1947",9,"high",NA,1,498.95,1940,"point_vincente","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7418706,-118.4107343
+"74","sppilot_19390830","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1939-08-30,NA,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19390830.2.246&srpos=56&e=-------en--20-SPNP-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Thousands of Persons Fascinated by Display of Marine Oddities \n More than 150,000 persons have visited the Marine Museum at Cabrillo beach to marvel at the varied specimens of ocean life on display. \n In San Francisco today visitors from all sections of the world gape at a replica of a monster turtle --- a 1,100-pound giant of the sea --- the original of which was a gift to the local museum. \n It is said to be the largest turtle ever caught and brought from tropical waters to these shores. Thirty-five years ago a turtle weighing slightly less than 1,000 pounds was caught and brought here. This was the only other time a turtle, even approaching the size of the recent catch, was landed in this section. \n Today the replica of the monster not only is one of the features of the exposition’s aquatic show, but also the center of interest at the local museum, where a duplicate plaster cast appears. \n The Cabrillo beach museum provides a place where persons interested in the nature lore of the Pacific coast may learn about the flora and fauna of the sea and shore and also bring in their own private collections of birds, fish, shells, and other specimens for identification. \n From this beginning the museum has grown until it now contains hundreds of fascinating specimens of the under sea and shore denizens of the Pacific. \n Many strange varieties of fish found in waters off the coast of California, Mexico and tropical islands to the southward are on display, as are preserved iguanas, Galapagos turtles, birds and rare creatures of many varieties seldom seen by the casual visitor to the sea shore. \n The giant sea crabs, many varieties of shell fish, specimens of plant life, snakes, and a great variety of other creatures are also displayed, many of them in settings depicting their natural habitat. \n Growth of the museum has been assisted through donations to it by various expeditions to Mexico, Central and South America, including those of Captain G. Allen Hancock, well known sportsman-explorer. The museum is under the direction of Dr. William L. Lloyd, former University of Pennsylvania naturalist. In addition to serving as curator of the institution, Dr. Lloyd conducts many nature study hobby groups for both children and adults at the municipal playgrounds of Hollywood and Los Angeles.""","2","""Cabrillo Museum Attracts Visitors""","1","leatherback","inferred","inferred","1100","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be reporting the same turtle as sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390310, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390529 (redundant) based on size, location, species, and mention that it was displayed in a museum with dr. Lloyd for years. Though the time of this article's publishing is nearly a decade later, all the details connect to the other articles, though perhaps the connection is not certain as for the others. Note that the timing of capture is not accurate to when this article specifically was published. Additionally, while the location isn't specified, I have reported it as point_vincente by assuming it matches all the details of the other articles.""","1939",7,"high",NA,1,498.95,1930,"point_vincente","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7418706,-118.4107343
+"75","sajournal_19380912","""Santa Ana Journal""",1938-09-12,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SAJ19380912.2.63&e=-------en--20-SAJ-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""WILMINGTON, Calif. GP) An 1100 pound leatherback turtle, which the boys on the bait boat Izu say may be the world’s largest, was unloaded with a derrick today. The turtle, measuring 88 inches from head to tail, was captured Saturday and kept on ice until the boat’s arrival last night.""","1","""1100-lb. Turtle Believed Record""","1","leatherback","reported","inferred","1100","7'4""","not_reported","""This article appears to be reporting the same turtle that is mentioned many times in the San Pedro News pilot, in sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390830, sppilot_19390310, and sppilot_19390529. This connection is seen in the weight/size of the turtle, and the boat on which it was found. The location is inferred based on the connection to the other reports.""","1938",7,"high",223.52,1,498.95,1930,"point_vincente","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7418706,-118.4107343
+"76","latimes_19050912","""Los Angeles Times""",1905-09-12,7,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164411437/17A6E603F61A4B73PQ/401?accountid=14026""","“Yesterday Smith Warren, A. J. Werner and Misses Alice and Grace E. Marvin of Los Angeles chartered the launch Twilight, Capt. W. H. Graves and while trolling off Point Vincent sighted a monster turtle, which Graves says was nine feet across, with a head like a barrel. The Twilight ran alongside and Graves drove a harpoon into it, but the monster did not linger and disappeared with the harpoon which had a red handle. The party spent the remainder of the afternoon chasing that red handle all over the channel, but failed in their attempt to lasso it. Graves had a Winchester on board but no cartridges. Fishing parties now have an added attraction presented them of chasing a red handle stick whenever they see it, with the knowledge that a turtle weighing over a ton is anchored on the other end of it.”","2","""RED STICK!—TURTLE!""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","known","2000","9'","not_reported","""the size says 'over a ton', so it is very general estimate, nevertheless is listed as 2000 pounds here. Species uncertain, but given size it could be a leatherback. It also says it had a head like a barrel, perhaps implying a loggerhead?""","1905",8,"high",274.32,1,907.18,1900,"point_vincent","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7418706,-118.4107343
+"77","sdut_19081021","""San Diego Union Tribune""",1908-10-21,7,NA,"""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-1394A13623FCDE56%402418236-13935C9A723643E1%406-13935C9A723643E1%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=8&pdate=1908-10-21""","“Percy Woods and the men who assisted him to land mammoth turtle caught in kelp beds off the harbor, showing size of crustacean as it lay on the beach. \n [image included] \n Battle Four Hours in Attempt To Land Huge Crustacean Weighing Half Ton \n After a four hour battle, lasting from 1 o’clock yesterday afternoon until 5. Percy Woods, a fisherman in the employ of the San Diego fish market. Landed a huge tortoise, weighing half a ton, perhaps the largest ever seen on this coast. When first sighted by Woods, who was lobster fishing off Point Loma, the monster crustacean was entangled in the kelp just outside the harbor and was fighting furiously for its liberty. Every movement entangled it more seriously in the treacherous kelp and had relief not so opportunely offered. it would undoubtedly have succumbed. \n With great effort, the turtle fighting every second, Woods succeeded in passing a rope around its body just behind the front flippers, and then cut loose the kelp twined about the turtle’s head and body. Starting the engine of his boat he then proceeded to tow the captive into port. The turtle resisted, but because of its inability to exert much strength in a backward direction, it was unable to stop the forward motion or the small gasoline schooner. Had Woods fastened the line behind the rear flippers, and attempted to tow the turtle into port backwards, it would easily have been able to turn the tables, probably carrying the fishing schooner to sea. \n As it was, it took Woods four long hours to reach the wharf of the San Diego Fish market, at the foot of D street, with his prize. Here, however, he met with further trouble. Transferring the rope to a small skiff, he thoughtlessly began to row ashore, thinking to tow the monster behind him. Finding that it was to be again put into motion, the turtle resisted and turned around, making for deep water. The skiff was dragged helplessly along, Woods’ efforts to the contrary being in vain. Finally fishermen on the wharf, sizing up the state of affairs, threw Woods a line, making one end fast to the wharf. \n Fights to Last Ditch \n Six or eight men working at the market fastened themselves to the rope, and their combined effort? were necessary to haul the skiff with the struggling turtle to the shore. Here the turtle was dragged out on the beach and walked around to the front of the market. Half a dozen men holding the rope were unable to retard its progress when it desired to continue on its way. the only means of controlling its action being to turn the head and keep the monster walking in a circle. \n The turtle was finally brought to the interior of the market where, before a crowd of curious onlokers, the rope was fastened to a workbench by C. E. Osborn, the manager of the fish market. The turtle, however, was not satisfied with its position, and promptly began dragging bench and all away from the wall to the opposite side of the big room. The rope was rapidly untied and hitched around a post supporting the roof. When this, too, was torn from its position, one of the men got busy and bored two holes in the floor, one on each side of a stout joist. The rope was then passed around this and the monster was finally secured. \n Manager Osborn is undecided what disposition to make of the big reptile, and in the meantime it is occupying the spot light on the floor of the fish market. The turtle measures eight feet in length over all, is forty-two inches across the back, the surface of the upper shell being twenty inches from the ground. It is thought to be of the species known as the loggerhead (capuana carcita), in rare instances seen in this section of the Pacific and easily the largest ever captured on the Southern California coast. It weighs about 1000 pounds. \n Shell Peculiarly Marked \n The shell of the tortoise is a peculiar one, being marked with six rows of protuberations. One of these lines each side of the shell, the others occurring in regular intervals to the center of the shell and running lengthwise, speculation as to the age of the monster is rife. Tortoise have been known to live 100 years or more. and it is believed that the species to which the captured one belongs retains its hold on life for a much longer period. \n One of the rear flaps has been completely cut off, supposedly bitten by a shark. The other rear flap is several feet in length, while the front flaps are fully three feet long. \n The shell of the tortoise is considerably marked and scratched, some apparently of recent date, while others may have been inflicted many years ago.”","2","""FISHERMEN CAPTURE MONSTER TORTOISE""","1","loggerhead","reported","known","1000","8'","""entangled in kelp""","""reported as a loggerhead, however, picture is included and could look like a leatherback as well""","1908",10,"high",243.84,1,453.59,1900,"point_loma","san_diego","california","usa",32.7123811,-117.2160231
+"78","stinson_sdherald_18870603","""San Diego Herald""",1887-06-03,NA,"53","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Mr. W.F. Cabral of Roseville [an early whaling and fishing community of San Diego Bay] caught a turtle weighing 1,200 pounds while fishing off the kelpbeds beyond Point Loma [entrance to Bay] on 1 June 1887. It was caught on an ordinary-sized fishing hook and in endeavoring to pull the turtle into the boat the fisherman was almost drawn overboard. The monster fish was exhibited for several days at Jorre's Wharf before going the way of all sea turtles—toward the tureen.""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","1200","not_reported","""off of kelp beds""",NA,"1887",9,"high",NA,1,544.31,1880,"point_loma","san_diego","california","usa",32.7123811,-117.2160231
+"79","sbmp_18890801","""Morning Press""",1889-08-01,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP18890801.2.41&srpos=6&e=-------en--20-MP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""S. F. Chronicle. \n A turtle nine feet long, four feet broad, and weighing 2000 pounds, was captured yesterday off Pigeon Point by Antonio Betling. He pursued it from sunrise till noon, and attempted to land it with one boat, which was nearly swamped. \n It finally required two boats and the assistance of eight fishermen to take the prisoner to the wharf. \n Betling and other fishermen say this is the largest turtle ever caught on the Pacific coast. \n The monster shell back was seen by many people yesterday, all of whom were struck with wonder at its prodigious size.""","2","""An Overgrown Turtle""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","2000","9'","not_reported","""Possible leatherback based on the size. Further descriptions of size include four feet wide""","1889",8,"high",274.32,1,907.18,1880,"pigeon_point","san_mateo","california","usa",37.1829202,-122.3928022
+"80","sppilot_19360813","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1936-08-13,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19360813.2.4&srpos=18&e=-------en--20-SPNP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""A giant leatherback turtle, weighing 475 pounds, was brought into San Pedro this morning by the tuna boat Garfield, commanded by Capt. Jerry Acalin. It was caught in tuna nets during the night while the Garfield was fishing off Oceanside. \n Brought into the Independent Fish company’s market at the municipal fish wharf, the turtle was kept alive pending delivery to some aquarium or zoo. It remained very active with its head and flippers when placed on the wet cement floor of the market. Captain Acalin said the leatherback caused considerable damage to his nets. \n Old-time fishermen declare the turtle to be an inhabitant of tropical waters. Its hard shell back is covered with heavy black skin, on which are lodged a number of small white barnacles.""","2","""Giant Turtle Brought Here""","1","leatherback","reported","known","475","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be the same observation as sppilot_19360819. the article also interestingly mentions that it has a hard shell, which isn't exactly true of leatherbacks, but it also 'heavy black skin' so likely the description is of a leatherback still.""","1936",9,"high",NA,1,215.46,1930,"oceanside","san_diego","california","usa",33.2259756,-117.3175415
+"81","sppilot_19360819","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1936-08-19,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19360819.2.20&srpos=36&e=-------en--20-SPNP-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Three miles north of the isthmus of Santa Catalina Island the crew of the San Pedro fishing boat Conte Di Savoia found this giant manta or sea bat swimming lazily along the surface of the sea. Capt. Tony Russo sunk a harpoon and for five hours the big fish fought. He took out 150 fathoms of strong line and sounded many times with five barrel buoys. The manta, when finally hoisted aboard, was found to weigh nearly a ton and to have a spread of 14 feet from the tips of its fins. Shown with the tropical fish in this picture are three members of the Conte Di Savoia’s crew, Joe Lucido, James Lucido and D. S Cline, engineer of the 88-foot fish boat. \n Below is the 475-pound leatherback turtle which Capt. Jerry Acalin and his crew of the San Pedro fishing boat Garfield brought to the Independent Fish Co. dock. He was caught in a tuna net off Oceanside. Shown in the picture with the turtle are Mrs. Dorothy Byrne and Carmine Mineghino. (News-Pilot Photos)""","2","""San Pedrans Land Manta, Turtle""","1","leatherback","reported","known","475","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be the same observation and turtle as sppilot_19360813""","1936",9,"high",NA,1,215.46,1930,"oceanside","san_diego","california","usa",33.2259756,-117.3175415
+"82","sajournal_19360814","""Santa Ana Journal""",1936-08-14,11,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SAJ19360814.2.177&srpos=17&e=-------en--20-SAJ-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""SAN PEDRO. The Fishing Boat Garfield brought to port today a 470-pound leatherback sea turtle captured last night off Oceanside when it became entangled in the boat's nets. \n Such turtles are rarely seen off California. It measured about five and one-half feet by three and one-half feet, which would be large even for tropical waters \n The turtle is being kept alive in the hopes an Aquarium may buy it.""","2","""Boat Makes Surprise Catch""","1","leatherback","reported","known","470","5'6""","""entangled in boat's nets""","""the article also mentions its width to be 3.5 (3'6"") feet.""","1936",10,"high",167.64,1,213.19,1930,"oceanside","san_diego","california","usa",33.2259756,-117.3175415
+"83","spdaily_19251224","""San Pedro Daily News""",1925-12-24,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPDN19251224.2.9&srpos=71&e=-------en--20-SPDN-61--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Total Receipts for November Totaled 13,830,379 Pounds, Report \n Total receipts of fish for the canneries and wholesale markets at San Pedro harbor for the month of November amounted to 13,830,579 pounds, according to the official report of the state fish and game commission, compiled under the direction of H. L. Williamson, in charge of the commercial fisheries department. \n Sardines led the list with 11,837,080 pounds. Yellowfin tuna came next with 781,403 pounds. Skipjack were third, with 495,038 pounds. The catch of barracuda totaled 129,923 pounds, while the mackerel catch was 173,222 pounds. \n The lobster catch was considerable larger than in former seasons. with a total catch of 63,248 pounds. Turtle, with 21 pounds; anchovies, 60 pounds; pompano, 8 pounds; flounders, 54 pounds, and clams, 75 pounds, formed the smallest in the last of 39 varieties of sea food caught.""","2","""SARDINES HEAD LIST OF FISH BROUGHT HERE""","cargo","green","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""The total number of turtles was not reported but the total weight of the cargo was just 21 pounds; additionally, no exact location was reported, but perhaps we can infer san pedro area, because that is where the newspaper is from""","1925",5,"medium",NA,114.9375,NA,1920,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
+"84","sppilot_19190327","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1919-03-27,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19190327.2.16&srpos=190&e=-------en--20-SPNP-181--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Three fishing vessels are now being fitted with proper equipment for catching Mexican turtles, and it is reported that several canning concerns are preparing to handle their catches. \n The turtles will be brought from Mexican waters, about a day and a half run from the harbor. Last year several tons of turtles were cared for, but the industry did not become established. \n Soup and canned turtle meat have heretofore come mostly from San Diego.""","1","""Going to Mexico for Turtles for Canning""","cargo","green","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""This article is very ambiguously written/worded, and location of capture is unclear whether it is san_diego or baja_california""","1919",4,"medium",NA,114.9375,NA,1910,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
+"85","stinson_sdherald_18720104","""San Diego Herald""",1872-01-04,NA,"51","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""In giving the shipments of merchandise by the steamer Orizaba on Tuesday we neglected to make a note of a small lot of green turtle which went up. The shipment in point of numbers was light, but in pounds it looks well. One of the last lot shipped, weighed 103 pounds. During the remainder of the winter, we are told, regular shipments of the green turtle will be made to San Francisco.""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","cargo","green","reported","not_reported","103","not_reported","not_reported","""no location is given, but likely can presume baja_california, base don context within the thesis.""","1872",6,"medium",NA,114.9375,46.72,1870,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
+"86","stinson_sdunion_19320825","""San Diego Union""",1932-08-25,NA,"57","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""On her second visit here with a similar cargo in the last few months, the vessel Mexico, Peter Koralish, skipper, was in port yesterday with a quantity of live turtles for the local market. \n Weighing anywhere from 85-150 pounds each, the turtles numbering about 60, will bring if sold here, about six or seven cents per pound 'on the hoof' according to reports along the waterfront. The heavy shell, it was said, causes a loss of between 60 and 70 percent in the net value of the turtles to the buyer. \n No market for the turtles had been found here yesterday, according to an official of one of the large fish companies, and in the event that the turtles cannot be disposed of either here or in San Pedro, the opinion was expressed that the cargo may be taken south and dried.""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","60","green","inferred","not_reported","150","not_reported","not_reported","""the location is not reported but based on context, it is likely in or around baja_california""","1932",6,"medium",NA,60,68.04,1930,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
+"87","sppilot_19251225","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1925-12-25,7,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19251225.2.71&srpos=106&e=-------en--20-SPNP-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""11,837,080 Pounds of Sardines Are Delivered \n Sardines formed more than 90 per cent of fish catches brought into San Pedro and Wilmington during the month of November, according to a report issued on Thursday by the Terminal Island office of the California Fish and Game Commission. The report was compiled by Deputy Commissioner H. L. Williamson. \n During the month a total of 13,830,579 pounds of fish of 37 varieties were delivered to local canneries and markets. Of this amount, 11,837,080 pounds were sardines. The canning season for this fish is not at its height. \n A feature of the monthly report was the fact that 63,248 pounds of lobsters were brought in. Also, that one turtle, weighing 21 pounds was sold at a local market. \n Following is the complete report of local fish deliveries for the month of November: Species Amount \n Abalone 802 \n Albacore 14,127 \n Anchovies 60 \n Barracuda 129,923 \n Black Cod 190 \n Bonita 13,020 \n Cabrilla 120 \n Clams 75 \n Flounders 54 \n Grunion 598 \n Halfmoon 4,185 \n Halibut 16,344 \n Kingfish 50,506 \n Lobster 63,248 \n Mackerel 173,222 \n Mixed Fish 5,552 \n Perch 6,807 \n Pompano 8 \n Rock Bass 2,179 \n Rock Fish 97,288 \n Sardines 11,837,080 \n Sculpin 1,979 \n Sea Bass, Black 1,310 \n Sea Bass, White 11,199 \n Shark 1,654 \n Sheepshead 3,897 \n Skate 2,688 \n Skipjack 495,038 \n Smelt 61,592 \n Sole 288 \n Swordfish 405 \n Tuna, Unclassified 1,724 \n Tuna, Blue Fin 7,100 \n Tuna, Yellow Fin 781,403 \n Turtle 21 \n Whitefish 15,699 \n Yellow Tail 29,194 \n Total pounds 13,830,579""","2","""FISH CATCHES IN NOVEMBER AT HIGH MARK""","1","green","inferred","not_reported","21","not_reported","not_reported","""The location is unclear because this is just a report of all of the fish that came in the month of November at the local port in San Pedro and Wilmington. There is not information about where these fish were captured.""","1925",6,"medium",NA,1,9.53,1920,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
+"88","cejournal_19530702","""Coronado Eagle and Journal""",1953-07-02,11,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=CJ19530702.2.114&srpos=47&e=--1846---1973--en--20-CJ-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Coronado’s fishing mayor, Lloyd Harmon, was one of the eight prize winners in San Diego Yellowtail Derby competition last week and one of the top individual performers among derby aspirants as well. \n Mayor Harmon landed in eighth place on the prize list with a 24 pound 4 ouncer, but his prize winning yellow proved the largest of 364 taken June 22. \n Although one day yellowtail catches soared as high as 501 during the week, a 1000-pound leatherback turtle run down and gaffed by a party of anglers aboard the Red Wing was the chief topic of conversation.""","2","""Harmon Takes Fish Derby Spot""","1","leatherback","reported","not_reported","1000","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be the same turtle as natcitysn_19530703 (redundant). Location is not specified, but is likely near Coronado/San Diego area, given that this is where the article takes place.""","1953",7,"high",NA,1,453.59,1950,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
+"89","natcitysn_19530703","""National City Star-News""",1953-07-03,NA,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=NCSN19530703.2.56&srpos=97&e=-------en--20-NCSN-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""A sudden flareup of dirty water slowed up yellowtail fishing at the Coronado Islands last weekend, but it appeared to be clearing rapidly early this week and good fishing for the July 4 weekend appeared to be in store. \n When the slow-down occurred at the Islands, the La Jolla and Point Loma kelp beds quickly took up the slack, and anglers had little difficulty fattening their sacks with barracuda, and calico bass, plus some white sea bass and yellows. \n Among National City anglers who switched to La Jolla and hit the jackpot were V. C. Daniel, who limited out on barracuda, and G. Tweed and L. Tweed. Charles Phillips stuck to the Coronado Islands through thick and thin and was rewarded with a 22 pound 5 ounce yellow. \n Before the dirty water moved in, anglers made daily catches of from 258 to 501 yellows, and veteran rod and reelers expected similar catches once conditions are back to normal. \n Most unusual catch of the week was the taking of a 1000 pound leatherback turtle by anglers aboard the Red Wing last Friday. First take of albacore, 18 of them, was made the same day by Ray Ward and Ben Burch, aboard the Channel Clipper, one hour south of the Coronado Islands.""","2","""'Dirty Water' Forces Anglers to Switch Areas""","1","leatherback","reported","not_reported","1000","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be reporting the same turtle as cejournal_19530702 (redundant). Location unspecified but potentially around the La Jolla/Coronado Islands area.""","1953",7,"high",NA,1,453.59,1950,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
+"90","stinson_sdunion_19020720","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1902-07-20,NA,"53","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Shipping Big Turtles. A 300 pound turtle was shipped to Los Angeles yesterday through Wells Fargo Express by the San Diego Fish Company. A number of these turtles have been sent out of San Diego during the past week or two.""","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","1","green","inferred","not_reported","300","not_reported","not_reported","""this article in particular refers to one turtle that was shipped, however it also mentions a few other turtles that were caught and shipped elsewhere in the same manner""","1902",5,"medium",NA,1,136.08,1900,"not_reported",NA,NA,NA,NA,NA
+"91","slotribune_18840509","""San Luis Obispo Tribune""",1884-05-09,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SLOTW18840509.2.18&srpos=9&e=-------en--20-SLOTW-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","“While at Monterey on Friday last we saw a monster sea turtle that had been caught that day in the bay by some fishermen. The turtle appeared to be fully seven feet in length from nose to end of tail and with four long flippers, like wings, giving it a breadth of about the same. The monster was estimated to weigh 500 or 600 pounds. The captors expressed the intention of selling it for exhibition in Woodwards Garden in San Francisco.”","1","""Monster Turtle""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","known","600","7'","not_reported","""Inferred to be a leatherback based on size. This may potentially be the same event/observation as sbmp_18840509 based on location and time""","1884",7,"high",213.36,1,272.16,1880,"monterey_bay","monterey","california","usa",36.7896928,-121.9442208
+"92","sbmp_18840509","""Morning Press""",1884-05-09,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP18840509.2.12&srpos=33&e=-------en--20-MP-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","“Friday while fishing in the bay on Monterey about five miles off shore, one of the boats of F. Nocefore & Co., saw a large turtle floating on the water. They succeeded in catching it with a gaff, and then securely tied it with ropes. Nine men tried to pull it into a boat, but were unable to move it. Three boats were required to tow it to Monterey. It is seven feet seven inches in length, four feet across the back, and has two fins three feet long. It is known as a humpback. —Santa Cruz Surf.”","2","""A Monster Turtle""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","not_reported","7'7""","""floating on the water""","""The article refers to the species as a humpback—they likely are referring to it as a leatherback based on size. This may be the same event/observation (redundant) as slotribune_18840509 based on location and time. It also further specifies the size to be 4 feet in width and has 3 foot fins""","1884",9,"high",231.14,1,NA,1880,"monterey_bay","monterey","california","usa",36.7896928,-121.9442208
