+"95","stinson_sdherald_18880712","""San Diego Herald""",1888-07-12,NA,"47","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""News Notes from National [City] and Coronado ... 'Many turtles in the Bay. Anglers report that many turtles are sighted daily from the wharf, but none have ever been killed or captured. The difficulty lies in the fact that the only way of catching these elusive reptiles is by shooting them, and the discharge of firearms from the wharf is prohibited.'""","1","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","not_reported","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""No precise number of turtles are reported to have been sighted, just 'many'. The exact location is not given, just says 'bay' but assumed to be mission/san diego bay""","1888",4,"medium",NA,NA,NA,1880,"mission_bay","san_diego","california","usa",32.777574,-117.229321
+"96","stinson_sdherald_18880712","""San Diego Herald""",1888-07-12,NA,"47","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""News Notes from National [City] and Coronado ... 'Many turtles in the Bay. Anglers report that many turtles are sighted daily from the wharf, but none have ever been killed or captured. The difficulty lies in the fact that the only way of catching these elusive reptiles is by shooting them, and the discharge of firearms from the wharf is prohibited.'""","1","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","not_reported","hawksbill","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""No precise number of turtles are reported to have been sighted, just 'many'. The exact location is not given, just says 'bay' but assumed to be mission/san diego bay. And same observation as row above but with a different species, because both are inferred to be present based on other articles""","1888",4,"medium",NA,NA,NA,1880,"mission_bay","san_diego","california","usa",32.777574,-117.229321
+"97","stinson_sdunion_18900617","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1890-06-17,NA,"47","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""At this time of the year there are numerous sea turtles in False [Mission] Bay, and during last week two men who make a business of catching the monsters were quite successful, clearing $98 for the week's work. The turtles were delivered in the city are worth four cents per pound at the restaurants.""","1","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","not_reported","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""sold for meat, but number not specified. Not enough to be consistent large cargo, but numerous""","1890",5,"medium",NA,NA,NA,1890,"mission_bay","san_diego","california","usa",32.777574,-117.229321
+"98","stinson_sdunion_18900711","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1890-07-11,NA,"47","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Some very large turtles were brought from False [Mission] Bay yesterday by the Chinamen. One weighed 250 pounds and was all two of them could carry swung by shoulder poles.""","1","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","not_reported","green","inferred","known","250","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1890",5,"medium",NA,NA,113.4,1890,"mission_bay","san_diego","california","usa",32.777574,-117.229321
+"99","sdunion_19160709","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1916-07-09,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19160709.2.4&srpos=4&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","“A turtle weighing 350 pounds, captured by Joe Mumford, will be on exhibition at Ocean Beach today, it was announced last night. \n Mumford, who is well known as a local fisherman, captured the turtle in Mission bay about noon yesterday. He thinks it is the largest turtle ever captured near San Diego. \n The big turtle was brought ashore by the combined efforts of several men. Mumford, who has caught many turtles, says his latest catch gave him more trouble in getting it ashore than all others combined.”","2","""350-Pound Turtle Caught at Beach""","1","green","inferred","known","350","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1916",8,"high",NA,1,158.76,1910,"mission_bay","san_diego","california","usa",32.777574,-117.229321
+"100","sdunion_19180616","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1918-06-16,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19180616.2.326&srpos=103&e=-------en--20-SDDU-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","""SPEARING deep sea fish as they swim into the bays of the coast line is one of the most fascinating branches of piscatorial sport. It requires an expert to hurl the harpoon into the bodies of the great fish as they fash by, many feet under water, but Paul of Ocean Beach is a real expert in this difficult art. Young Paul, poised on the bridge over the entrance to Mission bay, successfully harpooned a giant black bass or Jewfish, weighing 275 pounds. Young Paul and his prize are shown in the accompanying picture. \n At the lower left of the photograph is the shell of a giant sea turtle, also harpooned by Paul several days previous to his capture of the big fish. The turtle weighed almost 300 pounds and was caught by Paul with the aid of A. W. Brand of the Mission bath house, who also assisted in landing the giant fish.""","2","""Giant Bass Speared From Mission Bridge""","1","green","inferred","known","300","not_reported","not_reported","""the location is not explicitly stated for the turtle, but the bass was caught in mission bay a few days later, by the same man, so assumed to be the same area (or nearby)""","1918",8,"high",NA,1,136.08,1910,"mission_bay","san_diego","california","usa",32.777574,-117.229321
+"101","sdut_19210601","""San Diego Union Tribune""",1921-06-01,5,NA,"""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-13A0D7C8173632F3%402422842-13A0CA53226962EA%404-13A0CA53226962EA%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=3&pdate=1921-06-01""","“[image included] A 243-pound turtle was posed for his picture yesterday afternoon after he had been speared from Mission Beach bridge by B. M. Hill. It is estimated that the monster denizen of the sea was in his prime about the time the Declaration of Independence was signed. Standing behind the turtle to the left is W. W. Roberts, superintendent of the Neptune Sea Food company, who bought the catch from Hill (right). The spear which Hill sunk into the turtle’s neck is shown. \n Catch Weighed 243 Pounds; Its Final Form Will Be Turtle Soup on Epicures’ Table \n Just 243 pounds of potential turtle soup was enjoying; a personally-conducted sightseeing tour of Mission bay yesterday afternoon when he met with bad luck in five prongs. Today the huge turtle, one of the biggest caught near San Diego in many years, is residing temporarily in a private tank owned by the Neptune Sea Food company. He doesn’t know it, but in a short time he will be in many little cans and will be labeled as ""Sapphire Green Turtle soup stock.” \n Whether the denizen of the ocean was attracted to San Diego by reports of the far-famed climate here, or whether this is only one of several ports he intended to make on an extensive vacation trip, is a matter for speculation. At any rate he was probably far from home yesterday afternoon when B. M. Hill’s spear sank itself in his neck under Mission beach bridge. The turtle lives—or most of his kind do—far down the coast of lower California at a place known as Turtle Bay. \n The unusual visitor in San Diego yesterday measured four feet from the tip of his nose to the end of his shell. W. W. Roberts, superintendent of the Neptune company, estimated that the turtle was between 300 and 400 years old. Two men standing on top of his shell apparently caused him no discomfort. \n Hill, who lives at 85 Third street, was on the Mission beach bridge spearing for halibut and shark yesterday afternoon, when he saw the monster turtle swimming under the bridge. ”Just to see what the turtle would do.” Hill hurled his spear. His aim was perfect, as the prongs dug into the reptile’s soft neck. If the spear had hit the shell it would have glanced off, blunted, Hill believes. \n It was manifestly impossible to lift the huge creature over the rail on the bridge, so Hill had to tow it to the beach, which was no easy task. All four stout flappers of the turtle were brought into play to oppose Hill, and it resolved into a fight of muscle and wit. Hill won, after a half hour of struggling. The turtle was dragged up on the beach and turned over so he couldn’t crawl away, then lifted into Hill's machine, in which it was carried to the plant of the Neptune company, at the foot of Juniper street. \n Turtles of the size of the one caught yesterday are rarely found in San Diego waters, according to those who follow waterfront occurrences. \n When a Union photographer called on the turtle to take his picture yesterday the prisoner was still in Hill's automobile, and had to be removed from the machine and deposited on a truck to be placed in the sunshine. The turtle took a lively interest in the proceedings and appeared not to thoroughly approve of them. He waved his flappers around menacingly as he was being dragged from the automobile. No sooner was he safely landed on the truck than he tried to scramble off. When deposited on the scales to be weighed, the turtle persisted in resting two or three flappers and part of his weight on the floor, with utter disregard for the kind of “story” he would make for the papers. Finally he was turned over on his back. \n Late last night the turtle was resting easier in a small swimming pool of his own filled with salt water. There he will await the arrival of a French chef from Los Angeles, who will make soup out of him. Even the shell probably will be used, as it contains gelatine which will thicken the soup stock.”","2","""Wandering Chelonian Fails to Find Sanctuary In Waters of Mission Bay, and Falls Victim of Local Fisherman's Unerring Aim With Spear""","1","loggerhead","inferred","known","243","4'","""swimming under the mission bridge at the surface""","""article gives image to accompany and says this is the biggest turtle caught near san diego in recent years--gives context for the rarity/frequency and has led us to identify it as a loggerhead. Article mentions that it was put on a scale so the weight is likely accurate""","1921",9,"high",121.92,1,110.22,1920,"mission_bay","san_diego","california","usa",32.777574,-117.229321
+"102","latimes_19200902","""Los Angeles Times""",1920-09-02,1,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160850108/fulltextPDF/FE617DBE9A774635PQ/1?accountid=14026""","“Father and Son Testify They Were Ill-Fed on Trip. \n Get Judgment Against Owner of Coasting Vessel. \n Dollar-a-month Contract is Branded as ”Doctored.” \n The brine of the Pacific, beans and boiled turtle meat, the last named procured in the hot sands of the Magdalena Islands, formed the principal ingredients in the dish set before U. S. Commissioner Long yesterday, in the trial of the admiralty case involving the claims of Albert G. Stebernaler and his son. A. G., Jr., against A. H. Moore, owner and master of the gas schooner Newark. \n Slebernaler, Sr, sued for $318.50. said to be due as wages as second cook on the vessel, and the commissioner gave him a judgment for $198 50. Theeboy asked for $326 50, and he was awarded $206. The commissioner figured that the defendant Moore should be allowed two months to make the trip from the Magdalena Islands to Acapulco, Mex., and back to San Diego, and that he should pay the plaintiffs for the balance of the time, the voyage requiring from April to November, 1919. \n Slebernaler and the boy went to the Magdalena Islands to make assays for an amalgam corporation. Involving the deposits of manganese. It was expected that the Newark should call for them and bring them back to the United States, as the vessel had been utilized to carry provisions to the islands. But the vessel was delayed. \n When the two got ready to come back to this country they found themselves without funds and the testimony showed that they went before the American Consul at Manzaniello. Mex., Harry L Walsh, and signed ship’s papers binding them to serve on the Newark, that later on touched at the island, until it returned to San Diego or Los Angeles Harbor. The Siebernalers found it necessary to work their way back \n SUM PUT IN LATER. \n The ship’s papers show that the two men, aged 46 and 20 years, respectively, agreed to work on the boat for $1 a month, but both witnesses said that figure had been inserted after they had signed the agreement in the office of the Consul That was where the argument started. \n In the bill of complaint the elder Slebernaler sued for 165 days’ pay at $2 a day, as second cook on the Newark and the boy asked for the same rates as engineer for the same period. \n The father gave his testimony some months ago, and yesterday the son was on the stand. He said that both had been compelled to work hard, and that the most of the time they were compelled to subsist on beans and turtle meat, but the witness admitted that Capt. Moore ate at the same table and had the same food. Both testified that they were ill-fed, were compelled to go without shoes, and the son said that he suffered from sore feet for a long time after the Newark reached her home port at San Diego. \n Young Slebernaler stated that it was one of his duties to get up at 3 o’clock in the morning and go to the Mexican villages to get milk for Capt. Moore, and in addition he had to do the ordinary duties of a seaman, wash down the deck, etc He testified that Capt. Moore told him that his services were very satisfactory. Both the father and son thought a dollar a month was not enough for such work. But both denied that they had agreed to work for that amount, although the ship’s papers showed their signature to the agreement calling for the payment of the sum. \n The judgment rendered by the commissioner will act as a libel on the Newark”","2","""OBJECT TO DIET OF TURTLE MEAT""","not_reported","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""magdalena_islands = magdalena_bay? This article is about these people eating turtle meat captured from the area so the exact number and location is unclear""","1920",5,"medium",NA,NA,NA,1920,"magdalena_islands","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
+"103","vfp_18910623","""Ventura Free Press""",1891-06-23,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MFP18910623.2.11&srpos=5&e=-------en--20-MFP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""The crop of monster turtles at Magdalena Bay, Lower California has induced a company to experiment with canning the extract for exportation. The first lot has gone to England through La Paz. The Lower Californian of Ensenada reports also that fishing for hair seals is a growing industry at the head of the Gulf of California, miners buying the output at 50 cents a gallon.""","1","""Canned Turtle.""","cargo","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""This article does not explicitly name the capture of turtles at a certain time/date, but does mention that there is a 'crop' of turtles in Magdalena bay that can be used for canning.""","1891",6,"medium",NA,114.9375,NA,1890,"magdalena_bay","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
+"104","stinson_sdunion_18910615","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1891-06-15,NA,"52","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Extract of Turtle. A New Use for the Monsters of Magdalena-Ensenada. The crop of monster turtles in Magdalena Bay, Lower California, has induced a company to experiment with canning the extract for exportation. The first lot has gone to England through La Paz. The Lower Californian of Ensenada reports also that fishing for hair seals is a growing industry at the head of the Gulf of California, miners buying the output at 50 cents a gallon.""","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","cargo","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1891",6,"medium",NA,114.9375,NA,1890,"magdalena_bay","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
+"105","sdunion_19170301","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1917-03-01,14,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19170301.2.154&srpos=11&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""Seventeen Huge Tortoise Arrive Aboard Auxiliary Schooner from Magdalena \n San Diegans who have a keen appetite for turtle steaks and turtle soup will have an opportunity of obtaining large portions of these dishes at their favorite hotels and cafes today. Seventeen huge Magdalena bay turtles, some of them weighing close to half a ton, arrived from the lower coast yesterday on the auxiliary schooner Vaquero. While not so large as the famous Galapagos Island turtle the Magdalena species are said to be much more tender than the turtles which make their home on the islands off the coast of Panama. \n In addition to the turtles, the Vaquero brought 325 tons of magnesite ore consigned to the International Magnesite Company of Chula Vista. The Vaquero Is expected to square away for Magdalena bay for another magnesite and turtle cargo tomorrow. \n Bound for the Elida and Natividad islands to load guano the auxiliary schooner Newark sailed at 8 o'clock last night.""","2","""Turtle Steaks To Feature City Dinner Menu""","17","green","inferred","known","1000","not_reported","not_reported","""the weight is noted as what only some of them weighed, as it claimed some to be as much as half a ton"" ","1917",8,"high",NA,17,453.59,1910,"magdalena_bay","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
+"106","latimes_19151221","""Los Angeles Times""",1915-12-21,1,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160255369/2796BCF9F7524B0FPQ/47?accountid=14026""","“Thirteen Green Sea Monsters Here to Appears as Soup and Steaks on Tables of Christmas Gourmets. \n One of the oddest freight consignments ever unloaded at the local harbor was taken from the Southwestern Steamship Company’s Mexican freighter, the San Pedro, yesterday. It consisted of thirteen green sea turtles, weighing about 225 pounds each, which were purchased from Magdalena Bay fishermen by an agent of the Haniman Fish Company. The turtles are alive and will be delivered today to local cafe and hotel men They required great care en route from Mexico and were given daily salt water baths aboard the San Pedro. Each turtle was made as comfortable as possible in an individual roomy crate and occasioned considerable comment when unloaded from an auto truck at their destination, No 129 South Main street. The consignment means turtle steaks and turtle soup for Christmas diners at several Los Angeles cafes and grills.”","1","""TURKEY OR TURTLE?""","13","green","reported","known","225","not_reported","not_reported","""weight given is the reported average""","1915",8,"high",NA,13,102.06,1910,"magdalena_bay","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
+"107","laherald_19010627","""Los Angeles Herald""",1901-06-27,9,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19010627.2.206&srpos=166&e=-------en--20-LAH-161--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""From Magdalena bay, off the coast of Lower California, have been sent to Los Angeles three of the largest green sea turtles that have been seen here during the present season. \n These salt water monsters, consigned to the Zaiser Produce company, were eagerly taken by the leading restaurants, attracting during last night large crowds of spectators where exhibited. \n The largest turtle weighed 295 pounds, the next 276 pounds, and the smallest 110 pounds. \n Lovers of the real article of turtle soup will be regaled in the next few days, also for the next few weeks, as the monsters will be sliced and placed in refrigeration. \n The shell-backs, that will cover the half of a billiard table, will be polished and mounted. The owner of the largest of these epicurean morsels says he will devise a turtle table and use the mottled back for ornamentation.""","1","""TURTLES FROM MAGDALENA BAY""","3","green","reported","known","295","not_reported","not_reported","""The largest weight of the three turtles is the one that is reported, however, the other two turtles also had reported weights of 276 and 110 pounds""","1901",8,"high",NA,3,133.81,1900,"magdalena_bay","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
+"108","latimes_19151225","""Los Angeles Times""",1915-12-25,1,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160306413/fulltextPDF/6AC2E570F15D473APQ/1?accountid=14026""","“Sea Captain Disposes of His Duty-free Turtles, which Makes them Immediately Liable to Duty. \n Capt. E. A. Blair of the gasoline launch Freda has been cited to appear at the office of the collector of customs in the Federal Building, and answer the charge of bringing two turtles, weighing sixty pounds each, into the district without the payment of duty. \n About a week ago the Freda put in at San Diego, and Capt. Blair reported to the customs officials that he had two large turtles aboard that he had caught at Magdalena Bay. They were billed as “sea stores” which are not liable to duty. \n Later on, he steamed up to Long Beach, where he sold the turtles to the chef of the Virginia Hotel for $5.60 That act took the testudinate reptiles out of the “sea-store” class, and it will cost Capt. Blair, it is believed about $8 to settle with the government. \n Under the law, almost any sort of goods or chattels can be labeled “sea-stores” and entered free, but they must be used by the crew and not sold.”","2","""UNPROFITABLE SALE.""","2","green","inferred","known","60","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1915",8,"high",NA,2,27.22,1910,"magdalena_bay","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
+"109","sppilot_19160830","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1916-08-30,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19160830.2.7&srpos=28&e=-------en--20-SPNP-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Capt. Gust Johnson of the power schooner Vaquero which arrived this morning from Magdalena Bay brought a big hundred pound turtle which he has given to the Elks for a big feed next Monday night. The turtle is being well cared for and will be in prime condition for the feed. \n Tonight the Elks will give another of their delightful at home parties for members and their wives and sweethearts. There will be dancing, cards and light refreshments. Good music has been provided for the dance and a big attendance is expected.""","2","""TURTLE FEED FOR THE ELKS""","1","green","inferred","known","100","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1916",8,"high",NA,1,45.36,1910,"magdalena_bay","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
+"110","sdut_19370801","""San Diego Union Tribune""",1937-08-01,8,NA,"""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-13BC7F05023FB3A7%402428747-13AA09CC71726C2F%4039-13AA09CC71726C2F%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=8&pdate=1937-08-01""","“(continued from Page 7) \n Miss Sherman, who not only has enjoyed painting but has reveled in exhibitions of ancient and modern Chinese art. \n During their absence their San Diego apartments are being occupied by Mr. and Mrs. Tom Allen and their children, Betty and Freddie, who have returned from Ojai. They will remain here indefinitely. \n The spacious home and gardens of Mrs. Edward Snowden Baxendale and Mrs. Jennette Emerson at La Jolla was the happy setting of a party given Wednesday in honor of Miss Margaret Stowe of Alabama, who is the niece of Mr. Baxendale and a guest in the Baxendale residence. Luncheon was served in the patio and bridge played later. \n The men went into the kitchen last night at the home of Judge and Mrs. Clarence Terry to prepare a delectable turtle dinner while their wives frittered away the unaccustomed leisure. The party was inspired by the fact that a Japanese friend of the host and hostess had caught a large turtle in Magdalena bay and had presented it to the Terrys. \n The all-turtle menu began with turtle soup, Italian style; turtle Seekh Kabab, Indian style; French Le Navarin Ou Ragout de Turtle baked turtle, Terry style, and turtle steak. Judge Terry was chief chef. The party included Mr. and Mrs. Tucker Fisher, Mr. and Mrs. James Forward jr., Mr. and Mrs. Earl Gustafson, Mr. and Mrs. Herbert Hafter, Mr. and Mrs. Harold Karns, Mrs. and Mrs. James Louvau, Mr. and Mrs. Ronald Millar, Mr. and Mrs. Jefferson K. Stickney jr., Mr. and Mrs. Robert Thompson, Mr. and Mrs. Virgil Wyatt, Mr. and Mrs. Fred de Silva, Mr. and Mrs. Richard Patton. \n Stephanie Barker, daughter of Mrs. Terry Barker, left Friday with her aunt, Mrs. Brauner Hatfield, for San Francisco, where she will spend two or three weeks with relatives. Saturday with a barbecue supper at the Innes’ new barbecue pit which is picturesquely surrounded by tall graceful eucalyptus trees. She will compliment Miss Lucile Jackson of Austin, Tex., daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Ben Walter Jackson of Mexia, and Miss Ellen Brodnax, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Cox Brodnax of Mexia. Miss Jackson recently finished at C. I. A. in Dallas. \n Miss Innes’ guest will number 50. \n Mr. and Mrs. Robert Frazee entertained informally at dinner last night, their guests including Point Loma neighbors, Mrs. Frazee’s sister, Mrs. Frederick Muhs, and Mr. Muhs will return here from Berkeley about Aug. 10. \n Mrs. Lewis Utt, mother of Mrs. Muhs and Mrs. Frazee, is now in Vienna after having thoroughly enjoyed her trip through Norway and Sweden. In October she will meet Dr. Anita Muhl and Miss Zellah Ryan in southern France. The three travelers will go to India, via the Suez, and later to Australia for Christmas. \n Mrs. Frank Donovan Owners showed Mr. and Mrs. Wright Moncure of San Francisco, Coronado’s night life last week, the glamorous attractions including roller skating and smelt hunting! \n Mrs. Owens is about to be persuaded to join Lt. and Mrs. Louis Hunte (Emily Fenton) in the Canal zone sometime before Christmas. The Huntes have charming quarters. \n Mrs. John Griffin left yesterday for San Francisco with Mr. and Mrs. Marsden Smith of Baltimore, who have been her guests across the bay.”","2","""Casual Collegiates Spotlighted Before Campus Days Return; Many Entertain Attractive Guests""","1","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""single turtle from magdalena bay served for multi-course dinner at event""","1937",8,"high",NA,1,NA,1930,"magdalena_bay","comondu","baja_california_sur","mexico",24.5833326,-112
+"111","laherald_18861127","""Los Angeles Herald""",1886-11-27,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH18861127.2.13&srpos=231&e=-------en--20-LAH-221--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""The Mersch Bros. at stalls 15, 17, and 19, Mott market, have received a sea turtle which is the giant of the age in this line so far as Los Angeles goes. The ""monster of the deep"" weighs 640 pounds. Here is the chance for gourmets to get a steak, or a piece for soup which does not turn up every day. Go in and take a look at the fellow and feel his teeth. It is fine.""","2","""Turtle Soup or Steak.""","1","green","inferred","inferred","640","not_reported","not_reported","""the location was not specified, though it vaguely sounds as though it was from LA or the nearby area""","1886",7,"high",NA,1,290.3,1880,"los_angeles","los_angeles","california","usa",34.020479,-118.4117325
+"112","laherald_19070613","""Los Angeles Herald""",1907-06-13,10,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19070613.2.109&srpos=125&e=-------en--20-LAH-121--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""Special to The Herald. \n LONG BEACH, June 12.—Two years ago Capt. Billy Graves gaffed a huge turtle while out fishing in his boat, Twilight. Yesterday, while out on the banks with a party in the new boat Tourist, Capt. Graves met a turtle which he declares to be the same giant which escaped from him before. \n The big turtle yesterday swam along by the side of the boat and laid one of his ""flippers"" upon the craft as if he wanted to turn the Tourist upside down. Graves ran to get his rifle, but when he returned the monster was a hundred yards to leeward. The skipper attempted to follow, but the engine in the boat went wrong and the quest was abandoned.""","2","""GAFFED TURTLE SEEKS REVENGE ON SKIPPER""","1","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1907",7,"high",NA,1,NA,1900,"long_beach","los_angeles","california","usa",33.800208,-118.156115
+"113","laherald_19070901","""Los Angeles Herald""",1907-09-01,6,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19070901.2.93&srpos=216&e=-------en--20-LAH-201--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","“Special to The Herald. \n LONG BEACH, Aug. 31. -With the big devil fish recently captured near here with the gigantic turtle which every visitor to this beach will remember, and a monster swordfish caught near San Pedro, Robert Griffin, agent for the Diamond Boat company, will soon go ""on the road."" \n He expects to make good money with his assortment of deep sea wonders.”","2","""WILL TOUR WITH TURTLE, SWORDFISH AND OCTOPUS""","1","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""article says that the turtle was caught 'near' Long Beach, though exact location not reported; also claims that the turtle is 'gigantic' though no size is given""","1907",7,"high",NA,1,NA,1900,"long_beach","los_angeles","california","usa",33.800208,-118.156115
+"114","spdaily_19070729","""San Pedro Daily News""",1907-07-29,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPDN19070729.2.12&srpos=14&e=-------en--20-SPDN-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Long Beach, July 29.— Captain Bert Moody in the launch seabright, yesterday sighted a monster turtle and ran up to it, but the shellback was too quick for him and went to the bottom. He says the monster is twenty feet long and would weigh 3000 pounds. \n Captain Billy Graves sunk a harpoon into the old fellow a year ago and a month later Captain Faschall ran up alongside the same turtle and found the harpoon still imbedded in his shell. Its home is about five miles from shore near the fishing banks, but he fights shy of tourists.""","2","""Monster Turtle At Home.""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","3000","20'","not_reported","""given the (likely exaggerated) size and the fact that the shell was able to be harpooned, this was likely a leatherback, rather than a green""","1907",8,"high",609.6,1,1360.78,1900,"long_beach","los_angeles","california","usa",33.800208,-118.156115
+"115","ljjournal_19230126","""La Jolla Light and La Jolla Journal""",1923-01-26,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LJJ19230126.2.8&srpos=1&e=-------en--20-LJJ-1-byDA-txt-txIN-large+tortoise+visits-------""","""A huge turtle visited the coast in the vicinity of Hotel Windansea Thursday, and swam inquisitively along near the beach, so that people on shore got a good look at him. There are very large turtles in these waters, but it is not usual for them to be seen at this time of year. When La Jolla was a smaller town, a turtle was speared occasionally, and fed the whole place on turtle steak and soup. The meat is not unlike sirloin steak, dark, and of excellent flavor, and is easily fixed, but real turtle soup is another matter, having many ingredients, and requiring time and care in preparation.""","1","""Large Tortoise Visits South Shore""","1","green","inferred","known","not_reported","not_reported","""along the beach""","""This article mentions that the sighting of turtles in la jolla is quite rare""","1923",8,"high",NA,1,NA,1920,"la_jolla","san_diego","california","usa",32.8725212,-117.248572
+"116","sppilot_19320325","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1932-03-25,13,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19320325.2.177&srpos=26&e=-------en--20-SPNP-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""HERMOSA BEACH, March 25. — Olympic barge anglers were all agog yesterday after seeing a huge sea turtle swimming in the smooth water around the barge. Later in the day the turtle moved in close to shore and appeared to be having a great time riding breakers. The gigantic beast weighed in the neighborhood of 300 pounds. While these turtles are very plentiful on Santa Cruz Island and San Clemente Island, they are seldom seen near the mainland.""","1","""HUGE SEA TURTLE SEEN BY ANGLERS""","1","green","inferred","inferred","300","not_reported","""near shore riding breakers""","""The article notes that it is unusual to see a [green] turtle in this area: 'While these turtles are very plentiful on Santa Cruz Island and San Clemente Island, they are seldom seen near the mainland.' Location is inferred based on that it says it was near/based in Hermosa beach""","1932",7,"high",NA,1,136.08,1930,"hermosa_beach","los_angeles","california","usa",33.8646948,-118.3974148
+"117","stinson_sdherald_18870303","""San Diego Herald""",1887-03-03,NA,"51","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""'Two big sea turtles, one weighing 360 pounds, were brought up on the Pacheco yesterday, from Ensenada, and are now to be seen at Connor's market.' [classified ad]: 'Turtle soup for the whole county at Connor's Market. Go and see the big sea turtles.'""","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","2","green","inferred","known","360","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1887",7,"high",NA,2,163.29,1880,"ensenada","ensenada","baja_california","mexico",31.8423287,-116.6099414
+"118","sdunion_19080628","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1908-06-28,15,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19080628.2.82&srpos=119&e=-------en--20-SDDU-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","""The steamer National City, Capt. J. D. Higgins, arrived in port yesterday from San Pedro and discharged a portion of her cargo of lumber at the Western Lumber company’s wharf, proceeding on to the Russ wharf, where she is now completing unloading. \n The steam schooner Bernardo Reyes arrived in port yesterday afternoon from Ensenada, members of her crew bringing with them the report of the capture of a monster turtle en route. The turtle weighed in the neighborhood of 1999 pounds, it is alleged, and was speared as it lay asleep. Upon being hauled to the deck it was found to have six large ridges of fin-like material on its back, according to the statements of a member of the crew, a neck as large around as a man’s, and two semi-tusks protruding from its mouth. It measured over five feet from head to tail, it is asserted. The turtle died on the way here and was thrown overboard, said the captain of the Bernardo Reyes, hence the statements of the crew will have to be accepted as to the size and species of the monster of the deep.""","2","""WATERFRONT AND SHIPPING NEWS""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","1999","5'","not_reported","""descriptions of weight and length are estimates, and while species name is not explicitly reported, based on descriptions, it is clear to be a leatherback.""","1908",8,"high",152.4,1,906.73,1900,"ensenada","ensenada","baja_california","mexico",31.8423287,-116.6099414
+"119","sdunion_19080623","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1908-06-23,8,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19080623.2.96&srpos=1&e=------190-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""E. R. Kline Lands Rare Species Weighing 800 Pounds After Three Hours' Struggle. \n After fighting desperately for over three hours to regain its freedom an immense royal sea turtle was landed at the La Jolla bath house yesterday afternoon about 4 o'clock by K. B. Kline, who caught the big fellow in the cove near Del Mar. Although not placed on the scales it was estimated that the turtle would weigh at least 800 pounds. \n Kline was out in a small boat when he noticed the turtle asleep in the kelp near Del Mar. Approaching the sleeping denizen of the deep he succeeded in sinking his spear into its body. Then began the struggle between man and turtle, which only ended some three hours later when Kline landed his trophy at La Jolla. \n The turtle, owing to its immense size and particular species, was taken to the biological station for purposes of study. There it was cattalogued as a loggerhead, which, while infesting the Pacific, are seldom found so close inshore. The turtle measured more than six feet in length, with immense flippers, like a seal; a head larger than a man's head and powerful jaws.""","2","""Mammoth Turtle Makes Desperate Fight For Liberty""","1","loggerhead","reported","known","800","not_reported","""asleep in the kelp""","""would need to double check the size estimate--whether it seems reasonable. Also, the habitat report""","1908",10,"high",NA,1,362.87,1900,"del_mar","san_diego","california","usa",32.958574,-117.2637655
+"120","laherald_19140627","""Los Angeles Herald""",1914-06-27,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19140627.2.45&srpos=86&e=-------en--20-LAH-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""A 750-pound turtle with a sawtoothed back was the prize captured by Bert Maller and Charles Berger in a skiff eleven miles off Dana point, Capistrano, and brought to this city. The turtle is the largest ever caught In these waters.""","2","""TWO IN SKIFF TAKE 750 POUND TURTLE""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","known","750","not_reported","not_reported","""possible leatherback due to size and having 'saw-toothed back'; location specifically is dana point, capistrano""","1914",8,"high",NA,1,340.19,1910,"dana_point","orange","california","usa",33.4692889,-117.6884229
+"121","latimes_19100602","""Los Angeles Times""",1910-06-02,10,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/159414624/590BBC08B3D34453PQ/89?accountid=14026""","“GIANT TURTLE IS TAKEN ON MALIBU COAST. \n Threat of One Relative Against Another Follows the Preliminary Examination of Figueroa, Accused of the Murder of His Bride—Orators Train for High School Contest. \n SANTA MONICA, June 1-From the mystic Malibu coast has come a giant sea turtle that, according to some scientists, must have been celebrating the one hundredth anniversary of its birth about the time the three wise men were searching for the Star of Bethlehem. Those who profess to know something of the life and habits of these strange creatures of the deep arc authority for the statement that this monster could not have been less than 2000 years of age. This turtle was of the leather-back variety and weighed 1500 pounds and is said to have been one of the largest ever captured. It came from near the mouth of Corral Cañon, seventeen miles from here, and was discovered on the beach by William Campbell. \n The monster was alive when Campbell was passing along the strand. It had evidently been washed in by the tide and was probably sick, as but little resistance was offered by it. The thing was killed by beating its head with a rock. The flesh was cut away and the great shell, six feet long and almost five feet across, was preserved. The flippers were three feet long.”","1","""SEA GIVES UP A PATRIARCH""","1","leatherback","reported","inferred","1500","not_reported","not_reported","""location is corral canyon, near malibu. The shell along was 6 feet long and five feet wide, with flippers being 3 feet long""","1910",7,"high",NA,1,680.39,1910,"corral_canyon","los_angeles","california","usa",34.0346669,-118.7348047
+"122","sdut_19591101","""San Diego Union Tribune""",1959-11-01,NA,NA,"""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-13C3C5719FAB28F5%402436874-13BF771AB5FC70B4%403-13BF771AB5FC70B4%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=2&pdate=1959-11-01""","“[image included] \n —San Diego Union Staff Photo. \n Holding up two pilot fish attached to the back of a 50-pound sea turtle they caught off Coronados are Carter Mahrdt, 11, and his brother, Clark, 13. \n Two Encinitas boys got a bigger thrill out of their first deep sea fishing trip yesterday than they had anticipated. \n Clark Mahrdt, 13, and his brother, Carter, 11, went out seeking yellowtail and ended up with a 50-pound sea turtle. \n The brothers set out at 2:30 a.m. for the Coronados with their father, Wiliam Mahrdt, a masonry contractor, of 215 Vulcan Ave., Encinitas, and Hollis O’Hanlon, of 2004 Coast Blvd., Del Mar, in O’Hanlon’s 35-foot boat, the Boxer. \n They began fishing with live bait about daybreak. \n O’Hanlon said they were having very little luck when the turtle took the boys’ bait about 9 a.m. The hook caught in the shell of its mouth, and the boys managed to get it to the side of the boat. The skipper and their father brought it aboard. \n Attached to the turtle’s shell were two pilot fish. \n “The boys got more of a thrill from the turtle than they would have from a dozen yellowtail,"" O'Hanlon declared. \n He said the youngsters’ eyes were popping and their mouths were agape when they brought the catch close enough to the boat to see what it was. \n “We thought it was impossible,” declared Clark. \n O’Hanlon said the turtle was about 500 miles north of its regular territory. “They very seldom come this far north,” he said. \n The catch attracted a crowd of onlookers when it was landed at Pitcairn Marina on Shelter Island, where O’Hanlon’s boat is moored. \n The men said they planned to contact the Scripps Institution of Oceanography to see if officials there have any use for the turtle.”","2","""2 Young Encinitas Fishermen Land 50-pound Sea Turtle""","1","loggerhead","inferred","inferred","50","not_reported","not_reported","""claimed that the turtle was out of its normal habitat: 'about 500 iles north of its regular territory'. species id is based off of the included image""","1959",7,"high",NA,1,22.68,1950,"coronados","tijuana","baja_california","mexico",32.4207422,-117.2608143
+"123","sdunion_19120827","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1912-08-27,13,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19120827.2.148&srpos=10&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""Reptile Believed to Have Swam to These Waters From The Indies \n J. Preoata, Roy Edwards. J. Rodriguez, W. Westfall, D. Tann and C. Pruett, fishermen in the employ of the San Diego Fish company, yesterday captured what is believed to be the largest turtle ever caught in this vicinity. The men were engaged in fishing on the western banks; about five miles southwest of the Coronado islands, when a member of the party sighted the monster, which, when they finally landed it at the dock after a seven hour struggle, weighed 2500 pounds. \n The reptile measured 7 feet 2 inches from the tip of its nose to tip of tall, was 5 feet 2 inches wide and 2 feet thick. The liver weighed 100 pounds, and the shell, after it had been cleaned and scraped, weighed a trifle over 200 pounds. According to Edwards, who kept the shell as a souvenir, the turtle put up a terrific fight before it was finally landed. A steel harpoon was broken in the first attempt to kill the reptile. The men then determined to take a half hitch with a hawser around the monster's fins and tow it in port. This they finally succeeded in accomplishing, after a seven-hour-fight In which it broke away from its captors three times. The men repeatedly had to gaff the turtle in order to replace the line over its flippers. \n It is believed the turtle came from the Indies, taking several years to make the trip. The shell is not similar to those found on the species which frequent the Galapagos Islands and which are noted for their size. The reptile also had the pointed nose which is characteristic of the Indian turtle. \n The fishermen killed their game soon after it was landed at the San Diego Fishing company's wharf at the foot of D street and the meat put in cold storage. According to the captors the turtle was disporting with a small whale when it was first sighted by them.""","2","""Turtle Weighing 2500 Pounds Is Captured After 7-Hour Struggle""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","known","2500","7'2""","not_reported","""also noted the width and thickness as 5'2"" and 2 feet, respectively. Species was not identified so assumed green, but if the size was indeed so large this seems unlikely. Additionally, even if this were a leatherback, 2500 lbs seems too high.""","1912",8,"high",218.44,1,1133.98,1910,"coronado_islands","tijuana","baja_california","mexico",32.4207422,-117.2608143
+"124","sdunion_19180306","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1918-03-06,10,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19180306.2.142&srpos=35&e=-------en--20-SDDU-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","""A large leather back sea turtle was caught recently by the Ressiean brothers and was taken to George Kline, biologist at La Jolla. It was harpooned in the kelp fields off the Coronado Islands. The turtle is one of the largest on record, weighing 1570 pounds. \n It is 11 feet from tip to tip, has a head like a walrus, two distinct tails and a soft, fur-llke body. There are seven ribs running lengthwise of the body. The leather back is almost extinct now, having been hunted out in years past for the blubber, that was used for medicinal purposes.""","2","""Leatherback Turtle, 1570 Lbs., Caught""","1","leatherback","reported","known","1570","11'","""in the kelp fields""","""claims that leatherback turtles were nearly extinct at that time""","1918",10,"high",335.28,1,712.14,1910,"coronado_islands","tijuana","baja_california","mexico",32.4207422,-117.2608143
+"125","laherald_19140915","""Los Angeles Herald""",1914-09-15,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19140915-02.2.11&srpos=212&e=-------en--20-LAH-201--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""Charley Rand will give a dinner this evening at Baron Long’s Country club, complimentary to Mel Nordlinger, his partner upon a recent cruise to Cedros island. While they were away they hooked a 420-pound turtle, bringing it back alive. It has been cooked and will form the chief number on the menu. Dinner starts with turtle soup at 7 o’clock. It is an invitation affair.""","2","""COMPLIMENTARY DINNER GIVEN AT BARON LONG’S""","1","green","inferred","inferred","420","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1914",7,"high",NA,1,190.51,1910,"cedros_island","ensenada","baja_california","mexico",28.2012034,-115.2522841
+"126","latimes_18890909","""Los Angeles Times""",1889-09-09,5,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/163507545/fulltextPDF/C8D12580C68F44A0PQ/1?accountid=14026""","“The bird in his native Catalinian lair. \n A Turtle Turn—The Delights of Turtle Eggs—Catalina Rowers Surprised—The Yellow Tails Still to the Fore. \n Avalon (Santa Catalina Island), Sept. 6.—[Special correspondence of The Times.] A party from the Hotel Metropole were rowing around the point south of the hotel near Pebble Beach yesterday, when a curious round head popped out of the water, and a low hiss broke the stillness. One of the party was sure that it was a sea serpent; another thought it was something no one had ever seen before; but the head belonged to a fine green turtle, a big fellow, that would have tipped the scales at 500 pounds at least, and if turtle soup is not served at the hotel this week, it will not be the fault of the fishermen, who are camping on its trail night and day. \n Several of these reptiles have been seen about Catalina this summer, and it is supposed that they have been ashore, certain curious marks on the beach that puzzled the finders being laid at their door. If this is so there was a nest, and as out of any batch of turtle eggs laid a goodly per cent fail to change into young turtles, there must be some eggs there yet. \n Several parties have been out scouring the sandy beaches, and all sorts of trails have been followed up and worked in vain. The green turtle, in the experience of the writer, is fond of strolling on the sands on moonlight nights, and if by any chance the big fellows decide to come in there will be some great sport, and perhaps the would-be turtles would like some pointers from an old hand at the sport, who has “turned” a good many, and been laid out if not knocked out. \n On the last green turtle party it was my good fortune to attend, there were four or five ladies, so that six or seven couples were made up, and by 9 o’clock, just as the full moon was rising we were stationed all along the sandy beach of an island about one-fourth as large as Catalina. \n Every few moments the gentlemen of each party would start out and patrol the space between each other, walking rapidly along near the water watching for tracks. We did this for half an hour, when I noticed two tracks leading up from the water. \n Following them up on a run I almost stumbled on a big green turtle, busily engaged in throwing the sand out of a hole. The moment she saw me she turned, and shouting for help I seized her by the shell, and lifted. I raised her about a foot, then her big flipper came swinging around, filling my eyes full of san and almost knocking me over, while she went rapidly toward the water. Three of us caught her again about half way down and with a piece of board, tried to turn her, but not until the fourth man came, did we succeed in turning the largest green turtle I ever saw. They do not bite, but for throwing sand and scraping gravel, they beat the world. \n The turtle season to the south of here is in May or June, and what the big fellows are doing in shore now, when they should be in the high seas, is difficult to determine. \n La Paloma, Hancock Banning’s yacht, picked one up on her trip down from San Diego, and probably the one that has put in an appearance around Acalon will be caught. \n The fishing for the past week has been the best of the season, and is being enjoyed by large numbers of Los Angeles people. The summer rush is over, and ample accommodations are to be had by those who do not like the crowds of the mid-summer time. The Metropole has a delightful society of its own, about a hundred guests being there, many families having taken rooms for the month of September, which promises to be the gayest of the season. Admission day is to be celebrated here in fine style, a yacht race, tub race, pig hunt, swimming match and various diversions being arranged for. \n The Hermosa makes a grand excursion around the island with a band, and the Wanda and Hartie have arranged for special excursions to the various points of interest. \n The yellowtail fishing which started in so furiously the first of the week has kept up, and every day good catches have been made. \n Mr. Sumner is arranging for a grand goat hunt at Middle ranch, in which a number of Los Angeles sportsmen will participate.”","2","""GREEN TURTLE.""","4-5","green","reported","known","500","not_reported","not_reported","""This reports multiple different encounters, at different times, with the green turtles--therefore the num_turtles is slightly uncertain""","1889",9,"high",NA,NA,226.8,1880,"catalina_island","los_angeles","california","usa",33.3889849,-118.4549433
+"127","spdaily_19060508","""San Pedro Daily News""",1906-05-08,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPDN19060508.2.25&srpos=12&e=-------en--20-SPDN-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Fisherman Brings In Sixteen Hundred Pound Turtle. \n The largest turtle on record was captured yesterday afternoon by Jerrie Marincovich, an Austrian fisherman who kas fishing off Catalina Island. The animal weighs nearly sixteen hundred pounds and is one of the most remarkable fish of its kind. It measures eight full feet in width from tip to tip, and is over seven feet in length. \n The largest turtle ever landed previous to this catch was caught by Geo. Evich a little over a year ago and weighed 1280 pounds. \n This latest record comes as a surprise to many of the old as well as young fishermen who have never before beheld such a specimen. Jerrie Marincovich, with his crew, were fishing for barracuda when they discovered a huge black mass in their nets which took the combined strength of all on board to pull it up. The immense turtle while being dragged the boat was drowned before the craft came ashore here. It is not yet decided just what will be done with the creature, but it is most likely that the same person who stuffed the large shark which was caught some time ago will be notified at Los Angeles and it is most probable that he will buy the animal to stuff and put on exhibition.""","2","""SEA MONSTER CAPTURED.""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","1600","7'","not_reported","""This is the same observation (redundant) with latimes_19060509. other measurements include 8 feet in width; article also notes that another turtle was caught in the same area about a year prior, just slightly smaller in size. The prior capture refers to latimes_19040514. Inferred as loggerhead due to latimes_19060509.""","1906",8,"high",213.36,1,725.75,1900,"catalina_island","los_angeles","california","usa",33.3889849,-118.4549433
+"128","sppilot_19300706","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1930-07-06,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19300716.2.13&srpos=55&e=-------en--20-SPNP-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""760-pound sea turtle, brought into San Pedro several days ago, has met the usual fate of such specimens. His “in'ards’’ have been removed to fill a few hundred soup bowls, while his great outer shell is going on display along the Long Beach pike. The big turtle, of the leather back variety, was kept alive for several days at the Los Angeles Fish & Oyster company’s receiving station, municipal wharf. He was caught by a local fisherman in the vicinity of Catalina Island, and is believed to be the largest sea turtle ever captured in California waters.""","1","""THE USUAL FATE""","1","leatherback","reported","known","760","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1930",8,"high",NA,1,344.73,1930,"catalina_island","los_angeles","california","usa",33.3889849,-118.4549433
+"129","latimes_19060509","""Los Angeles Times""",1906-05-09,13,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164520858/590BBC08B3D34453PQ/145?accountid=14026""","“CHELONIAN REPTILE CAPTURED FROM A LAUNCH. \n Record for Deep-Sea Turtles Broken by a Catch of Fifteen-Hundred-Round Creature—Age Calculated at Between Two and Five Centuries. 'Tis a Reg’lar Festudo Lyra. \n [SPECIAL CORRESPONDENCE OF THE TIMES.] \n SAN PEDRO, May 8.—San Pedro seems to be able to hold its own in the matter of deep-sea monsters Just a year ago George Evich interested the scientific world by bringing to this port over half a ton of turtle caught by him in the launch Bell. Today Jerry Marincovich went Evich one better and captured a 1500-pound crustacean in his nets off Catalina Island. The desperate attempts of the huge crustacean to escape resulted in its death, and it is now on exhibition at the Morgan Oyster Company in this city. \n The turtle captured by Marincovich today is undoubtedly the largest crustacean ever captured and hundreds of people today have marveled at its size. Its age is estimated at between 200 and 500 years. \n The turtle belongs to the species Festudo lyra, or ""Sphargis mercavialis,"" these names having reference to the myth that the shell of some turtle was used by Mercury in the construction of the lyre. This family is Chelonian. \n Before Evich’s capture a year ago the largest marine turtle ever captured was taken in the Galapagos group of islands several years ago and was taken to London. It weighed 850 pounds and was known to have been over eighty years of age. It was still growing at the time of its death. \n The age of the turtle taken today is a matter of conjecture, but his corrugated coat of mail full of the knocks of time would indicate that he had existed for several centuries. \n The Chelonia family of turtles possess a tail but no teeth. Jaws are provided with a horny sheath with hard and sharp edges, forming a beak like a parrot’s Marine turtles are spread over the equatorial and subtropical seas, and sometimes stray beyond these limits, being seldom taken above San Diego \n The Chelonians are diurnal animals and only a few of the family are active during the night \n They are extremely quick in the water, either to seize their prey or to escape danger. The marine turtle periodically migrates to breeding stations. The species inhabiting temperate waters hibernate \n The marine turtle possesses great tenacity of life, surviving injuries to which other reptiles would succumb in a short time. The heart of a decapitated marine turtle will beat many hours after every drop of blood has been drained from the body and the muscles of the trunk and head show signs of reflex action twenty-four hours after the severing of the spinal cord. \n Some marine turtles are herbivorous and others carnivorous their prey consisting of fish frogs and other aquatic animals The limbs are modified into paddles by means of which they propel themselves through the water with extraordinary rapidity, but are unfit for locomotion on land, where their progress is as awkward as that of a seal The toes are enclosed in a common skin out of which only one or two claws project. \n The catch made today by Marincovich is important from a Scientific standpoint on account of the great size of the monster. \n The carapace is seven feet long and six feet wide and the weight is easily 1500 pounds. The monster will be mounted and sold to some museum.”","2","""SEA MONSTERS OFF SAN PEDRO.""","1","leatherback","reported","known","1500","not_reported","not_reported","""this appears to be the same observation as spdaily_19060508, and also refers to evich's catch from latimes_19040514, but is a different observation. The species is identified by the label of 'festudo lyra' and 'sphargis mercavialis', unsure if this is a leatherback or a loggerhead. Only the size of the carapce is given, as 7x6 feet.""","1906",9,"high",NA,1,680.39,1900,"catalina_island","los_angeles","california","usa",33.3889849,-118.4549433
+"130","sdut_19670525","""San Diego Union Tribune""",1967-05-25,NA,NA,"""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-13C6231276D26CA6%402439636-13C36A7358F28C88%4010-13C36A7358F28C88%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=5&pdate=1967-05-25""","“An 890-pound turtle with a shell 7½ feet long towed a 25-foot cabin cruiser 5 miles through Catalina Channel before it died of a broken back and exhaustion, four Long Beach fishermen reported. The Leatherback Turtle, rare in waters this far north, collided with the cruiser and the impact tore off the boat’s rudder and cracked the turtle’s shell. Tomas Dobson, the boat’s owner, said the crew gaffed the turtle, roped it with an anchor line, then fashioned a makeshift rudder as the turtle headed toward the harbor.”","2","""The World at a Glance""","1","leatherback","reported","known","890","not_reported","not_reported","""location could also be reclassified as (santa) catalina island; the shell is listed as 7.5 feet long""","1967",9,"high",NA,1,403.7,1960,"catalina_channel","los_angeles","california","usa",33.3889849,-118.4549433
+"131","sdut_19140730","""San Diego Union Tribune""",1914-07-30,14,NA,"""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-139A43ECF89E12A0%402420344-13979E268AA433E0%4011-13979E268AA433E0%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=8&pdate=1914-07-30""","“Union Photograph of 1175 Pound Turtle Brought to Port Yesterday. This Turtle is Said By Waterfront Men to be the Largest Specimen Ever Caught In Lower California Waters. [image included] \n Appearance of Shell Leads Fisherman to Believe Marine Giant Nearly 200 Years Old and Probably Come From Tropics. \n A huge turtle, weighing 1175 pounds, was brought to port yesterday morning by Captain J. Zollezzi, of the Italian fishing launch Belvedere. The turtle, said by waterfront men to be the largest specimen ever caught in Lower California waters, is nine feet in circumference and seven feet from the tip of its nose to the end of its short tail. The front flippers are four feet in length and almost a foot wide, tapering gradually until at the end they are about five inches wide. \n The jaws are a solid mass of teeth, over 200 being located in the upper jaw. They range from one-half to a quarter of an inch in length. The shell is ribbed and in spots pliable, which leads many fishermen to believe that the turtle is nearly 200 years old. The lower part of the jaw is streaked with gray. \n Captain Zollezzi reported that the turtle was captured after a terrific struggle five miles off Cape Colonet on the Lower California coast. The turtle became entangled in Zollezzi’s fishnets, and before it could be extricated about $50 worth of fishing gear was destroyed. \n C. Y. Wiley and M. Mose are seen in the photograph holding the turtle’s front flippers. Wiley believes that the sea reptile originally came from the Galapagos islands, below the equator, as the turtles in those waters are noted for their enormous size. The turtle will be placed on exhibition today at the American Union Fish Company’s plant at the foot of G street.”","2","""Sea Turtle Tips Scale at 1175 Pounds Catch Largest Taken in Coast Waters Reptile Landed After Terrific Battle""","1","leatherback","inferred","known","1175","7'","""entangled in fishnets""","""additional size details are given as 9 feet in circumference, 4 feet in flipper length. There is also an illustration to help with species identification, but still unclear. Location is given as 5 miles off Cape Colonet (ensenada/baja california)""","1914",9,"high",213.36,1,532.97,1910,"cape_colonet","ensenada","baja_california","mexico",30.9704609,-116.3335471
+"132","latimes_19130731","""Los Angeles Times""",1913-07-31,4,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/159796548/2796BCF9F7524B0FPQ/40?accountid=14026""","""Weighs Eight Hundred Pounds, Is Eight Hundred Years Old, Comes From California. \n [DIRECT WIRE TO THE TIMES.]·\n NEW YORK BUREAU OF THE TIMES, July 30.—[Exclusive Dispatch.] One member was added today to the ""zoo"" family in the Central Park menagerie. The newcomer is a large leatherback turtle, said to be one of the largest in captivity. It weighs 866 pounds, measures five foot, six inches from nose to the tip of tall and three feet, six inches across the back. \n Benjamin Katzenstein presented the turtle to the park department. He says it was caught in the Pacific Ocean off the California coast, and it is one of the deep sea species. \n Katzenstein pointed out sixteen squares on the turtle’s back and said that each of the squares represented fifty years of life. This would make the turtle 800 years old.""","2","""HERE'S RECORD GIANT TURTLE.""","1","leatherback","reported","inferred","866","5'6""","not_reported","""further size notes that it is 3'6"" in width""","1913",8,"high",167.64,1,392.81,1910,"california","na","california","usa",37.2691675,-119.306607
+"133","laherald_19080801","""Los Angeles Herald""",1908-08-01,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19080801.2.72.3&srpos=176&e=-------en--20-LAH-161--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""A two hundred-pound turtle—a dead one—was washed ashore at Balboa beach last week. No cause of death was apparent in the creature, according the Newport News. 'Perhaps the poor turtle was struck by the tusks of a wild ""blind pig"" while paddling past Newport.—Huntington Beach News.""","1","""Tragic Fate of Turtle""","1","green","inferred","known","200","not_reported","""washed ashore dead""","""this turtle washed ashore and was found dead""","1908",8,"high",NA,1,90.72,1900,"balboa_beach","orange","california","usa",33.5970701,-117.8911576
+"134","spdaily_19200225","""San Pedro Daily News""",1920-02-25,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPDN19200225.2.14&srpos=112&e=-------en--20-SPDN-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Sixty Per Cent of Investment in Southern California and Forty-four Per Cent of Total Values Created in Local Harbor. \n Fisheries students long have marveled at the phenomenal growth of California’s sea food industries during the war; but figures just issued by the State Fish and Game Commission have an equally compelling interest for the general public. \n Aided by the various enactments of the last legislature rendering all facts accessible concerning catches at their origin, and reinforcing them by requiring exact reports as to the pack, the fisheries statisticians have found that the preserving or fish alone has now gone considerably beyond the “Twenty Million a Year” class. Not counting the enormously growing fresh fish demand, now that Californians really have begun to take their fish more seriously as a food supply, the export fisheries business alone takes rank as a capital industry of the first magnitude on its showing during 1919. \n The compilation shows that nearly eight million was invested in canning, drying, salting, smoking and pickling sea products giving employment to no less than 8242 persons. This harmonious combination of capital and labor swelled the wealth of the state by the splendid total of $21,417,743. \n Southern California again led the state, and took rank as foremost center of production by contributions to this total of, respectively 60 per cent or $12,294,064 and 44 per cent or $9,694,482. The spreading sphere of the truly excellent California sardine in the world’s markets is attested by its pre-eminence in the total pack, surpassing even the ""sea chicken” already so well set up on a gastronomic pedestal of its own by the albacore tuna canners. \n Many species none too generally known in their fresh state, have been packed in varying quantities, some experimentally, others on such substantial basis as Monterey’s 3538 cases of the toothsome and delicate squid, only properly appreciated by the Mediterranean peoples. The epicurean green turtle, celebrated of bou-vivants the world over, figures to the extent of 7407 cases, the “raw material” having been imported from the sandy strands of Lower California bays where the turtles come ashore to deposit their eggs. \n Evidences of constant effort to add something to the food of the world are abundant in the fisheries compilation of 1919, suggesting general appreciation among the canners of the great truth that because it can, eventually the sea must feed the land. Packers of fish are embracing new products at a rate that speaks well for the building of an immense direct export business of canned sea foods from the ever-widening harbors of the south where produced, to those foreign peoples who already have learned to laugh at the meat barons through mastery of preparation, and appreciation of the food value of fish. \n When the figures on last year’s fresh fish business are compiled by the Fish and Game Commission, it is predicted upon the information already tabulated, that some more and bigger surprises are in store. While it is true that Californians as yet have not fully learned to eat fish, the figures make it apparent that they are rapidly learning. High meat prices, war premium upon every nature of food supply, and the wide advertisement given sea products by state conservation authorities played their part, but the genuine excellence of the Pacific Coast fish themselves when properly prepared is really doing it. Housewives are gradually learning that a fine fish is worth as much culinary attention, and as loving preparation as they would lavish upon a choice roast or fowl, or a piece of game. Foreign cooks learned that lesson long ago. The ready cooked products of the cannery, scientifically and deliciously prepared, have created considerable taste for the fresh product also, and the combination bids fair to go towards solving the present high cost of living by diversifying the present top-heavy demand upon meat, potatoes and a few other over-exploited staples. \n Despite the enormous tonnages represented by the figures, and notwithstanding the multiplication of canneries with capacities of 150 tons per day, the State fisheries experts insist that the possibilities of California’s marine resources are but scarcely scratched, as yet. Aliens make constant use of dozens of species little understood by Americans, fully equal to many in daily use, and superior to some. At present rates of increase, the astonishing totals of today’s fisheries business in another decade will seem trivial. Intensive methods, scientific preservation, elimination of some present wastes, are all along the trend of present times.""","2","""SAN PEDRO LEADS PORTS OF COAST IN PACKING FISH""","cargo","green","reported","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""part of the article is also continued on page 6; the article states that the location was 'lower california' aka Baja California. It also states a number of 'cases' of green turtle, but I am unsure if this means the number of individual turtles, or the number of cases of meat: 'The epicurean green turtle, celebrated of bou-vivants the world over, figures to the extent of 7407 cases'""","1920",8,"high",NA,114.9375,NA,1920,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
+"135","latimes_18990622","""Los Angeles Times""",1899-06-22,13,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/163891894/D4971430CE2047A5PQ/268?accountid=14026""","“SAN PEDRO, June 21. — [Regular Correspondence.] The prospect of establishing a cannery here for the packing of the sea products of the Lower California waters is favorable, according to S. W. Waring, late manager of the Catalina Conserving Company’s lobster-packing plant in this city. Mr. Waring returned Tuesday from a trip down the coast about one hundred and twenty miles beyond the Mexican line. Turtles of a kind very like the much-prized green turtle of commerce are there in abundance, and the transportation of them for packing at this port seems to be practicable. The turtles, which average about one hundred and twenty-five pounds in weight, and some of which weigh as much as 200 pounds, may be caught in shallow water. The natives capture them with the use of spears, but that method would not do for turtles to be brought here. They may be taken unawares by men wading in the water and approaching them when asleep. A quick little motion by hands grasping the lower edge of the shell on one side turns the turtle on its back, and it is then practically helpless. The turtle is made ready for transportation by tying its fins and placing a block of wood so that the head may not fall too far down and cause strangulation. Waring says he has shipped live turtles treated in that manner from Galveston, Tex., to New York city without injury to them. If the number of turtles brought to a cannery hero should be greater than could be used for a few days, they may doubtless be kept alive as long as desired in an in-closure in the bay. \n Eight kinds of meat may be obtained from the turtles, and from one of them weighing 125 pounds the edible parts weigh nearly one hundred pounds. In the same waters lobsters are plentiful and barracuda are abundant during the season when they are not found in such large numbers in the waters near here. There is no import duty on live or fresh fish taken in Mexican waters and brought into the United States, provided they are brought in boats of not less than thirty tons’ net register. If the fish bo in any manner preserved there is a duty of ¼ of a cent a pound imposed.”","2","""Prospective Sea-food Cannery""","cargo","green","reported","inferred","200","not_reported","""shallow water""","""says that average weight is 125, but max weight is 200. This article is about the prospect of establishing a cannery near LA and therefore would be fishing the turtles out of 'Lower California', and while it hadn't yet started, they were reportedly 'in abundance' for fishing at the time, and packing adn fishing is practical. Exact location just says 120 miles south of the US-Mexican border""","1899",8,"high",NA,114.9375,90.72,1890,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
+"136","stinson_sdunion_18890509","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1889-05-09,NA,"52","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Wharf and Waves. A Large Catch of Turtles. Executive Officer Harris, of the United States Survey Steamer Ranger, states that while off the Lower California coast, and bound for San Diego, they caught 177 turtles in one haul, the smallest weighing 50 pounds and the twenty largest weighing over 300 pounds""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","177","green","inferred","inferred","300","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1889",7,"high",NA,177,136.08,1880,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
+"137","stinson_sdherald_19830907","""San Diego Herald""",1983-09-07,NA,"48","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Speaking of turtles, however, we are indebted to Captain Bogart for the supply our bay now contains. In August 1856 [sic 1857], he returned from about three hundred and fifty miles down the coast with about fifty turtles. They were the kind known as the green turtle and rawbill. The largest weighed about three hundred and fifty pounds. They were all caught with a seine. Captain Bogart built a turtle pen in the bay, intending to send them to San Francisco, but they escaped from him and their children's children are often seen in our bay.""","2","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","150","green","reported","inferred","350","not_reported","not_reported","""same turtles/event as stinson_sdherald_18571003 (redundant)""","1983",8,"high",NA,150,158.76,1980,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
+"138","stinson_sdherald_19830907","""San Diego Herald""",1983-09-07,NA,"48","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Speaking of turtles, however, we are indebted to Captain Bogart for the supply our bay now contains. In August 1856 [sic 1857], he returned from about three hundred and fifty miles down the coast with about fifty turtles. They were the kind known as the green turtle and rawbill. The largest weighed about three hundred and fifty pounds. They were all caught with a seine. Captain Bogart built a turtle pen in the bay, intending to send them to San Francisco, but they escaped from him and their children's children are often seen in our bay.""","2","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","150","hawksbill","inferred","inferred","350","not_reported","not_reported","""same turtles/event as stinson_sdherald_18571003 (redundant), and same as row above but with a different species, because both are reported. The species is reported as ""rawbill"", which can be assumed as hawksbill""","1983",7,"high",NA,150,158.76,1980,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
+"139","sdherald_18571003","""San Diego Herald""",1857-10-03,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDH18571003.2.3&srpos=2&e=-------en--20-SDH-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","“Capt. J. C. Bogart, arrived here on Friday, from the lower coast, with 120 fine turtles, in good condition. He lost 60 by the breaking of the corral, which, with fifty that broke away some weeks ago, here, makes quite a loss. Wisdom comes with experience, as well in the construction of turtle pens as in other things.”","2","""The Sch Elizabeth Owens,""","120","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""the location was not further specified beyond the 'lower coast', so it is assumed to be baja""","1857",7,"high",NA,120,NA,1850,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
+"140","stinson_sdherald_18571003","""San Diego Herald""",1857-10-03,NA,"46-47","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""...The Schooner Elizabeth Owens, Captain J.C. Bogart, arrived here on Friday, from the lower coast, with 120 fine turtles in good condition. he lost 60 by the breaking of the corral, which with fifty [sic. or 40?] that broke away some weeks ago, here, makes quite a loss. Wisdom comes with experience, as well as in the construction of turtle pens.""","2","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","120","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""this article is reported in the san diego herald, and reports that 40-60 of these turtles escape from their corral into the san diego area""","1857",7,"high",NA,120,NA,1850,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
+"141","latimes_19170329","""Los Angeles Times""",1917-03-29,2,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160361911/590BBC08B3D34453PQ/65?accountid=14026""","""Competition Established in Lobster Importing. \n New Deal Below Line Giving Hope of Lower Cost. \n Big Fines for Trout and Quail Law Violators—Turtle. \n Competition in Lower California fisheries, so earnestly advocated by the Fish and Game Commission as a means of increasing supply and thereby lowering prices to fish consumers, set in with a most business-like rush yesterday. \n Two boats came into San Diego yesterday with load of lobsters, and the three concerns now operating below the line expect to average five tons each per week once they get into action. This will mean fifteen or more tons of lobsters coming into market in place of less than five, and local dealers believe the price cannot be longer monopolized by anyone so there is a chance hereafter of the general public getting a taste of this delicacy which heretofore has been the special prerogative of the rich. \n Sunday night Deputy Pritchard took two inspectors, to San Diego, who are commissioned to go south with the boats of the importing concerns, who hold permits from the Carranza government. These officers are paid by the importers as required by law, and their duties are to certify that the provisions of the California law governing lobster importation are respected. A third inspector has been asked for by the 10th of April. \n In addition to granting permits overriding the concessionaires operating for the de facto government of Lower California, President Caranza has taken the California fish and game laws for his guidance in conserving the West Mexican fisheries, and has appointed his own inspectors to accompany the boats of his concessionaires to see that they take no young fish, use no trawlnets or other gear destructive of the future supply. Carranza has taken the ground that much of the ablest fisheries thought and study available has been spent on the California fisheries conservation problem, which is very similar, and instead of merely patterning after it, has adopted it in toto. \n Work of the concessionaires under this new competitive arrangement fostered by the simplifying of importation under rulings of the Fish and Game Commission, will extend to the fresh-fish trade as rapidly as they can get organized for it. Owing to the distances to be traveled, fast boats with refrigeration are essential to bring up the halibut, which so far have been caught mostly by San Pedro and San Diego fishermen on a 2 cents ??? [scanned newspaper is illegible] ???  … cents more has been added, so the Los Angeles trade have had to pay an average of 8 cents, and the freight. By the new arrangement the Carranza concessionaires will combine several of these profits, and prices to the public can be materially reduced. The supply is said to be greater than California ever had, and the feeding banks are better. \n The territory is divided among the Carranza concessionaires, and the floating cannery John C. Heston is now in Magdalena Bay working on turtle and oversize lobsters until the albacore began to run, when she will go to putting up tuna. Lobsters of legal size will be inspected, boiled and shipped to California in cold storage, as the distance is too great to bring them in alive: but live shipments from closer points are now being made. \n The former controllers of the Lower California fisheries had an eighty-foot yacht bought presumably for patrol purposes, but she is tied up in San Diego under the Federal order denying clearance to any transferred vessel for foreign ports because of international complications. The former patrol boat Ensenada is tied up without a crew since the memorable “battle of the halibut banks” wherein some poachers decoved her close aboard then rammed her, chasing her ashore. \n This has been a week of much action and big fines in field patrol circles, trout “sooners,” six of them, falling victims to Deputy James II. Gvger on San Jacinto reservoir for snagging “spawners”. They had fifteen fish in their car weighing forty-five pounds and a Riverside justice imposed $25 fine on each, or $150 for the day’s fishing. \n At the same time, Deputy Tipton Mathews served a warrant on a last year’s quail case, wherein two men now in Taft were arrested for exceeding the quail limit in Monterey county and placed under $100 bond each. \n Late advices from San Diego are that three boats have come in with lobsters from Lower California this week and another is due the 4th of April. Besides lobsters, these boats brought in seventy turtles from 100 to 200 pounds each, which will swell at 8 cents a pound in Los Angeles. This is not as cheap as it seems owing to the great waste; only about one-third is usable, but the meat is rich and delicious.""","2","""MEXICAN FISH COME IN FAST""","70","green","inferred","inferred","200","not_reported","not_reported","""location is a little vague but likely magdalena bay""","1917",7,"high",NA,70,90.72,1910,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
+"142","sdunion_19171106","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1917-11-06,7,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19171106.2.109&srpos=93&e=-------en--20-SDDU-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","""Six Galapagos island turtles that strayed from the equator north of the thirtieth parallel, and several hundred speckled lobsters that will be used to temporarily reduce the high cost of living, (perhaps) arrived here from the lower coast yesterday on the auxiliary schooners Alenaire and Superior. \n The lobsters were fine looking specimens. The turtles were delivered to local cafes that make a specialty of turtle steaks and turtle soups. They weighed a trifle over two tons, according to the skipper of the Superior. Eliminating the teeth and tang of the sea from the total weigh, the six Galapagos Island ""horses"" approximated 2700 pounds.""","2","""Turtles, Lobsters Galore Arrive For Local Cafes""","6","green","inferred","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""exact location not given, but likely in baja california, since it specifies that they strayed north of the 30th parallel and that they came from the 'lower coast'""","1917",7,"high",NA,6,NA,1910,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
+"143","laherald_19020802","""Los Angeles Herald""",1902-08-02,8,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19020802.2.178&srpos=4&e=-------en--20-LAH-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy-------""","""An immense turtle arrived in this city yesterday over the Santa Fe from San Diego. It was captured off the coast of Lower California by the crew of a tramp steamer.\n The turtle was purchased by the Zalser Produce company of this city, and when the bill of lading was made out in San Diego, the turtle weighed 325 pounds. It barely tipped the scales at 310 pounds yesterday afternoon, the loss being caused by the hot weather and absence of the several buckets of salt water with which it was doused many times a day on ship board. \n Nevertheless his turtleship was able to crawl around at a lively rate when disturbed. When the turtle ceases to exist genuine turtle soup will be obtainable at several of the downtown restaurants.""","2","""GENUINE TURTLE SOUP TO BE A REALITY""","1","green","inferred","inferred","325","not_reported","not_reported","""the location was not further specified beyond the 'lower california', so it is assumed to be baja; includes illustration of the turtle""","1902",7,"high",NA,1,147.42,1900,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
+"144","cejournal_19650422","""Coronado Eagle and Journal""",1965-04-22,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=CJ19650422.2.31&srpos=6&e=--1846---1973--en--20-CJ-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""The giant sea turtle named “Malintzin” cried without words, crying so that Carlos Boccaccio couldn't eat one bite of her. \n Carlos used to own a restaurant in Tijuana, where sea turtles are a standard delicacy. This particular “Tartatuga” weighed 300 pounds, and was about to be put to her death, when large tears formed and dropped from her eyes. \n Carlos, the gourmet, saw this; saw this creature without words making her final protest to something she didn’t understand, and he never tasted her fine meat. \n Other turtles, yes. \n Both before Malintzin and since. \n Even we have savored turtle soup that Carlos has made, and hope some day to taste turtle liver saute, turtle steak, salad, scaloppini, and all the other dishes the animal is noted for, even vitamin oil. \n “Malintzin” carries the Aztec name of the princess who married Cortez. She was caught by a Mexican fisherman in warm Baja waters and sold to Carlos. She, and all others like her, was kept alive with wet sacks covering her reptile back. And like all others, she preferred Baja waters because of its warmth and also because she was isolated there. \n She was ashore digging a hole and laying her eggs when she was captured. Her eggs weren’t disturbed, so her babies would be able to take to the sea ten weeks later when they learn to swim well, 25 miles an hour like their mother could. \n They will probably hear about their 225 variations of relatives, all the way from the tiny turtle in the pet store to the giant tortoise. \n And they too will probably forget all words of caution and land ashore on a warm, isolated beach to lay their eggs. \n Perhaps fishermen will be there again to flip them onto their backs so they cannot get away and take them to market. \n Then again there will be more steaks, soups, sautes and vitamin oil.""","2","""The turtle cries with big tears""","1","green","inferred","known","300","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1965",8,"high",NA,1,136.08,1960,"baja_california","na","baja_california","mexico",30.3593768,-115.5309449
+"145","laherald_18880427","""Los Angeles Herald""",1888-04-27,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH18880427.2.19&srpos=92&e=-------en--20-LAH-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""On Wednesday evening Miss Mary McGarvin, while strolling on the beach at Alamitos, discovered the body of a huge turtle, which had been washed ashore. A great crowd went to the beach yesterday from Long Beach to view the monster. It measures nine feet wide from tip of fin to tip of fin across, and the body is seven feet long. It is estimated to weigh one thousand pounds. It is peculiar in appearance, and although of the turtle species, yet it is not identical with the turtle with which we are familiar. The shell is soft and is covered by a dark skin. It is doubtless an estray from the tropics.""","2","""An Immense Turtle""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","known","1000","7'","""washed ashore""","""More generally, the location could be labelled as near Long Beach; the species is identified as a possible leatherback given the size, the description of the soft, dark shell, and the report that it is not a familiar species. Further details on the size are that it was an estimated nine feet wide.""","1888",9,"high",213.36,1,453.59,1880,"alamitos","los_angeles","california","usa",33.7666973,-118.1741112
