-"","clip_id","newspaper_name","fulldate","p_number","secondary_p_number","url","text","narrative_detail","headline","num_turtles","species","spp_id","location","location_certainty","weight_lb","length_ft_inches","habitat","notes","year","data_quality_index","data_quality_cat","length_cm","decade"
-"1","latimes_19190306","""Los Angeles Times""",1919-03-06,7,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160705637/2796BCF9F7524B0FPQ/39?accountid=14026""","“Inscription Cut in Shell,' “E. J. W., El Dorado, 1858,” Creates Interest. \n [LOCAL CORRESPONDENCE] \n VENICE, March 6. — An aged sea turtle was caught off the Venice breakwater today. On its back was the following inscription: ""E.J.W., El Dorado, 1858.” \n The lettering was etched deep into the tortoise shell and must have been done many years ago. What the name “El Dorado” signified can be left to anyone’s imagination. It may be the name of some ship that sailed the southern seas in the days of the ‘49‘ers, or it might mean merely a name carved by some sailor without any special purpose. \n The shell was badly damaged in getting the turtle ashore. so after some vain speculation by the fishermen, the creature was tossed back into the ocean.”","1","""AGED SEA TURTLE HAULED ASHORE""","1","green","inferred","venice","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1919",7,"high",NA,1910
-"2","sppilot_19171210","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1917-12-10,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19171210.2.11&srpos=15&e=-------en--20-SPNP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""W. W. Beach, of 437 North Mesa street, has been experimenting for some years in canning various kinds of crustaceans and other sea animal life, and has now hit upon what he thinks will be one of the most highly priced delicacies every placed on the market. The product is called “turtle stock,” and consists of Calipash, Calipee and other edible parts of the green turtle, put up In a concentrated form for the making of turtle soup. \n Enormous numbers of these green turtles have been brought up from Magdalena and Turtle bays for years past and sold alive in the Los Angeles and San Francisco markets. This is probably the first successful method of canning the turtle so that it can be placed on the market in a practicable and preserved form. \n The formula states that the soup should contain besides the one pound can of turtle stock, one pound of carrots, one pound of ham, 1/2 pound of veal, 1/2 pound of onions, 2 eggs, hard-boiled, 1/2 lemon, 2 pounds of tomatoes, strained, 1 large glass of port, sherry or maideria wine, 1 large tablespoonful of beef drippings, 1 tablespoonful of Cury. This will make one gallon of the most delicious turtle soup, enough for sixteen people, which will keep for several days, if reheated once in forty-eight hours. \n Mr. Beach has arranged to have the turtles brought here in local boats and the product will be put up by the White Star Canning Co., at East San Pedro. It is thought that the possibilities of the industry are large and that one more form of food has been wrested from the mysteries of the great Pacific ocean, lying practically unexplored and teeming with many forms of life which may yet go far toward solving the problem of supplying the ever increasing demand for something new in food stuffs.""","2","""WILL PACK TURTLES AT WHITE STAR""","cargo","green","reported","turtle_bay, magdalena_bay","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""This is connected to sppilot_19190319. This is not a reporting of a specific cargo shipment/capture, nor is it for one particular time. It merely claims that there were consistent shipments of these turtles over the years to fuel the canning industry. Additionally, it cites both magdalena and turtle bays as locations, hence the two labels for location""","1917",8,"high",NA,1910
-"3","sppilot_19190319","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1919-03-19,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19190319.2.4&srpos=33&e=-------en--20-SPNP-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""W. W. Beach, now of San Diego, was here today renewing old acquaintances. For the past year he has been connected with the Neptune Sea Food Co. in the canning of turtles. The meat is put up in various forms, including turtle soup, turtle broth and escalloped green turtle. \n The amount of meat handled amounted from one and one-half to two tons per day, some of the turtles weighing over 500 pounds each. Mr. Beach states that the product finds a ready sale in the market and that another company will be organized soon to carry on the business on a still larger scale. \n Mr. Beach lived for a number of years and at one time was engaged in the canning of lobsters at Terminal.""","2","""W. W. Beach Canning Turtles at San Diego""","cargo","green","reported","turtle_bay, magdalena_bay","inferred","500","not_reported","not_reported","""This is connected to article sppilot_19171210. Neither of them are about individual turtle observations/sightings, but both indicate the abundance and frequent capture of turtle cargo that is being shipped—specifically for W.W. Beach— for canning in the LA area. Weight given is a general maximum they give, but it also says half a ton to 2 tons per day are shipped. Additionally, no specific location for capture but given that it is being shipped to the same person as sppilot_19171210 we can likely assume baja_california and/or turtle bay/magdalena bay.""","1919",7,"high",NA,1910
-"4","sdunion_19170423","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1917-04-23,5,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19170423.2.77&srpos=55&e=-------en--20-SDDU-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","""Seventy-Four Turtles Thrown Overboard to Lighten Craft Awaiting Aid.  \n Laden with a cargo of seventy-five turtles, the fishing schooner Apex arrived in port yesterday in tow of the fishing launch Beaver. \n The Apex left Turtle bay with a cargo of 150 turtles, but was compelled to heave seventy-four overboard at a point about 130 miles south of San Diego when the propeller dropped off and the craft became unmanageable in the high seas. \n A jury rig enabled the Apex to maintain steerageway until sighted and picked up by the Beaver. Some of the turtles which were thrown back Into the sea weighed, according to the skipper of the Apex, nearly 300 pounds. \n The cargo of the Apex was the largest single shipment of turtles that has arrived here from the lower coast in many months. Chicago, San Francisco and Los Angeles cafes, which make a specialty of turtle steaks and turtle soup, will obtain the greater part of the shipment. \n Receipts of lumber by sea yesterday measured nearly 1,000,000 feet. The steam schooner Washington, after discharging her lumber cargo, loaded 400 tons of citrus powder for delivery at San Francisco and Seattle.""","2","""FISHING SCHOONER TOWED INTO PORT""","150","green","inferred","turtle_bay","known","300","not_reported","not_reported","""the weight is what was given in the article as the weight for 'some of the turtles', but not necessarily the average or exact value""","1917",8,"high",NA,1910
