-"8","spdaily_19170608","""San Pedro Daily News""",1917-06-08,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPDN19170608.2.14&srpos=54&e=-------en--20-SPDN-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""In the warehouse of the Avalon Fish company is a large turtle weighing 160 pounds. It was captured at Turtle Bay, two hundred miles down the coast from San Diego, from which place it was brought to the southern city, whence it was shipped by rail to San Pedro.""","2","""West Shore Notes""","1","green","inferred","turtle_bay","known","160","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1917",8,"high",NA,1910
-"9","sppilot_19290122","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1929-01-22,13,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19290122.2.197&srpos=71&e=-------en--20-SPNP-61--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""The 102-foot yawl rigged yacht Talayha, owned by Lazard Lippman, San Pedro merchant, was back in the harbor yesterday, following a successful two weeks’ cruise down the Mexican coast. On the trip Lippman and his party of friends spent the time fishing and hunting. They secured several deer in the mountains near San Quentin Bay, Lower California. Off Cedros Island the party took red abalones for dinner and at Turtle Bay they captured a 300-pound green sea turtle.""","2","""Lippman’s Yacht Talahya Returns From Long Cruise""","1","green","reported","turtle_bay","known","300","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1929",9,"high",NA,1920
-"10","sppilot_19290905","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1929-09-05,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19290905.2.15&srpos=1&e=-------en--20-SPNP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""TURTLE BAY TURTLE DISAPPEARS Speaking of Turtle bay, the shelled animal from which it gained its name because of the great numbers of turtles there in the early days, is now to be found there but rarely. The advancing front line of civilization and appetites for soup have driven the turtle to his last stronghold, farther down the Mexican coast. San Pedro fishermen cruising down the coast occasionally capture one of the large creatures. One such turtle brought back to the municipal fish wharf alive some time ago had laid a peck of eggs on the trip. Capture of one of the turtles is always an occasion for celebration by the fishermen. The heavy fishing in Turtle bay is believed to have caused the animals to leave.""","1","""FISH PACKERS MARKING TIME""","0","green","inferred","turtle_bay","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""While this article mentions the capture of one turtle, it is predominantly about the ABSENCE of many turtles in Turtle bay as they once were. The single turtle captured was a marked rarity. This demonstrates a change in the cargo-yielding capacity of Baja California.""","1929",7,"high",NA,1920
-"11","sdunion_19071029","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1907-10-29,14,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19071029.2.147&srpos=71&e=-------en--20-SDDU-61--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------"" ","“W. J. Forbes Hooks Huge Turtle Which Carries Boat to Sea; Capsizes \n Word has been received from Lower California that William J. Forbes, prominent in the affairs of Wall street while thousands of miles removed from the financial disturbance of the eastern metropolis, had a close call the other day from drowning in the waters of Todos Santos bay, thirty or forty miles south of Ensenada. \n Forbes, who has a number of warm friends in San Diego, is the guest of Walter Nordhoff, who owns a splendid country home on the shores of the bay, and who is famous as an entertainer. The Wall street financier was out fishing from a small boat, accompanied by a boatman, when a huge turtle was hooked. Forbes was unable to land the fish, and within a short time they were off for a cruise, the destination of which they knew not. It was worse than sailing under sealed orders in that there was no certainty that the destination would ever be known. \n However, after the turtle had towed them for an hour or two the men in the boat, becoming exasperated, resolved to either land the fish or overturn the boat in the attempt. The effort resulted in the capsizing of the craft. \n In the advices received here nothing is said about the boatman, and it is supposed that he was more fortunate than Forbes, who was in the water nearly two hours before the rescue. \n Local friends are awaiting the full details of the accident.”","2","""NEW YORK BANKER NEARLY DROWNS""","1","green","inferred","todos_santos_bay","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""seems like it could be a different species other than green, even though we assumed that, because green seem a little bit too small too tow a boat for that long, this also appears to be the same turtle event as laherald_19071030 and sbmp_19071030""","1907",8,"high",NA,1900
-"12","laherald_19071030","""Los Angeles Herald""",1907-10-30,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19071030.2.12&srpos=31&e=-------en--20-LAH-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""William J. Forbes' Boat Upset Near Ensenada and He Struggles for Two Hours In the Water \n By Associated Press. \n SAN DIEGO. Oct. 29.—Word has just been received from Ensenada of the narrow escape from death of William J. Forbes, prominent in the affairs of Wall street, who is a guest of Walter Nordhoff, at his home at Puenta Banda. Mr. Forbes was fishing on the bay in a small boat, accompanied by a boatman, when their line was taken by a sea turtle. The line held and the turtle towed the boat for some distance. \n The fishermen determined to land the turtle if possible, and in their efforts they overturned the boat. The turtle got away with the tackle and the fishermen were in the water for two hours before they were rescued.""","2","""BIG TURTLE ENDANGERS LIFE OF CAPITALIST""","1","green","inferred","todos_santos_bay","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""this appears to be the same event as sdunion_19071029, from the SD union, and sbmp_19071030. location specifically given as Puenta Banda but assumed to be todos santos bay because of connection to sdunion article""","1907",8,"high",NA,1900
-"13","stinson_sdherald_18870902","""San Diego Herald""",1887-09-02,NA,"53","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""A 600 pound turtle will form a novel feature of the St. Louis Grand Army Encampment. It will be in the San Diego exhibit, having been caught in the bay of Ensenada de Todos Santos, in Lower California.""","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","1","green","inferred","todos_santos_bay","known","600","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1887",7,"high",NA,1880
-"14","sbmp_18871014","""Morning Press""",1887-10-14,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP18871014.2.3&srpos=5&e=-------en--20-MP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","“Sights for the Curious at the Santa Cruz Fair. \n Santa Cruz, Oct. 13. —The second day of the County Fair has proved to be a success. The display of live stock is good and many fine thoroughbreds are on exhibition. An Italian fisherman at Soquel yesterday caught in his net a monster turtle which measured eight feet in length and over seven feet in width. It weighs about one thousand pounds. It is now on exhibition at the Fair. It is said to be the largest turtle ever caught on this coast.”","1","""A MONSTROUS TURTLE""","1","leatherback","inferred","soquel","known","1000","8'","not_reported","""possible leatherback based on size, including 7 feet in width""","1887",7,"high",243.84,1880
-"15","latimes_18871014","""Los Angeles Times""",1887-10-14,5,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/163445030/D4971430CE2047A5PQ/285?accountid=14026""","“Santa Cruz, Oct. 13.—An Italian fisherman at Soquel yesterday caught in his net a monster turtle, which measures eight feet in length and over seven in width. It weighs about 1000 pounds. It is now on exhibition at the fair. It is said to be the largest turtle ever caught on the Coast.”","1","""Something of a Turtle.""","1","leatherback","inferred","soquel","known","1000","8'","""caught in net""","""width is 7' Species unclear--could be either leatherback based on size, or green based on inference. Says it is largest turtle ever caught on the coast.""","1887",8,"high",243.84,1880
-"16","stinson_sdherald_18710615","""San Diego Herald""",1871-06-15,NA,"51","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""A firm in this city is in receipt of a letter from a large house in San Francisco making inquiries in relation to the possibility of receiving a steady supply of turtle through this port. The San Diego firm has replied offering to contract to supply any stated quantity, from one up to ten thousand pounds, by each steamer, or even more if desired. Turtles are found in abundance at Turtle Bay... or at Scammon's Lagoon... and can be supplied to the San Francisco dealers very cheaply.""","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","cargo","green","inferred","scammons_lagoon, turtle_bay","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""no precise number given, just says that they are found 'in abundance', and seemingly enough to support a fishery/industry.""","1871",6,"medium",NA,1870
-"17","sdunion_19190525","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-05-25,10,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190525.2.115&srpos=3&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","“Turtles on the way to San Diego to be transformed into turtle soup, turtle meat and turtle oil. Top picture shows one section of confining pen at Scammon’s lagoon, with a number of the prisoners; middle, schooner Catarina, which brings the turtles here, aground on the shore of the lagoon. Below, loading the turtles on the Catarina. \n Fishing for turtles is the interesting work of George Naylor and his brother William, sons of E. H. Naylor, 1904 Atlantic street, and Richard Johnson, all of this city. While interesting, the pursuit of turtles from 100 to 400 pounds is the hardest kind of work, as well as being extremely dangerous. \n The Naylor boys and Johnson are under contract with the Lower California Canning company of National City to supply turtles, and they make their catches In Scammon’s lagoon, 400 miles south of San Diego. \n Methods used in turtling differ, but the local boys use a net strung over about 1200 feet of water, the lower part anchored to the bottom and the other on the surface, buoyed by corks. This net is strung across the channel used by the turtles in going to and from their feeding grounds in shallow water. The turtles are approached while feeding and are frightened towards the net, where they become entangled In the meshes, which are about a foot in width. \n Wherever a turtle becomes caught, the corks on the surface are pulled under, and the turtles, in rowboats, pull in the net and land the turtle in the boat. Considerable skill is necessary to accomplish this part of the work, for in many cases the turtles are much heavier than the man in the boat. \n From the rowboats the catch is transferred to a motorcraft which lands the turtles on shore. A specially constructed pen, capable of confining hundreds, next receives them, and there they remain until the schooner Catarina arrives. \n The Catarina is chartered by the turtles and makes from two to three round trips a month between San Diego and Scammon's lagoon. Upon the Catarina's arrival at the lagoon the turtles are brought from the pen to the motor boat and thence to the school. Great care is necessary during the transfer, and, despite all precautions, many of the turtles escape. \n Two of the three partners remain on the turling grounds all the time, one of them making the semi-monthly journey on the Catarina. On arrival of the catch here the work of canning the meat and soup and extracting oil from the shell and fat of the turtles begins. The size of the Catarina’s cargo varies from 15 to 30 tons, and, while the craft is away on the trip to the cannery, the work of refilling the confining pens at Scammon's lagoon goes on unceasingly. \n The turtle canning Industry is a comparatively young one here, as the Naylor boys and Johnson started only three months ago. shortly after their return from the army. The investment of the partners amounts to more than $10,000. The cannery also represents a considerable investment.”","2","""Turtle Fishing in Mexican Waters for Cannery Is Work of 3 Former Soldiers""","cargo","green","inferred","scammons_lagoon","known","400","not_reported","not_reported","""the reported weight was 100 to 400 lbs, here we reported the maximum. Further estimates of weight and total cargo were given that could be used to calculate an estimate of num_turtles, but none given""","1919",7,"high",NA,1910
-"18","sdunion_19190402","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-04-02,9,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190402.2.119&srpos=6&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","“Steaks and Soup Put Up by Packing Concern; Supplied By Three Boats. \n San Diego has a new industry. It is the canning of green turtle steak and turtle soup. The plant is located near the foot of Thirty-second street in the buildings formerly occupied by the Lower California Chemical company for the manufacture of aniline dyes. \n The National City Commercial company, headed by J. K. Blackman. has taken over the Lower California Chemical company’s property and converted it into a turtle canning plant. A number of retorts for cooking turtles have been installed. Apparatus for making 800 gallons of turtle soup at a time also has been placed in the plant. \n The turtle cannery is supplied by a fleet of three boats. Two ocean-going launches are employed in capturing turtles in Scammell’s lagoon, just south of Ensenada. The power schooner Catarina brings the turtles to San Diego, making three voyages a month. The Catarina arrived from the south last week with 45 tons of live turtles and is now at Scamell’s lagoon loading another cargo. \n The products of the turtle cannery will be distributed throughout the United Slates.”","2","""TURTLE CANNING PLANT IS CITY’S NEWEST INDUSTRY""","cargo","green","reported","scammons_lagoon","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's, National City Commercial company, general estimates of weight/cargo were given to calculate. Perhaps talking about the same hunting trips as sdunion_19190422 but unsure""","1919",8,"high",NA,1910
-"19","sdunion_19190305","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-03-05,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190305.2.35&srpos=101&e=-------en--20-SDDU-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","“SAN DIEGO soon will have the novel distinction of being the world's first turtle-oil exporting port. \n According to word received here yesterday from the lower coast, a gang of Mexicans is employed in Scammell’s lagoon killing turtles from which an excellent grade of soap oil is obtained. \n The lagoon is said to be fairly alive with turtles ranging from 75 to 200 pounds in weight. At one period recently the Mexicans had turtles piled up like cordwood waiting to be cut up and rendered for the oil. \n The first shipment of turtle oil is expected here from Ensenada this month. The oil sells wholesale at $1 a gallon. \n Shark oil also is imported here from the Lower California coast. This oil is utilized in the manufacture of cosmetics.”","1","""Turtle Oil New Import From Lower California to San Diego""","cargo","green","inferred","scammons_lagoon","known","200","not_reported","not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's""","1919",6,"medium",NA,1910
-"20","sdunion_19190505","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-05-05,6,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190505.2.87&srpos=1&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""Loaded with 260 turtles, the power schooner Catarina arrived from Scammell’s lagoon, Lower California, late Saturday. \n The turtles are consigned to the turtle packing plant of the National City Commercial company at the upper end of the bay. \n It Is estimated that Catarina's cargo will make about 5000 gallons of turtle soup and fully 1000 cases of turtle meat. \n The local turtle soup and turtle meat packing plant is the only one of Its kind on the Pacific coast. Since the plant opened several weeks ago it has handled nearly 1000 turtles.""","2","""TURTLE CARGO ARRIVES FOR PACKING COMPANY""","260","green","inferred","scammons_lagoon","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's""","1919",8,"high",NA,1910
-"21","sdunion_19191013","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-10-13,7,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19191013.2.94&srpos=12&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------"" ","""Two hundred and fifty turtles formed the cargo of the power schooner Catarina arriving from Scammon's lagoon yesterday morning. The turtles were consigned to the turtle soup factory at the upper end of the bay. \n The purse seiner St. Nicholas, bound from San Pedro for the lower coast, put into port yesterday with a leaking gasoline tank. A quick, reply from the S. O. S. call for plumbers enabled the St. Nicholas to leave for the fishing banks at dusk.""","2","""TURTLE CARGO HERE FOR SOUP FACTORY""","250","green","inferred","scammons_lagoon","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1919",8,"high",NA,1910
-"22","stinson_altaca_18710210","""Alta California""",1871-02-10,NA,"51","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""San Diego, February 10th. —Arrived, schooner Cygnet, from Scammon's Lagoon, with 100 turtle; forty of them will be shipped direct to Chicago.""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","100","green","inferred","scammons_lagoon","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1871",8,"high",NA,1870
-"23","sdunion_19190422","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1919-04-22,5,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19190422.2.50&srpos=2&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""National City Concern Packs 20 Tons of Meat and Hundreds of Cases Daily. \n The turtle soup canning plant of the National City Commercial company at the upper end of the bay is packing 20 tons of turtle meat and several hundred cases of turtle soup daily, according to members of the sea-products packing concern. \n The ocean-going launch Lion entered port from Scammell's lagoon yesterday with 96 turtles. The auxiliary schooner Catarina will sail today for the Lower California coast for another turtle cargo. \n During the last month the Catarina and other craft have brought more than 270 turtles here to be cooked and pecked at the National City plant. \n San Diego packed turtle steak and soups is in great demand among the fashionable cafes and restaurants of the Pacific coast.""","2","""BUSINESS BRISK AT TURTLE PLANT""","96","green","inferred","scammons_lagoon","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Scammell's = Scammon's, National City Commercial company""","1919",8,"high",NA,1910
-"24","sbweekly_18770721","""Santa Barbara Weekly Press""",1877-07-21,8,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SBWP18770721.2.105&srpos=1&e=-------en--20-SBWP-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""A 700-pounder. \n This morning, as the U. S. Coast Survey steamer McArthur was steaming from her late anchorage in Beecher’s Bay, Santa Rosa Island, toward the coast, a large turtle was descried just astern of the ship, lying in the water breast upwards. It was apparently disabled, as was then supposed, by the ship’s propeller. Those who were below decks were surprised by the sudden order of “hard-a-port!” and as they rushed on deck, to inquire why, were soon shown the immense specimen swimming off, showing its head every now and then as it came to the surface to breathe. The ship was heeded directly for it, and the dingy made ready to lower, one of the men, who professed to be a great turtle-catcher, in the bows ready for the attack upon the monster. The ship having approached to within a short distance of the object of its travels, the headway was checked and the boat was lowered. A few strokes with the oars brought the dingy to close range, and when the turtle came up to breathe, over went the man, landing astride the turtle's back. No doubt the reptile was surprised, for its surprise was evinced by its diving under the boat, carrying with it its unwelcome rider. It was a question whether to sink or to dismount, so our friend chose the latter alternative, coming to the surface blowing like a porpoise, while the turtle made off with its utmost speed. The boat followed in its wake, which could easily be distinguished by the broken water. A second dash was made, and again a third, but without avail. Meanwhile, whenever the opportunity offered, rifle shots were fired from the ship, all missing the mark. The chase became very exciting, especially as upon the fourth attempt the turtle showed signs of fatigue, diving under the boats without attempting to make off. Finally the ship approached and as the turtle rose to the surface to breathe, a rifle shot from the forecastle ended its career. The boat then ran alongside the turtle, and making a line fast to its flippers, towed it to the ship. The task of preparing it to hoist on board was no small one, as the turtle was immense, and not being quite dead was continually opening and closing its huge jaws. Eventually it was hoisted on board by the fall of one of the boats, and laid on its back on deck. It was the largest specimen ever seen by any one on board, weighing probably in the neighborhood of seven hundred pounds. One of the rear flippers was gone, although the accident had not been of late occurrence. This probably accounts for its inability to make better speed through the water. It was a female of the hawk-bill species. It had evidently been stunned by contact with the ship or the ship's propeller, as the monster’s head was bruised. The measurements taken were as follows: From the tip of the nose to that of the tail 6 feet 6 inches; from the tip of one fore flipper to that of the other 8 feet; around the breast 7 feet 10 inches. The McArthur came into port this afternoon with her immense prize.""","2","""A MONSTER TURTLE""","1","leatherback","reported","santa_rosa_island","known","700","6'6""","not_reported","""the location could also be categorized more broadly as 'channel_islands; other measurements included were: 'from the tip of one fore flipper to that of the other 8 feet; around the breast 7 feet 10 inches', and the reported weight is said to be an estimate ('in the neighborhood'. Species could be mis-identified as a green""","1877",9,"high",198.12,1870
-"25","latimes_19040807","""Los Angeles Times""",1904-08-07,11,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164260036/C8D12580C68F44A0PQ/3?accountid=14026""","""James Kennedy of Sawtelle captured the carcass of a rare turtle in the surf near the long wharf today. It weighs 400 pounds and is at least seven feet long and four feet across, with peculiar ridge lengthwise across its back""","2","""RARE TURTLE""","1","leatehrback","inferred","santa_monica","known","400","7'","""carcass in the surf""","""Leatherback inferred based on size, and description of ridge on back. Additional size measurements include a 4-foot width""","1904",9,"high",213.36,1900
-"26","latimes_19151105","""Los Angeles Times""",1915-11-05,10,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160160767/590BBC08B3D34453PQ/128?accountid=14026""","“SANTA MONICA, Nov. —A 200-pound turtle, the largest brought to the long wharf in two years, was part of the catch of S. Kutonoff, a fisherman in the Japanese village, who brought it here today in a boat. It was caught in a net near Santa Cruz Islands.”","2","""CATCH BIG TURTLE""","1","green","inferred","santa_cruz_islands","known","200","not_reported","""caught in net""","""caught near santa cruz islands, but brought into the santa monica wharf""","1915",9,"high",NA,1910
-"27","latimes_19030121","""Los Angeles Times""",1903-01-21,7,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164143422/C8D12580C68F44A0PQ/14?accountid=14026""","""Avalon, Jan. 20.—A very unusual catch was made this morning. Mr. and Mrs. F. J. Young of San Francisco, who were out fishing with R. S. Compton, spied two objects on the water near them, which proved to be sea turtles. Mr. Compton steered his launch quietly up along side of them and thrust a gaff hook into one and landed it in the boat. The other took alarm and escaped. The one captured was brought in and placed in the aquarium, where it is keeping company with “Path,” the big sheepshead, which has heretofore had a big tank to himself. The turtle is estimated to weigh forty pounds, and is two feet in length in its normal state, but when “rubbering” adds about six inches to the length of its neck. \n Island Pick-Ups. \n Thomas McD. Potter, who last summer proved himself the most indefatigable, as well as the most successful angler that ever struck Catalina has had a boat built for his special use, and is now here enjoying himself. The boast is of the usual fishing type, a little more roomy than most of them, equipped with a five-horse-power Union engine. Mr. Potter was out this morning and caught eight sheepshead and a tom cod, the latter a very rare fish about Catalina. The new boat was christened the “Alleen.” \n H. B. Hurd of Chicago had one of the largest catches of albicore yesterday that has been made here for some time. It numbered eighteen , and included a record fish, a huge fellow weighting forty-five pounds, with side fins like wings. Mr. Hurd was out with George Johnson, in the Kathryn. \n Gilmour Sharp has bought the Spray from Capt. McDonald, and will use it as a private fishing and pleasure boat. \n Hon. A. T. Gray and wife and Miss Elizabeth M. Gray of Vermont, Mrs. Brainerd Avery of New York City, Mrs. Ethel Gray Buxton and her two daughters, Misses Mae and Corinne, form a party who have been at Hotel Metropole for a few days. \n Superintendent R. H. Ingram of the Southern Pacific with his wife and daughter, and Mrs. W. D. Woolwine and daughter of Los Angeles, were among the arrivals at the Metropole today. \n W. T. Green and wife of Salt Lake, frequent visitors to Catalina, are again at the Grand View.""","2","""Boatman Lands Sea Turtle""","2","green","inferred","santa_catalina_island","inferred","40","2'","not_reported",NA,"1903",7,"high",60.96,1900
-"28","latimes_19001211","""Los Angeles Times""",1900-12-11,7,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/163972934/441C656BF472400FPQ/328?accountid=14026""","“A sea turtle weighing about seventy-five pounds was captured at Cherry Valley last week by Richard Markel, who is in charge of the pumping station for the new town at the Isthmus at Howlands. Markel was returning from the Isthmus in a row boat when he observed something creating a commotion in a bed of kelp. which he soon ascertained to be a turtle, one of whose flippers had become entangled in the kelp and held him a prisoner Markel had a shotgun and a charge in the head finished the animal. It contained more than 200 eggs. \n Cyrus Bellows of Humboldt, Iowa, Horace Bellows and Frank L Wingard of Long Beach this morning in a short time caught two skipjacks, a yellowtail and an albicore. \n N. Swenson entered one of his moonlight scenes in a photographic contest engineered by the Grit Publishing Company of Williamsport, Pa., and was second in the list of honorable mention. \n A. A Carraher, who has been at San Jacinto Hot Springs for three weeks, is home again. \n A. P. Sawyer of the Post-Intelligencer of Seattle, is enjoying a rest at Hotel Metropole. \n Dr. Mortimer Ayers of Pasadena and Ralph M. Weaver have been spending a few days on the island. \n Alfred Reed, wife and maid, Mrs. Mary B Reed and Masters Alfred, Nelson and Charles Reed of Pittsburgh are at the Metropole for a two weeks’ stay. \n Mr. and Mrs. Compton Lundle of Oceanside, golf devotees, are enjoying themselves on the Catalina links \n Mr. and Mrs. G. M. Hobbs, Chicago; James T. Grau and wife. Santa Rosa; John T. Dickson and Miss Belle Dixon, Houston, Tex, and Mr. and Mrs J. Fleming, Trinidad, Colo, were among the arrivals at Hotel Metropole today.”","2","""ISLAND BREVITIES""","1","green","inferred","santa_catalina_island","known","75","not_reported","""entangled in kelp""","""The location is given more specifically as Cherry Valley on Santa Catalina island""","1900",9,"high",NA,1900
-"29","laherald_19010825","""Los Angeles Herald""",1901-08-25,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19010825.2.58&srpos=167&e=-------en--20-LAH-161--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""(Special Correspondence.) \n SANTA BARBARA. Aug 24 —J. W. Nier of this city has classified the large sea turtle caught on Wednesday in this channel as a native of the Mediterranean sea. It is Spargis coriacia, and its being on this coast is the greatest of wonders. The thing is not edible. \n It was killed Thursday afternoon because it seemed to be dying from being so long out of water. It had been placed in a large tank house, where it was kept wet down, but in this it was apparently not happy, for with its large and powerful flippers it smashed out one of the sides of the house and got into the yard of the Larco fish market, where it was kept. It was then placed in a tank that was so small that it could not move in any way. Putting it in this cramped position was probably the cause of its death. \n How the turtle got into these waters would be an interesting study in ocean currents. It must have come from the Mediterranean. The skin and shell have been saved.""","2","""SANTA BARBARA'S BIG TURTLE COMES FROM FAR OFF WATERS""","1","leatherback","inferred","santa_barbara_channel","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""The article reports the species to be 'Spargis coriacia' which seems to be some kind of leatherback or similar based on googling. It also notes that Santa Barbara is an unusual place (for the time) to find a turtle of this species.""","1901",8,"high",NA,1900
-"30","sbweekly_19010822","""Santa Barbara Weekly Press""",1901-08-22,5,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SBWP19010822.2.42&srpos=3&e=-------en--20-SBWP-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""Measures Over Six Feet in Length and Will Weigh More Than Six Hundred Pounds. \n Landed on the Beach After a Terrible Struggle—Is Considered of Great Value—Will be Put on Exhibition—Exceptionally Good Eating. \n Sebastian Larco is in possession of a large turtle caught by his men yesterday afternoon, which is considered to be worth hundreds of dollars to a museum or institute. \n Every afternoon about 4:30 o’clock the fishermen in his employ pull in their nets, which are set in the channel about a mile and a half out. Yesterday, while they were returning, they noticed several large splashes resembling a whale in distress, located in the center of the kelp. They immediately turned their boat in that direction, and on arriving at the spot noticed that the cause of the disturbance was a monster turtle, caught in the kelp and unable to extricate itself. After considerable hard work a rope was fastened to it and an effort made to bring it into the boat. Finally the monster was dragged in,  but scarcely had it been landed before it struck one of the men with its wing, or fin, sending him headlong into the ocean, and then crawled back into the water. \n The fisherman succeeded in getting back into the boat again, and, regardless of his wet condition, set about to securely fasten the rope to their prize. A terrible struggle ensued, the monster of the ocean fighting viciously for freedom, and the two men working to prevent him from doing so. \n At last the work was accomplished, but it was considered better not to again attempt to land him in the boat. The rope was securely fastened and the turtle towed to the beach and landed. \n A wagon was called and their capture was safely landed at Larco’s residence. Then another struggle began. The turtle objected to being taken into the fish house, and three powerful men were unable to master him. They were thrown aside by the turtle, carried upon his back and occasionally thrown down. More assistance was needed, and not until six men with ropes attached to the monster’s body were they able to conquer him. \n Captain Larco was interviewed by a representative of The Press and his account of the capture was as follows: \n “The boys have secured a great prize. It is a pure green turtle, and of great value, not only as a curiosity, but its meat is of the finest quality. During all my experience of almost half a century, I never heard of but one other being captured on the Pacific coast. It was taken by government officials aboard the survey boat McCarty, near San Francisco, many years ago. I should judge that the one captured by the boys weighed about 1000 pounds. They do not as a rule enter shallow water, but live thousands of miles at sea. It was a great surprise to me when I saw it.” \n The exact weigth of the turtle is not known, yet it will weigh over 600 pounds. IT measures almost seven feet in length by four feet across the back, and from tip to tip of the front fins over twelve feet. \n Mr. Larco will exhibit it, beginning tomorrow. An effort will be made to have it put in the bath house, where thousands of people could see it. By throwing salt water over it daily, it can be kept alive indefinitely, and no doubt a large price will be offered for it by some museum. \n Captain Alexander of the Santa Rosa was at the dock when it was brought in, and will ascertain its value when he reaches San Francisco. \n Plano Larco and Pietro Marfolli were the fishermen who captured it.”","2","""MONSTER GREEN TURTLE CAUGHT BY FISHERMEN IN THE CHANNEL""","1","green","reported","santa_barbara_channel","known","1000","7'","""caught in the kelp""","""specific location not given, just says 'in the channel', which I assume to be the santa barbara channel. Further measurements given as: 'It measures almost seven feet in length by four feet across the back, and from tip to tip of the front fins over twelve feet.'""","1901",10,"high",213.36,1900
-"31","sbweekly_18770804","""Santa Barbara Weekly Press""",1877-08-04,8,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SBWP18770804.2.61&srpos=4&e=-------en--20-SBWP-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","“A deep-sea turtle of extraordinary size, weighing nearly 400 pounds, was exhibited in Newport, Rhode Island, on Thursday. It was caught in the vicinity. — [Boston Weekly Transcript. \n We can beat that. A turtle weighing nearly 1200 pounds was caught in the Santa Barbara channel week before last.”","1","""A Bigger One.""","1","green","inferred","santa_barbara_channel","known","1200","not_reported","not_reported","""possible other species given the size, however, no notes on species are given""","1877",7,"high",NA,1870
-"32","sbmp_19010925","""Morning Press""",1901-09-25,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP19010925.2.11&srpos=7&e=-------en--20-MP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------"" ","“Dr. L. G. Yates has Charge of the Interesting Work. \n THIRTY-FIVE FEET OF STITCHING \n Will be on Exhibition in Some up Town store in a Short Time—Monster Curiosity. \n Unless something happens mighty quick, the big turtle which was captured by Larco in the channel last month, will not know himself at the resurrection. For the past four days Dr. L. G. Yates has been engaged in mounting the monster. A representative of The Press called on the doctor yesterday. Aproned and bloused, he was stitching away, closing the last seam in the turtle’s hide. There is something like 35 feet of stitching on the monster’s body. \n In a few days the work will be completed, the eyes put in to give it a more lifelike appearance, and the former inhabitant of the briny deep will look complacently on the moving crowd from some up-town window. He has already been treated to a most remarkable diet, one that will last him for some time to come. Dr. Yates was just ramming home the last charge of dessert yesterday. It consisted of a generous stepmother supply of excelsior. Previous to that course he had been treated to a full meal of blocks, hay, straw, boards and several pieces of wagon-tire steel. There is nothing delicate now about the appetite of the turtle. \n The skin has yet to be treated with stains in order that it may retain its natural color. Then it will be varnished, and the head poised at the proper angle. The specimen will be one of the most interesting caught in the waters of the western coast.”","2","""LARCO'S TURTLE BEING STUFFED""","1","leatherback","inferred","santa_barbara_channel","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Perhaps given size could be a leatherback""","1901",7,"high",NA,1900
-"33","laherald_19050105","""Los Angeles Herald""",1905-01-05,12,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19050105.2.130&srpos=4&e=-------en--20-LAH-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------#""","“CAPTURED IN WATERS NEAR SANTA BARBARA \n Creature Measures Seven Feet From Head to Tall and Its Weight Is Eight Hundred Pounds \n Special to The Herald. \n SANTA BARBARA, Jan. 4.— Captain Gourley of the pleasure fleet and one of his assistants, Albert Stafford, captured a huge sea turtle yesterday afternoon which proved to be the largest specimen ever caught in these waters. When hauled onto the pleasure pier and carefully anchored down with stout ropes to prevent trouble it was estimated that the turtle weighed at least 800 pounds, and several competent judges placed its weight at considerably above this figure. \n The turtle was first discovered by Stafford, who came across it while rowing alone in a small skiff. The reptile was entangled in some kelp, and was asleep at the time Stafford sighted it. Not liking to venture too near in so small a boat, Stafford returned and was joined by Gourley. The pair went after the turtle in a sail boat and succeeded in gaffing it through one of the flippers, after which they managed to throw ropes over it and tow it to the pier. The turtle put up a hard fight all the way and several times attempted to clamber aboard the boat. It was finally hoisted to the pier, after a few exciting moments for those who were engaged in the work. \n The picture was taken within a few minutes of the time the big turtle was finally made fast to the pier. The turtle measured seven feet from head to tail.”","2","""MONSTER TURTLE TAKEN PRISONER""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","santa_barbara","inferred","800","7'","""entangled in some kelp""","""I thought it would be a leatherback based on the image and the descriptions of size, but unsure. Location just generally says 'near Santa Barbara""","1905",8,"high",213.36,1900
-"34","laherald_19010822","""Los Angeles Herald""",1901-08-22,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19010822.2.43&srpos=82&e=-------en--20-LAH-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","“(Special Dispatch to The Herald.) \n SANTA BARBARA, Aug. 21.—The largest and strongest turtle ever caught, an all around record breaker, was captured this afternoon after a long and terrible struggle in the channel here. The captor was the fiteen-year-old son of a fisherman named A. Larco. The trutle is a 700-pounder, and is of a species unknown to the host of Mexican coast and South seas sailors. \n Young Larco found the turtle caught in the kelp that fills the water for about one mile off this coast. The gigantic creature was thrashing about to get itself untangled. Larco tried to get it into his boat, but the thing fought viciously. It struck Pietro Marfolli, who was with Larco, with one of its flippers and knocked the man the whole length of the boat. He was bruised and stunned. \n Finally the lad lassoed the turtle and towed it ashore. It struggled all of the way and it took two hours to make the mile. On shore Captain Alexander of the steamer Santa Rosa and his sailors saw the creature but they said they had never seen its like. Down its back there run seven high ridges. It is very vicious and strikes at all who get near it. The weight and size are the astounding features as well as the fact that the turtle was caught by a boy. It will be exhibited here and may be sold to the coming circus.”","2","""GIGANTIC TURTLE IS CAPTURED OFF THE SANTA BARBARA COAST""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","santa_barbara","inferred","700","not_reported","""caught in the kelp""","""given the size and description (having seven high ridges), this is likely a leatherback, and therefore this identification would be inferred""","1901",8,"high",NA,1900
-"35","sbweekly_19010829","""Santa Barbara Weekly Press""",1901-08-29,6,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SBWP19010829.2.38&srpos=2&e=-------en--20-SBWP-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""The monster turtle that was captured by the Larco boys Wednesday afternoon died about 1 o’clock yesterday afternoon. It is supposed that It was Injured while being brought to the beach, and alto when being placed in the fish house. Many people were at the Larco residence during the morning to view the curiosity, and various scientists were searching for the proper species to which it belonged. There is some talk of having it stufl,ed. The idea is a good one, and were it carried out would make a valuable addition to the collection of curios now at the chamber of commerce. THE TURTLE. Dr. Yates contributes the following account of the ocean turtle, classifying the Santa Barbara find: The curious reptile known by the name of turtles, or tortoises, are remarkable for the peculiarity of being a vertebrate animals Wi.a their bones worn on the outside of the lody. They are divided into several general classes with different habits and characteristics. Some are confined to the land, others to the fresh water, and still others inhabit the ocean, which they rarely leave. . Some of these animals furnish the tortoise-shell of commerce. The kind most valued for this product is the ""hawkbill turtle,” which is found on portions of the west coast of Mexico; while the flesh of this turtle is not considered edible, its eggs are thought to be a great delicacy; the female fs very prolific, laying a large number of eggs at intervals of three weeks, which is also the period required to hatch the young. They are laid In the sand and hatched by the heat of the sun. When the hatched young have to run the gauntlet of many enemies before they reach the sea, toward which they instinctively rush. The writer has a specimen of the above species. The “green turtle” is the best known of the sea turtles. Its flesh is highly prized by epicures. The specimen captured by Mr. Larco’s fishermen is the “Luth,” or leathery turtle (Sphargis coriacia), so called from the fact of its having no horny plates, the bones being covered with a strong leathery skin. The eyelids open vertically Instead of horizontally, giving it a comical expression. About the back are seven longitudinal ridges, sharp and slightly toothed. The Jaw has two curved sharply pointed teeth, and the extremity of the lower Jaw is strongly hooped. The flesh is not good for food, as it causes many symptoms of poisoning in those who eat it It feeds on fish, and other marine animals, and lays about three hundred and fifty , eggs at a time. One of its best known hatching places is the Turtle Islands of Florida. It Inhabits the warm regions of the Atlantic, Pacific and Indian oceans, and grows to a great size, often weighing more than 1600 pounds and attains a length of 8 feet From the great development of its fore legs, it ib a good long-distance swimmer, and often ventures far out to sea, and is sometimes driven to strange countries. In a specimen eight feet long, the fore legs, which are devoid of claws, are about thfce feet long. Us general color Is a dark blackish olive, spotted and blotched with light yellow or white.""","2","""Monster Turtle.""","1","leatherback","reported","santa_barbara","inferred","not_reported","8'","not_reported","""specific location not given, just mentions Santa Barbara. Exact measurements of size and weight are also not given, but general measurements for the species as a whole is provided""","1901",8,"high",243.84,1900
-"36","slomt_19050324","""Morning Tribune"" ",1905-03-24,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SLOMT19050324.2.5&srpos=4&e=-------en--20-SLOMT-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""E.S. Hoyt Has Negative of a Rare Curiosity—Photographs Furnished to Academy of Science \n E. S. Hoyt is the owner of a kodak negative of rare value. Prof. F. M. Anderson of the Academy of Science of San Francisco has sent here to have some photographs developed from this negative from which wood cuts will be made to be used in embellishing a write-up soon to be printed. The subject of the photog[r]aph is a mammoth turtle captured in Santa Barbara in 1901 and seen by Mr. Hoyt at that time when he secured the negative while the huge animal was on a pair of trucks. \n It will be remembered that a monster turtle was captured in the waters off the coast of Santa Barbara last summer. The two turtles were alike, the latter being somewhat the larger, and are the only two of the kind ever captured in California waters. Neither lived and it happens that Mr. Hoyt has the best negative known to exits of the first turtle. \n Some of the pictures were finished yesterday at the Lewis gallery and sent to the Academy of Science. The matter being prepared for the writeup will doubtless be of considerable value and interest as the turtles were monsters and of a very rare species.""","2","""PICTURE OF BIG TURTLE""","1","green","inferred","santa_barbara","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Though the year of publishing is 1905, this turtle was captured in 1901. The species is unspecified and no actual size is given, so it is inferred to be green, however, given the rarity and how it is labelled as a 'mammoth', it is possibly a leatherback. The article references another turtle of the same species being captured in a nearby area the prior year and says: 'The two turtles were alike, the latter being somewhat the larger, and are the only two of the kind ever captured in California waters.'""","1905",8,"high",NA,1900
-"37","sbmp_18930721","""Morning Press""",1893-07-21,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP18930721.2.32&srpos=106&e=-------en--20-MP-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Mr. N. Gutierrez, in company with Jennie Larco, caught a big sea turtle yesterday weighing 60 pounds. This is the first one caught at Santa Barbara in the past four or five years.""","2","""CITY AND VICINITY""","1","green","inferred","santa_barbara","inferred","60","not_reported","not_reported","""Included text is not the entire article, only the relevant text of the story. Article mentions that it is rather rare to find a turtle in Santa Barbara at this time (first one in the past 4-5 years)""","1893",7,"high",NA,1890
-"38","latimes_19050108","""Los Angeles Times""",1905-01-08,4,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164264960/D4971430CE2047A5PQ/301?accountid=14026""","“Large Santa Barbara Turtle and Its Captors. \n Capt. George Gourley and Albert Stafford of Santa Barbara caught a monster sea turtle in the ocean near the pleasure wharf of that city Tuesday. Still alive the big animal specimen was hauled to the pleasure wharf and made fast with ropes. Its estimated weight is 800 pounds. \n Stafford first saw the turtle while rowing in a small skiff. It was entangled in seaweed and seemed to be asleep. Stafford went ashore and found Gourley and the two put out in a sailboat. The managed to gaff it in one of the flippers and throw ropes about it so they could bring it to the pier. The turtle made fierce resistance. \n According to a classification of Prof. Dodge of the High School of Santa Barbara the monster specimen is a Sphargis coriacea, or leather-backed turtle. When it was hauled to the wharf there were a number of small fish known as remoras attached to it.”","2","""Huge Leather-back Taken from the Sea""","1","leatherback","reported","santa_barbara","known","800","not_reported","""asleep entangled in seaweed""","""article lists species as leatherback, and also mentions the historic latin name, sphargis coriacea""","1905",10,"high",NA,1900
-"39","sdunion_19110922","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1911-09-22,10,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19110922.2.111&srpos=1&e=-------en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle+AND+alejandro+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+doves+NOT+house-------""","""Legends of Peninsula Say Old Tortoise Alejandro Was Tamed by Selkirk. \n Old Alejandro has been captured alive, away down south of San Quintin, and now lives ignominiously upon the floor of the San Diego Fish company’s market at the foot of D street. \n Alejandro, the pet turtle of Robinson Crusoe, according to the legends of the Mexicans of Lower California, has been the terror of all fishermen since the whaling days, and so tradition says, in the days of the whalers fought the whaling boats. Antedating the days of the whaling ships that sailed hence from the coast of Maine, the old turtle wrested for his domain with the Spanish galleons, gold laden, and Manila bound. \n Many are the tales that the Mexicans tell their little children of the prowess of Old Alejandro, the turtle that knew no fear and that could sink a whale-boat with one stroke of his powerful flipper. Like the bugaboo of the northland, he would “get the little children if they did not watch out.” \n They say that he was gentle once, several hundred years ago, when he followed Robinson Crusoe around the shores of Magdalena bay, although he is not mentioned in the historical accounts that tell of the presence there of Alexander Selkirk (Robinson Crusoe. It is a matter of history that Selkirk was at that time the second-mate of a British warship. \n Legend following history, the folklore of the peninsula, is full of incidents in the life of old Alejandro. \n He was captured two weeks ago, speared by an Indian fisherman many miles below San Quintin. \n In company with two others of his kind, the famous old tortoise is awaiting the headsman’s knife and will soon round out his semi-millenium in a pot of soup on the range of a restauranteur.""","1","""Robinson Crusoe's Pet Turtle Speared Near Port of San Quintin""","1","green","inferred","san_quintin","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""much of this report appears to be fiction or exaggerated for entertainment, but some aspects may be true.""","1911",6,"medium",NA,1910
-"40","laherald_19040507","""Los Angeles Herald""",1904-05-07,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19040507.2.58&srpos=17&e=-------en--20-LAH-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","“1200-Pounder Is Named for President \n (Special to The Herald.) \n SAN PEDRO. May 6.—""Teddy Roosevelt,"" a 1200-pound turtle, which was caught yesterday by Capt. George Evitch twelve miles off the coast, is to go to the St. Louis exposition. Hundreds of people took a look at the monster today. As soon as arrangements can be made it will be sent east by rail. \n The turtle measures seven feet in length and is four feet wide. From tip to tip of his flippers the distance is about ten feet. It has the appearance of being several hundred years old. Three ridges run lengthwise on the shell. \n Several bullet holes are records of previous ineffectual attempts at his capture. The shell is of a greenish hue and very rough. The turtle Is classified scientifically as ""festudo lyra."" \n When angered the turtle spouts like a whale and threshes the water into foam. It is believed that a school of these turtles has been carried here in some ocean current. The steamer Santa Cruz, which came into port today, captured nearly a dozen smaller specimens in the channel this morning.”","1","""HUGE TURTLE IS TO GO TO FAIR""","1","leatherback","reported","san_pedro","inferred","1200","7'","not_reported","""'festudo lyra' = testudo lyra, a historic name for leatherbacks; four feet in width and from tip to tip of flippers is about 10 feet; article also notes almost a dozen more smaller leatherbacks were caught a few days later in the same area""","1904",7,"high",213.36,1900
-"41","laherald_19040509","""Los Angeles Herald""",1904-05-09,10,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19040509.2.146&srpos=88&e=-------en--20-LAH-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""SAN PEDRO, May 8—lt is not a certainty that the big turtle caught by George Evitch will be sent to St. Louis. The managers of the Chutes in Los Angeles have offered Capt. Evitch $500 for the turtle. The price set upon his head is just double that amount.""","2","""Chutes Want Monster Turtle""","1","green","inferred","san_pedro","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""could potentially be another species, given that the size is noted to be so large. Cannot confirm without further desriptions""","1904",7,"high",NA,1900
-"42","sppilot_19390310","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1939-03-10,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19390310.2.5&srpos=60&e=-------en--20-SPNP-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------:","""A replica of the big leatherback turtle which was captured off San Pedro several weeks ago is crated at Cabrillo beach marine museum and awaiting shipment to the San Francisco exposition. With the specimen is a porpoise and several rare fish which will go on display as San Pedro’s contribution to the exposition.""","1","""Museum to Ship Turtle to Fair""","1","leatherback","reported","san_pedro","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be reporting the same turtle observation as sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390830, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390529 (redundant). While the location is reported in this article as san_pedro, it is likely more specifically point_vincente, assuming that it is indeed the same specimen as the other four articles, though admittedly the connection is a little weaker for this article. Timing is a few months off.""","1939",8,"high",NA,1930
-"43","sppilot_19390529","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1939-05-29,22,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19390519.2.249&srpos=110&e=-------en--20-SPNP-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""It was started as a hobby enterprise several years ago. Today it’s an institution and so great is its lure to thousands of visitors from all over the country that plans are being laid to build an elaborate building especially for it. \n This briefly is the history of the Cabrillo Beach Marine museum, which provides an attractive point of interest for visitors to the harbor area. \n Last year when a 1,100-pound giant turtle, caught in Mexican waters, was on display, thousands of visitors’ came especially to see the giant specimen. And hundreds of letters arrived from various sections of the country seeking information. \n A plaster cast life-size replica of the turtle in now on display at the San Francisco Golden Gate Exposition. \n The museum was started as a hobby in order to provide a place where persons interested in the nature lore of the Pacific coast could learn about the flora and fauna of the sea and shore, and also bring their own private collections of birds, fish, shells and other specimens for identification. \n From this beginning the museum has grown until it now contains hundreds of fascinating specimens of the under sea and shore denizens of the Pacific. Growth of the museum has been assisted through donations to it by various expeditions to Mexico, Central and South America, including those of Captain G. Allen Hancock, well known sportsman-explorer. The museum is under the direction of Dr. William L. Lloyd, former University of Pennsylvania naturalist.""","2","""Permanent Building Planned for Cabrillo Beach Museum""","1","leatherback","reported","san_pedro","inferred","1100","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be reporting the same turtle/observation as sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390830, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390310. This article is reporting in retrospect and therefore timing of this article is off from the time of capture by about a year. The location of capture is not given, so I reported it as San Pedro, however based on the other sources it seems point vincente would be more accurate of a location. Additionally, the species is noted as reported, even though not explictly stated, based on previous reports.""","1939",8,"high",NA,1930
-"44","sppilot_19130920","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1913-09-20,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19130920.2.23&srpos=94&e=-------en--20-SPNP-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Capt. Dan Kain's launch Tillikum stumbled onto a Japanese razor-back turtle sunning of the surface of the deep about four miles off the end of the breakwater. By dexterous manipulation the quarry was potted with a harpoon and towed to the Long Beach municipal dock alive Saturday. He measured about 6x4 feet and weight undressed approximately 1200. \n Old timers hereabouts classify the speciman as a member of a Japanese family of razor-backs never before seen in these waters and probably carried hither by the Japanese current. Veteran fishermen say it’s the choicest table product of all deep sea turtles. \n Capt. Kain will have the shell and skin mounted and will in a double sense try out the oil for its virtues as a rheumatism cure. The skeleton is still on exhibition at the end of the municipal pier. The meat was sold at a local fish market.""","2","""CAPTURED JAPANESE TURTLE WEIGHING 1200 POUNDS""","1","leatherback","inferred","san_pedro","inferred","1200","6'","""sunning of the surface of the deep""","""The article notes the dimensions to be about 6x4, and the location is inferred as san_pedro, because that is where the newspaper is from, but it was said to be 'about four miles off the end of the breakwater.' and then was brought to the Long Beach dock. The species is named as a 'Japanese razor-back' but given the size, it's most likely a leatherback; it is noted as being rare in this area""","1913",8,"high",182.88,1910
-"45","latimes_19090718","""Los Angeles Times""",1909-07-18,10,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/159327339/fulltextPDF/831523F0C774447FPQ/1?accountid=14026""","“FISHERMAN TRIES VAINLY TO GET IT IN PORT ALIVE. \n Monster Seven Feet Long and Believed to Be Thousand Years Old. Second One of Species Ever Captured Off Coast Kills Itself in Struggle for Freedom. \n SAN PEDRO. July 17.—After trying for two days to tow into port alive a big turtle of rare species, Steve Williams, an East San Pedro fisherman, arrived home this morning, but the monster was dead. The turtle became entangled in a buoy rope attached to his nets, and ropes were made fast to its flippers. Had not the engine of Williams's launch become disabled, the turtle would not have killed itself in its efforts to regain its freedom. \n It is the second of its kind ever captured in these waters and weighs about 1000 pounds. It is estimated that it was 1000 years old. It was more than seven feet in length, and measured nearly nine feet between the extremities of its front fllþpers. \n Unlike the land turtle, It has two long front flippers and two short hind ones, like those of a seal. The shell is soft, with five ridges down the back. Fishermen occasionally see turtles of this species in the fishing banks, but say they are very timid, and that a boat cannot get close enough to capture them. This one was caught in twenty fathoms of water. \n Williams will have his prize mounted and sell it to a museum.”","2","""THOUSAND-POUND TURTLE CAUGHT.""","1","leatherback","inferred","san_pedro","inferred","1000","7'","not_reported","""species is inferred based on description of a soft shell and five ridges on the back""","1909",7,"high",213.36,1900
-"46","sdut_19610707","""San Diego Union Tribune""",1961-07-07,NA,NA,"""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-13C1722552DF9770%402437488-13C16EB6A122C338%402-13C16EB6A122C338%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=6&pdate=1961-07-07"" ","""A 1,000-pound turtle, described as the largest ever seen in Southern California waters, lies on dock at Long Beach. A fishing boat saw the turtle about two miles off San PEdro break-water and used grappling hooks to make the catch. Turtle was towed into Long Beach harbor.""","1","""Record Catch In Coast Waters""","1","leatherback","inferred","san_pedro","known","1000","not_reported","not_reported","""although the species is inferred, there is a fairly clear photo with a leatherback. This, in addition to size, makes it a fairly clear identification. Location specifically is given as 'two miles off San Pedro breakwater'""","1961",7,"high",NA,1960
-"47","latimes_19050102","""Los Angeles Times""",1905-01-02,13,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164327510/17A6E603F61A4B73PQ/416?accountid=14026""","“MAMMOTH TURTLE. \n On the return from the whistling buoy to San Pedro breakwater, a huge turtle was closely passed. It was clumsily flapping its way to windward, the huge forward flippers seeming to cause but little progress. The commodore got the pikepole ready as the yacht was swung around for the encounter, but the huge beast did not wait for the tourney and disappeared. This turtle was caught the same night in the nets of the Italian fishermen and brought into San Pedro the next morning, where it turned the scales at 1600 pounds and was sent to the St. Louis Exposition as a California product. It probably came, however, from more southerly islands and seemed to be of the same species which Darwin found!\ in the Galapagos Islands, and which he described in his ""Naturalist's Trip Round the World.” This species attains great age and size, is possessed of much sugacity and makes long ocean passages from one island to another. How this one got so far off his course is a mystery.”","1","""PARADISE FOR YACHTSMEN""","1","leatherback","inferred","san_pedro","inferred","1600","not_reported","not_reported","""the species is not specified, however, it claims to be the same one that Darwin found in the Galapagos, and makes long ocean passages from one island to another. Also note the size.""","1905",6,"medium",NA,1900
-"48","latimes_19020812","""Los Angeles Times""",1902-08-12,5,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164090597/17A6E603F61A4B73PQ/420?accountid=14026""","“E. W. Scripps of San Diego, who, with his family and party of friends, in two yachts and a power launch, were in the bay here a week ago, are now sojourning at the Isthmus, and will probably remain there for the next two months. Mr. Scripps has just chartered two more boats here, the yacht Avalon, and the fishing launch Mero, and they went up to the Isthmus this morning. The outing is taken for the benefit of the health of one of Mr. Scripps’s boys, and the family is accompanied by a tutor, a physician, a secretary and a storekeeper, besides servants. They will use the Avalon for making trips to San Nicholas and perhaps other islands of the channel group. They are out for a good time, and expense cuts no figure. The party is well pleased with the Isthmus, and they are negotiating for a lease of the Barracks and the cottages near by. \n D. J. Alberga, with the Pacific Coast Biscuit Company, had the honor of bringing in the only big fish today. He got a 195-pound black sea bass, which kept him out of mischief for an hour and a half. A picturesque feature of his catch was when his boatman, “Shorty” Bencke, skipper of the Catalina, finding the fish was about to carry the line into his propeller, jumped overboard without divesting himself of his clothing and diving under the boat prevented the fouling of the wheel and saved the fish! With Mr. Alberga were M. E. Conboy and F. J. Matthie of Tucson. \n The launch Idlewild took a party to the Isthmus. The trip was arranged by Mrs. L. J. Merritt for the members of the Moscos and Cosmos Clubs of Pasadena and their friends on the island. The party consisted of Mrs. L. J. Merritt, Mrs. George Kelly and Mrs. J. H. Farley of Stanford; Mr. and Mrs. Ammerman of Duluth, Mr. and Mrs. Earl Barnhart of Chicago; Misses Alice Ammerman, Daisy White, Evelyn Merritt, Bertha Merritt, Daisy White: Messrs. Horace White, John Newkirk of Berkeley, W. Arthur McNally and Mr. Newberry. \n F. L. Hober, W. H. and Harry Bollyn ran up against a fighting shark this morning. They were out for sand dabs, of which they made a good catch, and were coming home, trolling a line for yellowtail, when a white shark about four feet in length took the bait. He fought hard till they got him up near the boat, and then he made a lunge for them, and came near landing in the boat. He made several more tries at it seeming determined to get at them, when one of the men clubbed an ore and dispatched the vicious brute. \n H. T. Kendall of Pasadena, who now has the honor of holding the word’s record for biggest fish, has method in having the monster 419-pound fish mounted. He is vice-president of the Potter Hotel Company at Santa Barbara, and proposes to give the big bass a place of special honor in the hall or lobby of the new hotel, which is the largest lobby contained in any hotel in the United States, which makes it appropriate that it should contain the largest fish. \n Capt. Trefethen of the Hermosa reports having seen an enormous sea turtle yesterday on his trip over from San Pedro. It was not more than a hundred feet away from the track of the vessel, and he had a good view of the creature, which he asserts was from eight to ten feet across the back. The captain says this is the largest turtle he has ever seen in his experience of more than twenty years in these waters. \n The Mascot took out a fishing party yesterday, composed of Miss Carrie Connell of Phoenix, K. C. Kitt of Tucson, Misses Corinne Frances, Celia Knowles, Leota Latimer, G. Campbell, W. Culver and A. Cooper of Los Angeles, who caught seven yellowtail and three barracuda. \n James Burton Adams, editor of the Denver Post, with his wife, is again visiting Santa Catalina. Mr. Adams says they design taking up their residence in Los Angeles at no distant day. \n Mrs. Helen Safford, who has been here the guest of Mrs. Ed Stanton for a fortnight, left for her home in Los Angeles this morning regretted by a large circle of friends. \n J. A. Simpson, master mechanic of the Southern Pacific Railway at Los Angeles, accompanied by his wife, is taking an outing on the island for a week. \n Miss Sylvia Bowman of Pasadena and Miss Mabel Leaverton of Hollywood, are guests of Miss G. Myrtle Stotts at Beach Haven cottage. \n Maj. H. C. Eggers of Fresno joined a number of his townsmen and friends at the Grand View for a few days.”","2","""ISLAND BREVITIES""","1","green","inferred","san_pedro","inferred","not_reported","10'","not_reported","""specifically size is given as 8 to 10 feet across the back""","1902",7,"high",304.8,1900
