+"51","stinson_sdunion_19030524","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1903-05-24,NA,"48","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Peter Stanovitch brought in fourteen large turtles which he had caught during the night, the largest one weighing 500 pounds [230 kg]. The turtles were captured in the shallow waters of the mud flats where they breed at this time of the year.""","2","""History of Sea Turtles in San Diego Bay""","14","green","inferred","san_diego","inferred","500","not_reported","""mud flats""","""fishermen supposedly believed that these turtles were the children of the children of the turtles from baja california that escaped from their corral in the san diego bay in 1857, but evidence is unclear""","1903",8,"high",NA,14,226.8,1900
+"52","latimes_18990204","""Los Angeles Times""",1899-02-04,15,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/163853622/590BBC08B3D34453PQ/70?accountid=14026""","“Among the fishermen outside yesterday in the choppy sea were two young men from San Diego, who had great sport catching a school of sea turtles into which their smack happened to run. The turtles were upon the surface of the sea, sunning themselves, and floating lazily along. The boys rigged up strong lines with nooses, and stole silently alongside the biggest fellow, who seemed to be asleep. With a quick jerk one of his flippers was caught, and the rope jerked taut. \n There was a lively sequel, and the big turtle pulled himself together and tried to dive. He could not, and reappearing, he started on a fast gait to the southward. The toys gradually hauled him to the gunwale, where with another line they got him into the boat. He weighed 276 pounds. In this manner they caught thirteen big turtles, running from eighty to 245 pounds. It is not often that the turtles are found off this part of the coast, as they prefer the warm water of the Gulf of California. \n The fish catch yesterday was: Halibut, 100; lobsters, 150 pounds, trout, 100, croakers, 175; silver smelt. 135 pounds.”","1","""FUN WITH SEA TURTLES.""","13","green","inferred","san_diego","inferred","276","not_reported","not_reported",NA,"1899",6,"medium",NA,13,125.19,1890
+"53","sdunion_19100905","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1910-09-05,15,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19100905.2.130&srpos=14&e=------191-en--20-SDDU-1--txt-txIN-turtle-------""","""Turtle Caught by Steve Zolezzie While He Was Fishing in Ocean South of San Diego. \n Ocean-Going Monster Weighing 487 Pounds Becomes Entangled in Net \n A giant green turtle which became entangled in nets of Steve Zolezzi, a local fisherman, was captured by him and a companion in the kelp near the boundary line yesterday morning. \n The big chelonian was found about 5 o'clock, when the fishermen went out to pay their usual morning visit to their nets. It had completely enmeshed itself and its captor worked hard for several hours before it was finally freed and the nets were hauled away from its vicious back-shaped jaws. One section of the nets was practically destroyed, involving a loss of about$50 to the owners. \n Despite the enormous size of the turtle it was finally dragged into Zolezzi’s boat by the fisherman and his companion and brought to San Diego. It was landed at the wharf of the San Diego fish market, where it was the cynosure of curious eyes during the entire day. It will not be killed until Monday, when, according to the statements of the market management last night, the edible portions of its huge hulk will be sold to Harry Rudder of the Union cafe. \n The turtle tipped the scales at 487 pounds.""","2","""GIANT TURTLE ENTRAPPED; SOON TO BE IN THE SOUP""","1","green","reported","san_diego","inferred","487","not_reported","""in the kelp near the boundary line""","""note that the name of the man who caught turtle is spelled two different ways (typo). While 487 lbs seems perhaps unrealistically heavy, it is an exact weight reported to be measured by scales, not an estimate.""","1910",9,"high",NA,1,220.9,1910
 "54","sdunion_19070621","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1907-06-21,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDDU19070621.2.27&srpos=26&e=-------en--20-SDDU-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey-------""","""Monster Found By Fisherman Off Coast Evidently In Toils Before \n HAS NAME AND DATE BURNED IN SHELL \n Evidently Picked Up By British Ship Sea Bride in August 1881 \n Of all the peculiar and mysterious things given up by the sea, one of the most puzzling ever found by San Diego fishermen was discovered yesterday in the shape of a gigantic turtle captured in a fish net by Julius Solissa, who is considered the cleverest fisherman in the city, despite his youth. \n Solissa, who is employed by Charles Holmquist, president of the San Diego Fish company, went out in his big gasoline fishing launch to the cod banks on his regular trip last Wednesday morning at 1:30 o'clock. Nothing out of the ordinary routine of fishing occurred, as the running was slow and poor for hours. \n Young Solissa's trained eye soon discovered that something was wrong with the nets and the disturbance suddenly grew, so that it appeared as though some monster were churning the water under the boat. \n Solissa believed at first that some of the seals which patrol the nets in big herds had become entangled in the nets and nettled back to wait until they had been drowned by being held under the water for a long time. \n But the row continued and Solissa finally concluding that a large fish must be the cause of the trouble proceeded to drag in the nets to look them over. \n He was greatly surprised to find an enormous turtle, which struggled so violently that it wound the strings of the net around its flippers and cut them until the blood ran and the flesh was torn to shreds. \n Solissa lowered the net and made off to find his mates in the other launches. Returning, three other fishermen aided Julius to' make the turtle fast to the boat, but it was all they could do to handle it, as the turtle pulled them around like mere children. They were finally compelled to give up the task of pulling the turtle into the boat, and were satisfied to tow it in with them on Thursday noon. \n When the turtle was finally landed in the shipping room of the San Diego Fish company, the big surprise came. \n Although the monster's back shell was covered with slime and barnacles, when these were scraped off and the shell fairly cleaned, numerous characters were plainly visible. It appeared that they had been either burned or cut deep into the shell years ago. \n After some study, the following inscription was deciphered: \n “Br. S. Sea Bride —Aug. 12, 1881—3S.—86 W."" was on the left side of the turtle's shell, looking toward the head, while these words were on the right side: \n ""If Found Please Notify— Thomas Fletcher—Brawley Road. Rivington, Eng.” \n It would appear from an Interpretation of the inscription that a sailor or officer of the British ship Sea Bride, caught this turtle in Pacific waters off the coast of South America, August 12, 1881, in latitude 3 South and longitude 86 west, branded or cut the characters indicated on the turtle's shell and threw it back into the ocean again. \n The turtle must, therefore, he at least 26 years old, and it is probable that it is much older, as it tipped the scales at 1,902 1-2 pounds—nearly a ton— while it measured 5 feet 2 inches over the shell from front to rear. \n It was wrinkled about the neck like a clown's collar and was a pathetic sight as it lay on the shipping room floor, helpless as a baby, its great flippers all torn to pieces by its own futile struggles in the fishing net. \n The turtle was immediately sold by evidently quite wary and wise to have escaped the wiles of man and its fellow monsters of the deep for over 26 years, but its feminine curiosity for the fishing net probably caused its capture an ddeath. \n The turtle was immediately sold by telegraphic order to a big fish market in Los Angeles and by this time is probably nothing but fine French soup and steaks on the fashionable tables of that city.”
-","2","""HUGE TURTLE CAPTURED IN NET BEARS MESSAGE""","1","green","inferred","san_diego","inferred","1902","5'2""","not_reported","""this turtle was captured twice, this being the second time. The first was off the coast of South America 26 years prior. this also seems to be reporting the same turtle observation/capture as laherald_19070622, latimes_19070622, and sdut_19070621. This exact article can also be found in the sd union tribune archives at ""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-13910D6796D5FE01%402417748-13910B4FBB102280%402-13910B4FBB102280%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=3&pdate=1907-06-21""""","1907",7,"high",157.48,1900
-"55","laherald_19070622","""Los Angeles Herald""",1907-06-22,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19070622.2.11&srpos=133&e=-------en--20-LAH-121--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""By Associated Press. \n SAN DIEGO, June 21.— One of the puzzles of the sea came to the surface yesterday when a gigantic sea turtle was captured by Julius Solissa, a fisherman in whose net it had become entangled. The monster weighs 1902 1/2 pounds, almost a ton, and its shell is five feet two inches from tip to tip. On Its back plainly is burned or carved this inscription: ""British ship Sea Bride, .August 12, 1881, 2 south, 86 wset. If found please notify Thomas Fletcher, Brawley road, Rivington, England."" \n It would appear from this that the turtle was captured twenty-six years ago in the South Pacific, and that it was released after the lettering had been burned in its shell.""","2","""TURTLE FIRST CAUGHT IN SOUTH PACIFIC IS SAN DIEGO PUZZLE""","1","green","inferred","san_diego","inferred","1902.5","5'2""","not_reported","""this appears to be the same turtle that is reported about in sdunion_19070621, latimes_19070622, and sdut_19070621; additionally, wset is a typo of west in the article itself""","1907",7,"high",157.48,1900
-"56","laherald_19120828","""Los Angeles Herald""",1912-08-28,13,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=LAH19120828.2.111&srpos=134&e=-------en--20-LAH-121--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+tippy+NOT+%22good+night%22-------""","""The 200-pound shell of the largest turtle in the world, captured yesterday near San Diego after a terrific battle of seven hours on the sea, will serve as a bulletin board for the San Diego 1915 fair in the exhibit rooms of the Los Angeles chamber of commerce, if James A. Jasper, special representative of the southern city's chamber here, is successful in his efforts to secure the shell. \n The turtle measured nearly eight feet in length, six feet in width and was two feet thick. It weighed 2500 pounds. \n ""I am exerting every effort to secure the shell for the exhibit rooms of the chamber of commerce here,” said Mr. Jasper. “I propose to advertise the San Diego fair with a sketch of the exposition grounds on its shell.”""","2","""World’s Biggest Turtle Shell to Boost 1915 Fair""","1","leatherback","inferred","san_diego","inferred","2500","8'","not_reported",NA,"1912",7,"high",243.84,1910
-"57","natcitysn_19660529","""National City Star-News""",1966-05-29,NA,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=NCSN19660529.2.27&srpos=1&e=-------en--20-NCSN-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""A National City man, Ludwick Egger, could hardly believe his eyes when a giant sea turtle surfaced near his boat while he was fishing in San Diego Bay this week. \n “I never heard of sea turtles around San Diego,” he said, “but there he was.” The turtle surfaced about 50 feet from the boat the first time he was spotted and later surfaced about 35 feet away, Egger said. \n Egger said he had a fishing companion in the boat, “and he can vouch for the turtle.” Egger, who lives with his wife at 43 L Ave., said he is taking a ribbing from friends who insist he saw the turtle because he was intoxicated. \n Egger said he saw the “big flippers” of the turtle “and I got so hungry. I dearly love turtle. Those big flippers make wonderful roasts.” \n Egger said he is concerned about the turtle causing a speedboat accident “My grandson has a speedboat, and if he hit that big fellow it would wipe out the boat” \n A member of the San Diego Zoo’s Reptile division staff said, “Although it is certainly not an everyday occurrence, sea turtles do get up this far north. I have heard of four or five instances where turtles were sighted in the bay.” \n The turtles weigh from 200 to 800 pounds and abound in the southern waters.""","2","""It's a Sea Turtle—In San Diego Bay?""","1","green","inferred","san_diego","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""The article notes that there have been 4-5 other sightings in the recent past, and 'although it is certainly not an everyday occurance [to see sea turtles in san diego bay], sea turtles do get up this far north'. The location says San Diego bay and the article doesn't note the weight of this exact turtle, but says that generally the turtles are 200-800 lbs.""","1966",8,"high",NA,1960
-"58","latimes_19070622","""Los Angeles Times""",1907-06-22,11,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/159114704/590BBC08B3D34453PQ/86?accountid=14026""","“SEA MONSTER WEIGHING ALMOST TON IS CAPTURED \n On Its Shell Is Discovered Inscription Showing That the Animal Had Been Captured by British Sailor in Southern Seas Twenty-six Years Ago. [BY THE ASSOCIATED PRESS— P.M.] \n SAN DIEGO, June 21.—One of the puzzles of the sea came to the surface yesterday, when a gigantic turtle was captured by Julius Solissa, a fisherman in whose net it had become entangled. The monster weighs 1902½ pounds, or almost a ton, and its shell is five feet two inches from tip to tip. On its back, somewhat plainly, is burned or carved this inscription: \n “British ship Sea Bride, August 12, 1881. 8 south, 86 west. If found please notify Thomas Fletcher, Brawley Road, Rivington, England."" \n It would appear from this that the turtle was captured twenty-six years ago in the South Pacific and that he was released after the lettering had been burned in its shell.”","2","""GIANT TURTLE BEARS MESSAGE""","1","green","inferred","san_diego","inferred","1902.5","5'2""","not_reported","""same observation (redundant) as laherald_19070622, sdut_19070621, sdunion_19070621. could possibly be a leatherback based on size""","1907",7,"high",157.48,1900
-"59","sdut_19550927","""San Diego Union Tribune""",1955-09-27,NA,NA,"""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-13C1810E91B2E52E%402435378-13C13C52EC1C0E73%4014-13C13C52EC1C0E73%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=2&pdate=1955-09-27""","“[image included] Crowd inspects captured 750-pound turtle on freight elevator. Charles Sweeney, at left, and Billy Cromer, second from right, are among seven sailors who caught it. At right is Arvid Roper, U. S. Grant Hotel chef who gave it temporary home. \n LARGEST IN CAPTIVITY \n By JOE BROOKS \n A 750-pound turtle last night reached the comparative tranquility of a commercial aquarium near Palos Verdes after some harrowing experiences in the San Diego area. \n It is the largest turtle on exhibit anywhere. And if it remains alive, it will be the first Leatherback turtle that ever did so in captivity. \n If not, it'll make a mammoth pot of turtle soup. \n The turtle's bad luck started Sunday when it ran afoul of a boatload of seven U. S. sailors while loafing in a kelp bed off Rosarito Beach. \n TWO-HOUR STRUGGLE \n The sailors were imbued with that natural human instinct of catching anything big that one encounters. They lassoed the critter with the anchor line and after a two-hour struggle succeeded in getting it ashore and hoisting it into their car trunk. \n On the trip to San Diego the weight of the huge reptile broke the car springs. Then on arrival, no one could be found to take custody of the creature. The zoo couldn't. The city pound wouldn't. \n The sailors finally left the turtle in the basement of the U.S. Grant Hotel. Sam Hinton, curator of the aquarium at Scripps Institution of Oceanography, placed it [t]emporarily in the quarantine tank at Scripps. \n ""There were four of us and we nearly killed ourselves getting that turtle moved,"" Hinton said. \n TOOK 10 MEN \n Three men from Marineland Aquarium arrived in a pickup truck late yesterday afternoon to take the turtle there. \n Ten men lifted it out of the tank and placed it on its back on a mattress on the truck bed. \n A cargo net was used to truss it for the trip north. \n Leatherbacks are the largest species of turtle, Hinton said. But none has ever lived in captivity, ""although this one looks like it has a good chance,"" he said. ""It is in good shape."" \n ""We'll put it in the porpoise tank,"" said Frank Calanbrino, assistant collector at Marineland. ""People love to see those big lumbering things swimming around.""”","2","""Monster Turtle Survives Ordeal""","1","leatherback","reported","rosarito_beach","known","750","not_reported","""loafing in kelp bed""","""article gives accompanying image""","1955",10,"high",NA,1950
-"60","latimes_19210511","""Los Angeles Times""",1921-05-11,6,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/160939571/pageviewPDF/6F8781D478ED49A8PQ/1?accountid=14026""","“[LOCAL CORRESPONDENCE] \n REDONDO BEACH, May 10.— Hearing that Redondo Beach has something unusual in the way of a harbor, a big spotted turtle, a kind often seen off the coast of Mexico and the West Indies, made port here, Tuesday. A sixty-foot mackerel line in the hands of Ed Shortridge and Johnny Greenback, local fishermen, towed the turtle to shore. It weighs at least 1000 pounds, it being impossible for six men with timbers to lift it. Fishermen here say that they have never seen this species in these waters before. Those who are familiar with the kind- estimate the age of this .one as between 1500 and 2000 years. This specimen will be on exhibition here for several days.”","2","""LOTS OF SOUP.""","1","leatherback","inferred","redondo_beach","known","1000","not_reported","not_reported","""the specific species is not reported, the article just claims it to be a 'big spotted turtle' and is rare for this area. this is perhaps a leatherback, not a green? especially given the size?""","1921",8,"high",NA,1920
-"61","sdbee_18870920","""San Diego Daily Bee""",1887-09-20,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SDB18870920.2.12&srpos=6&e=-------en--20-SDB-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Two car loads of San Diego county products left for St. Louis yesterday, consisting of fruits, vegetables, minerals and grain of as fine a quality as can be shown from any county in the State. Ensenada sent her quota from Baja California, among which was an immense living sea turtle, with which the Bay of Punta Banda abounds. \n At Fallbrook the cars will be reinforced with two living full-sized ostriches in full feather, grown without irrigation. While many of the fruits may not stand transportation, and arrive in as good condition as when sent, there will be sufficient to base a proper opinion on what this heretofore neglected country can do comparatively with others, whose boom commenced and died down to a legitimate condition long before ours was generally known to exist. \n Our Grand Army Corps, who have taken this portion of the St. Louis exhibit in charge, know our important responsibility as representative men. \n Mr. Wm. Lyons accompanies the procession, and what he lacks in exhibit can be made up with his, “literary specials’’ and placer gold samples, with which he is well provided. \n Several representatives of our local press will be in St. Louis, and many of our other citizens, all of which will have a tendency to give San Diego a satisfactory representation, and prove that such men as Lyons, Large, Hartzell, and others, were not propagated and brought to their present condition on climate alone; and after exhibiting themselves they can exhibit their rations. When the late Professor Louis Agagssiz remarked at a public assembly that that the climate of San Diego would produce a fortune to each of its then 2500 inhabitants there were but few that then appreciated the prophecy. Some 20,000 people have come to this city alone within fifteen months, and come to stay with us, while the county at large has been filled up in proportion. \n R. W. Dana in his phenomenal experiences with the Wide Drayers in 1834 and 5 reports San Diego as then the only port of safety on the “northwest coast.” characterizing about all others as the hell holds of California. \n Mrs. Helen Jackson in her “ramona,” written from personal observation here, pays a continual tribute to the climate and its productions, and speaks of a special suburb as “Oneonta,” or the place of rest. It’s a mottled question whether the “Oneonta” known to the trade and now sold in 25x140 lots has been proselyted to that purpose. Mr. Peck, of “Peck’s Sun,” even excels Mrs. Jackson in proving up the resting qualities of our climate. He relates that he came here to hunt and fish, but while enjoying a day’s rest previous to this hunting, he found resting was the very thing to do, and kept at it while here. In his last letter from Riverside to his paper he opens it with, “The old gentleman is still resting.” Mrs. Maria Burton, widow of General Burton, U.S.A., in her narration of the “Don and Squatter” from 1872 up to a recent date, confirms all previous statements as to climate that the radical element of emigration failed to interrupt. Our real estate agents of to-day keep up the refrain and in many instances seem to emulate the historic “Silas Megg” and “drop into poetry” without meaning to plagiarize. At the same time there is nothing abnormal in the condition of San Diego.""","1","""SAN DIEGO BEGINNING TO BOOM""","1","green","inferred","punta_banda","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""In the article it only mentions the sure existence of one sea turtle, however, it says that the Bay of Punta Banda 'abounds' with many sea turtles""","1887",7,"high",NA,1880
-"62","sbmp_19071030","""Morning Press""",1907-10-30,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP19071030.2.30&srpos=1&e=-------en--20-MP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","“SAN DIEGO, Oct. 29.—Word has just been received from Ensenada of the narrow escape from death of William J. Forbes, prominent in the affairs of Wall street, who is a guest of Walter Nordhoff, at his home at Puenta Banda. Mr. Forbes was fishing on the bay in a small boat accompanied by a boatman, when their line was taken by a sea turtle. The line held and the turtle towed the boat for some distance. The fishermen determined to land the turtle if possible, and in their efforts they overturned the boat. The turtle got away with the tackle and the fishermen were in the water for two hours before they were rescued and not one would have been able to stand it much longer.”","2","""TURTLE LEADS BOATMEN TO NARROW QUARTERS""","1","green","inferred","punta_banda","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Appears to be the same sdunion_19071029 and laherald_19071030. Species is unclear; either green or leatherback; location in article says puenta banda, but assumed to be punta banda""","1907",8,"high",NA,1900
-"63","slotelegram_19201229","""San Luis Obispo Daily Telegram""",1920-12-29,2,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SLODT19201229.2.28&srpos=28&e=-------en--20-SLODT-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""There was an enormous turtle in the bay at Port Harford yesterday.""","1","""TWENTY-ONE YEARS AGO""","1","green","inferred","port_harford_bay","known","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""Though the year of publishing is 1920, this turtle was captured 21 years prior (1899). The species is unspecified and no actual size is given, so it is inferred to be green, however, given that it is said to be 'enormous', it could possibly be a leatherback""","1920",7,"high",NA,1920
-"64","pvnews_19470828","""Palos Verdes Peninsula News""",1947-08-28,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=PVPN19470828.2.37&srpos=86&e=-------en--20-PVPN-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks+NOT+velvet-------""","""J. Adams \n Dr. William Lloyd, Director of the Cabrillo Reach Marine Museum, addressed members of the Friday Morning Study Club when they were conducted through the museum following their potluck picnic at the beach. Dr. Lloyd called attention to the fact that all wild creatures have the same problems as humans, that is, home, food and self-protection and their body is so developed to meet these problems. Of particular interest was the pelican, which has a seven or eight foot wing expansion. The wide wing spread compared to the body length enables the pelican to fly very slowly in its search for fish in the water. The bill is distinctive in its narrow top and scoop-net lower hill which spreads to hold a large quantity of fish. \n The huge leatherback turtle on display was harpooned off Point Vincente and weighed 1100 pounds and was estimated to have been between 300 and 1000 years old. \n During luncheon announcement was made by Mrs. Elmer Higgs, Club President, of the Overseas Relief Drive sponsored by church and civic groups as well as the Philharmonics and Artists concert series. Club members were invited to attend the Community Sing at Anderson Memorial 7:30 p. m., September 2nd. when they would be special guests. \n The first regular program of the year was announced for October 10th and memberships are now being received by Mrs. John Langer, Membership Chairman, 702 Ellery Drive. The luncheon was arranged by Mrs. Violette Boone, hospitality chairman. Also introduced were Mrs. W. H. Leisk, the club’s first president; Mrs. Fred Wright, charter member; and Mrs. L. R. Tarr, honorary life member.""","2","""Study Club Holds Potluck Picnic at Cabrillo Beach""","1","leatherback","reported","point_vincente","known","1100","not_reported","not_reported","""The location can be labeled more broadly as la_county""","1947",9,"high",NA,1940
-"65","sppilot_19381102","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1938-11-02,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19381102.2.12&srpos=14&e=-------en--20-SPNP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""The 1100-pound giant sea turtle caught offshore early in September by Capt. Abe Bolton and crew of the bait boat Izu will be preserved for Cabrillo beach marine museum. \n Bolton and his men, according to Playground Director Ernest Ehrke, have given the specimen over to the playground department. A Wilmington cold storage firm, which kept the huge amphibian frozen solid for nearly six weeks, co-operated by writing off all charges, Ehrke said. It was to be moved by truck today. \n The turtle will be prepared for exhibit by Dr. William Lloyd, museum curator. Authorities are puzzled over the appearance of this species in California waters, declaring it is an equatorial turtle.""","2","""Place 1100-Pound Sea Turtle In Cabrillo Beach Museum""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","point_vincente","inferred","1100","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be the same observation and turtle as sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390830, sppilot_19390310, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390529. Therefore, although no exact location is given, simply 'offshore', as well as no reported species, the information is inferred from those articles.""","1938",7,"high",NA,1930
-"66","sppilot_19380912","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1938-09-12,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19380912.2.32&srpos=16&e=-------en--20-SPNP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Soup meat and museum curio. That probably will be the fate of a giant 1100-pound turtle, a record catch, brought in over the weekend by Capt. Abe Bolton and his crew of the bait boat Izu. \n Now resting on ice at the Wilmington Ice company, the behemoth of the turtle family, he measures more than three feet across and seven feet long, puzzled the skipper and the ice company today as to what will become of it. Some of the meat is usable but to stuff the giant turtle would cost money and neither Ernest Ehrke of the Cabrillo beach marine museum nor Capt. G. Allan Hancock, leading marine curiosity seeker, appeared interested. \n Meanwhile, Jimmie Comer and other members of the bait boat crew were checking records which show, so far, that theirs is the largest turtle ever caught. \n The 1100-pound monster was surface swimming when first sighted near Point Vicente but led the boat on an hour chase before Bill Tagami hurled a harpoon through his leather-covered back. The turtle’s appearance so far north puzzled authorities who say this species is native to equatorial waters. Sharing credit for the catch with Capt. Bolton, Tagami' and Comer are Frank Bottles, Fred Thomas and I. Furukawa, all of San Pedro.""","2","""San Pedrans’ 1100-lb. Turtle Believed Largest Ever Caught""","1","leatherback","inferred","point_vincente","known","1100","7'","""surface swimming""","""This appears to be reporting the same observation and event as sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390830, sppilot_19390310, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390529, making this clipping redundant, but with a bit more detail. The size is reported to be 3 feet across, and the authors note that it is unusual at this time to see this species (possible leatherback) in these waters. The article is on page 1 and continued on page 2.""","1938",9,"high",213.36,1930
-"67","sppilot_19470818","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1947-08-18,6,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19470818.2.104&srpos=89&e=-------en--20-SPNP-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""The leatherback turtle on display was harpooned off Point Vincente and weighed 1100 pounds, Dr. Lloyd said. It is estimated to have been between 300 and 1000 years old at the time of its capture. It is the largest known sea turtle to have been captured. \n Dr. Lloyd, in showing a Gila Monster, told the group that it is the only poisonous lizard in this country, and stated that the lizards found in this area are beneficial to gardens for they kill harmful insects. \n The museum is operated by the Los Angeles Recreation and Parks department, and during the past year has been visited by 300,000 persons. Dr. Lloyd has addressed 253 groups in that time. The museum is distinctive in that it is the only one in the country which exhibits marine life exclusively.""","2","""Aged Turtle""","1","leatherback","reported","point_vincente","known","1100","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be reporting the same turtle as sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19390830, sppilot_19390310, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390529 based on size, location, species, and mention that it was displayed in a museum with dr. Lloyd for years. Though the time of this article's publishing is nearly a decade later, all the details connect to the other articles, though perhaps the connection is not certain as for the others. The article states the turtle is estimated to be between 300 and 1000 years old. Note that the timing of capture is not accurate to when this article specifically was published""","1947",9,"high",NA,1940
-"68","sppilot_19390830","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1939-08-30,NA,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19390830.2.246&srpos=56&e=-------en--20-SPNP-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Thousands of Persons Fascinated by Display of Marine Oddities \n More than 150,000 persons have visited the Marine Museum at Cabrillo beach to marvel at the varied specimens of ocean life on display. \n In San Francisco today visitors from all sections of the world gape at a replica of a monster turtle --- a 1,100-pound giant of the sea --- the original of which was a gift to the local museum. \n It is said to be the largest turtle ever caught and brought from tropical waters to these shores. Thirty-five years ago a turtle weighing slightly less than 1,000 pounds was caught and brought here. This was the only other time a turtle, even approaching the size of the recent catch, was landed in this section. \n Today the replica of the monster not only is one of the features of the exposition’s aquatic show, but also the center of interest at the local museum, where a duplicate plaster cast appears. \n The Cabrillo beach museum provides a place where persons interested in the nature lore of the Pacific coast may learn about the flora and fauna of the sea and shore and also bring in their own private collections of birds, fish, shells, and other specimens for identification. \n From this beginning the museum has grown until it now contains hundreds of fascinating specimens of the under sea and shore denizens of the Pacific. \n Many strange varieties of fish found in waters off the coast of California, Mexico and tropical islands to the southward are on display, as are preserved iguanas, Galapagos turtles, birds and rare creatures of many varieties seldom seen by the casual visitor to the sea shore. \n The giant sea crabs, many varieties of shell fish, specimens of plant life, snakes, and a great variety of other creatures are also displayed, many of them in settings depicting their natural habitat. \n Growth of the museum has been assisted through donations to it by various expeditions to Mexico, Central and South America, including those of Captain G. Allen Hancock, well known sportsman-explorer. The museum is under the direction of Dr. William L. Lloyd, former University of Pennsylvania naturalist. In addition to serving as curator of the institution, Dr. Lloyd conducts many nature study hobby groups for both children and adults at the municipal playgrounds of Hollywood and Los Angeles.""","2","""Cabrillo Museum Attracts Visitors""","1","leatherback","inferred","point_vincente","inferred","1100","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be reporting the same turtle as sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390310, sajournal_19380912, and sppilot_19390529 (redundant) based on size, location, species, and mention that it was displayed in a museum with dr. Lloyd for years. Though the time of this article's publishing is nearly a decade later, all the details connect to the other articles, though perhaps the connection is not certain as for the others. Note that the timing of capture is not accurate to when this article specifically was published. Additionally, while the location isn't specified, I have reported it as point_vincente by assuming it matches all the details of the other articles.""","1939",7,"high",NA,1930
-"69","sajournal_19380912","""Santa Ana Journal""",1938-09-12,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SAJ19380912.2.63&e=-------en--20-SAJ-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""WILMINGTON, Calif. GP) An 1100 pound leatherback turtle, which the boys on the bait boat Izu say may be the world’s largest, was unloaded with a derrick today. The turtle, measuring 88 inches from head to tail, was captured Saturday and kept on ice until the boat’s arrival last night.""","1","""1100-lb. Turtle Believed Record""","1","leatherback","reported","point_vincente","inferred","1100","7'4""","not_reported","""This article appears to be reporting the same turtle that is mentioned many times in the San Pedro News pilot, in sppilot_19381102, sppilot_19380912, sppilot_19470818, sppilot_19390830, sppilot_19390310, and sppilot_19390529. This connection is seen in the weight/size of the turtle, and the boat on which it was found. The location is inferred based on the connection to the other reports.""","1938",7,"high",223.52,1930
-"70","latimes_19050912","""Los Angeles Times""",1905-09-12,7,NA,"""https://www.proquest.com/docview/164411437/17A6E603F61A4B73PQ/401?accountid=14026""","“Yesterday Smith Warren, A. J. Werner and Misses Alice and Grace E. Marvin of Los Angeles chartered the launch Twilight, Capt. W. H. Graves and while trolling off Point Vincent sighted a monster turtle, which Graves says was nine feet across, with a head like a barrel. The Twilight ran alongside and Graves drove a harpoon into it, but the monster did not linger and disappeared with the harpoon which had a red handle. The party spent the remainder of the afternoon chasing that red handle all over the channel, but failed in their attempt to lasso it. Graves had a Winchester on board but no cartridges. Fishing parties now have an added attraction presented them of chasing a red handle stick whenever they see it, with the knowledge that a turtle weighing over a ton is anchored on the other end of it.”","2","""RED STICK!—TURTLE!""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","point_vincent","known","2000","9'","not_reported","""the size says 'over a ton', so it is very general estimate, nevertheless is listed as 2000 pounds here. Species uncertain, but given size it could be a leatherback. It also says it had a head like a barrel, perhaps implying a loggerhead?""","1905",8,"high",274.32,1900
-"71","sdut_19081021","""San Diego Union Tribune""",1908-10-21,7,NA,"""https://sandiegouniontribune.newsbank.com/doc/image/v2%3A136E6A0F0DF56B38%40NGPA-CASD-1394A13623FCDE56%402418236-13935C9A723643E1%406-13935C9A723643E1%40?search_terms=turtle&text=turtle&content_added=&date_from=1871&date_to=1973&pub%255B0%255D=136E6A0F0DF56B38&page=8&pdate=1908-10-21""","“Percy Woods and the men who assisted him to land mammoth turtle caught in kelp beds off the harbor, showing size of crustacean as it lay on the beach. \n [image included] \n Battle Four Hours in Attempt To Land Huge Crustacean Weighing Half Ton \n After a four hour battle, lasting from 1 o’clock yesterday afternoon until 5. Percy Woods, a fisherman in the employ of the San Diego fish market. Landed a huge tortoise, weighing half a ton, perhaps the largest ever seen on this coast. When first sighted by Woods, who was lobster fishing off Point Loma, the monster crustacean was entangled in the kelp just outside the harbor and was fighting furiously for its liberty. Every movement entangled it more seriously in the treacherous kelp and had relief not so opportunely offered. it would undoubtedly have succumbed. \n With great effort, the turtle fighting every second, Woods succeeded in passing a rope around its body just behind the front flippers, and then cut loose the kelp twined about the turtle’s head and body. Starting the engine of his boat he then proceeded to tow the captive into port. The turtle resisted, but because of its inability to exert much strength in a backward direction, it was unable to stop the forward motion or the small gasoline schooner. Had Woods fastened the line behind the rear flippers, and attempted to tow the turtle into port backwards, it would easily have been able to turn the tables, probably carrying the fishing schooner to sea. \n As it was, it took Woods four long hours to reach the wharf of the San Diego Fish market, at the foot of D street, with his prize. Here, however, he met with further trouble. Transferring the rope to a small skiff, he thoughtlessly began to row ashore, thinking to tow the monster behind him. Finding that it was to be again put into motion, the turtle resisted and turned around, making for deep water. The skiff was dragged helplessly along, Woods’ efforts to the contrary being in vain. Finally fishermen on the wharf, sizing up the state of affairs, threw Woods a line, making one end fast to the wharf. \n Fights to Last Ditch \n Six or eight men working at the market fastened themselves to the rope, and their combined effort? were necessary to haul the skiff with the struggling turtle to the shore. Here the turtle was dragged out on the beach and walked around to the front of the market. Half a dozen men holding the rope were unable to retard its progress when it desired to continue on its way. the only means of controlling its action being to turn the head and keep the monster walking in a circle. \n The turtle was finally brought to the interior of the market where, before a crowd of curious onlokers, the rope was fastened to a workbench by C. E. Osborn, the manager of the fish market. The turtle, however, was not satisfied with its position, and promptly began dragging bench and all away from the wall to the opposite side of the big room. The rope was rapidly untied and hitched around a post supporting the roof. When this, too, was torn from its position, one of the men got busy and bored two holes in the floor, one on each side of a stout joist. The rope was then passed around this and the monster was finally secured. \n Manager Osborn is undecided what disposition to make of the big reptile, and in the meantime it is occupying the spot light on the floor of the fish market. The turtle measures eight feet in length over all, is forty-two inches across the back, the surface of the upper shell being twenty inches from the ground. It is thought to be of the species known as the loggerhead (capuana carcita), in rare instances seen in this section of the Pacific and easily the largest ever captured on the Southern California coast. It weighs about 1000 pounds. \n Shell Peculiarly Marked \n The shell of the tortoise is a peculiar one, being marked with six rows of protuberations. One of these lines each side of the shell, the others occurring in regular intervals to the center of the shell and running lengthwise, speculation as to the age of the monster is rife. Tortoise have been known to live 100 years or more. and it is believed that the species to which the captured one belongs retains its hold on life for a much longer period. \n One of the rear flaps has been completely cut off, supposedly bitten by a shark. The other rear flap is several feet in length, while the front flaps are fully three feet long. \n The shell of the tortoise is considerably marked and scratched, some apparently of recent date, while others may have been inflicted many years ago.”","2","""FISHERMEN CAPTURE MONSTER TORTOISE""","1","loggerhead","reported","point_loma","known","1000","8'","""entangled in kelp""","""reported as a loggerhead, however, picture is included and could look like a leatherback as well""","1908",10,"high",243.84,1900
-"72","stinson_sdherald_18870603","""San Diego Herald""",1887-06-03,NA,"53","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Mr. W.F. Cabral of Roseville [an early whaling and fishing community of San Diego Bay] caught a turtle weighing 1,200 pounds while fishing off the kelpbeds beyond Point Loma [entrance to Bay] on 1 June 1887. It was caught on an ordinary-sized fishing hook and in endeavoring to pull the turtle into the boat the fisherman was almost drawn overboard. The monster fish was exhibited for several days at Jorre's Wharf before going the way of all sea turtles—toward the tureen.""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","1","leatherback","inferred","point_loma","known","1200","not_reported","""off of kelp beds""",NA,"1887",9,"high",NA,1880
-"73","sbmp_18890801","""Morning Press""",1889-08-01,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP18890801.2.41&srpos=6&e=-------en--20-MP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""S. F. Chronicle. \n A turtle nine feet long, four feet broad, and weighing 2000 pounds, was captured yesterday off Pigeon Point by Antonio Betling. He pursued it from sunrise till noon, and attempted to land it with one boat, which was nearly swamped. \n It finally required two boats and the assistance of eight fishermen to take the prisoner to the wharf. \n Betling and other fishermen say this is the largest turtle ever caught on the Pacific coast. \n The monster shell back was seen by many people yesterday, all of whom were struck with wonder at its prodigious size.""","2","""An Overgrown Turtle""","1","leatherback","inferred","pigeon_point","known","2000","9'","not_reported","""Possible leatherback based on the size. Further descriptions of size include four feet wide""","1889",8,"high",274.32,1880
-"74","sppilot_19360813","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1936-08-13,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19360813.2.4&srpos=18&e=-------en--20-SPNP-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""A giant leatherback turtle, weighing 475 pounds, was brought into San Pedro this morning by the tuna boat Garfield, commanded by Capt. Jerry Acalin. It was caught in tuna nets during the night while the Garfield was fishing off Oceanside. \n Brought into the Independent Fish company’s market at the municipal fish wharf, the turtle was kept alive pending delivery to some aquarium or zoo. It remained very active with its head and flippers when placed on the wet cement floor of the market. Captain Acalin said the leatherback caused considerable damage to his nets. \n Old-time fishermen declare the turtle to be an inhabitant of tropical waters. Its hard shell back is covered with heavy black skin, on which are lodged a number of small white barnacles.""","2","""Giant Turtle Brought Here""","1","leatherback","reported","oceanside","known","475","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be the same observation as sppilot_19360819. the article also interestingly mentions that it has a hard shell, which isn't exactly true of leatherbacks, but it also 'heavy black skin' so likely the description is of a leatherback still.""","1936",9,"high",NA,1930
-"75","sppilot_19360819","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1936-08-19,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19360819.2.20&srpos=36&e=-------en--20-SPNP-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Three miles north of the isthmus of Santa Catalina Island the crew of the San Pedro fishing boat Conte Di Savoia found this giant manta or sea bat swimming lazily along the surface of the sea. Capt. Tony Russo sunk a harpoon and for five hours the big fish fought. He took out 150 fathoms of strong line and sounded many times with five barrel buoys. The manta, when finally hoisted aboard, was found to weigh nearly a ton and to have a spread of 14 feet from the tips of its fins. Shown with the tropical fish in this picture are three members of the Conte Di Savoia’s crew, Joe Lucido, James Lucido and D. S Cline, engineer of the 88-foot fish boat. \n Below is the 475-pound leatherback turtle which Capt. Jerry Acalin and his crew of the San Pedro fishing boat Garfield brought to the Independent Fish Co. dock. He was caught in a tuna net off Oceanside. Shown in the picture with the turtle are Mrs. Dorothy Byrne and Carmine Mineghino. (News-Pilot Photos)""","2","""San Pedrans Land Manta, Turtle""","1","leatherback","reported","oceanside","known","475","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be the same observation and turtle as sppilot_19360813""","1936",9,"high",NA,1930
-"76","sajournal_19360814","""Santa Ana Journal""",1936-08-14,11,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SAJ19360814.2.177&srpos=17&e=-------en--20-SAJ-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""SAN PEDRO. The Fishing Boat Garfield brought to port today a 470-pound leatherback sea turtle captured last night off Oceanside when it became entangled in the boat's nets. \n Such turtles are rarely seen off California. It measured about five and one-half feet by three and one-half feet, which would be large even for tropical waters \n The turtle is being kept alive in the hopes an Aquarium may buy it.""","2","""Boat Makes Surprise Catch""","1","leatherback","reported","oceanside","known","470","5'6""","""entangled in boat's nets""","""the article also mentions its width to be 3.5 (3'6"") feet.""","1936",10,"high",167.64,1930
-"77","spdaily_19251224","""San Pedro Daily News""",1925-12-24,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPDN19251224.2.9&srpos=71&e=-------en--20-SPDN-61--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Total Receipts for November Totaled 13,830,379 Pounds, Report \n Total receipts of fish for the canneries and wholesale markets at San Pedro harbor for the month of November amounted to 13,830,579 pounds, according to the official report of the state fish and game commission, compiled under the direction of H. L. Williamson, in charge of the commercial fisheries department. \n Sardines led the list with 11,837,080 pounds. Yellowfin tuna came next with 781,403 pounds. Skipjack were third, with 495,038 pounds. The catch of barracuda totaled 129,923 pounds, while the mackerel catch was 173,222 pounds. \n The lobster catch was considerable larger than in former seasons. with a total catch of 63,248 pounds. Turtle, with 21 pounds; anchovies, 60 pounds; pompano, 8 pounds; flounders, 54 pounds, and clams, 75 pounds, formed the smallest in the last of 39 varieties of sea food caught.""","2","""SARDINES HEAD LIST OF FISH BROUGHT HERE""","cargo","green","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""The total number of turtles was not reported but the total weight of the cargo was just 21 pounds; additionally, no exact location was reported, but perhaps we can infer san pedro area, because that is where the newspaper is from""","1925",5,"medium",NA,1920
-"78","sppilot_19190327","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1919-03-27,1,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19190327.2.16&srpos=190&e=-------en--20-SPNP-181--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Three fishing vessels are now being fitted with proper equipment for catching Mexican turtles, and it is reported that several canning concerns are preparing to handle their catches. \n The turtles will be brought from Mexican waters, about a day and a half run from the harbor. Last year several tons of turtles were cared for, but the industry did not become established. \n Soup and canned turtle meat have heretofore come mostly from San Diego.""","1","""Going to Mexico for Turtles for Canning""","cargo","green","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","not_reported","""This article is very ambiguously written/worded, and location of capture is unclear whether it is san_diego or baja_california""","1919",4,"medium",NA,1910
-"79","stinson_sdherald_18720104","""San Diego Herald""",1872-01-04,NA,"51","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""In giving the shipments of merchandise by the steamer Orizaba on Tuesday we neglected to make a note of a small lot of green turtle which went up. The shipment in point of numbers was light, but in pounds it looks well. One of the last lot shipped, weighed 103 pounds. During the remainder of the winter, we are told, regular shipments of the green turtle will be made to San Francisco.""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","cargo","green","reported","not_reported","not_reported","103","not_reported","not_reported","""no location is given, but likely can presume baja_california, base don context within the thesis.""","1872",6,"medium",NA,1870
-"80","stinson_sdunion_19320825","""San Diego Union""",1932-08-25,NA,"57","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""On her second visit here with a similar cargo in the last few months, the vessel Mexico, Peter Koralish, skipper, was in port yesterday with a quantity of live turtles for the local market. \n Weighing anywhere from 85-150 pounds each, the turtles numbering about 60, will bring if sold here, about six or seven cents per pound 'on the hoof' according to reports along the waterfront. The heavy shell, it was said, causes a loss of between 60 and 70 percent in the net value of the turtles to the buyer. \n No market for the turtles had been found here yesterday, according to an official of one of the large fish companies, and in the event that the turtles cannot be disposed of either here or in San Pedro, the opinion was expressed that the cargo may be taken south and dried.""","2","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","60","green","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","150","not_reported","not_reported","""the location is not reported but based on context, it is likely in or around baja_california""","1932",6,"medium",NA,1930
-"81","sppilot_19251225","""San Pedro News Pilot""",1925-12-25,7,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SPNP19251225.2.71&srpos=106&e=-------en--20-SPNP-101--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""11,837,080 Pounds of Sardines Are Delivered \n Sardines formed more than 90 per cent of fish catches brought into San Pedro and Wilmington during the month of November, according to a report issued on Thursday by the Terminal Island office of the California Fish and Game Commission. The report was compiled by Deputy Commissioner H. L. Williamson. \n During the month a total of 13,830,579 pounds of fish of 37 varieties were delivered to local canneries and markets. Of this amount, 11,837,080 pounds were sardines. The canning season for this fish is not at its height. \n A feature of the monthly report was the fact that 63,248 pounds of lobsters were brought in. Also, that one turtle, weighing 21 pounds was sold at a local market. \n Following is the complete report of local fish deliveries for the month of November: Species Amount \n Abalone 802 \n Albacore 14,127 \n Anchovies 60 \n Barracuda 129,923 \n Black Cod 190 \n Bonita 13,020 \n Cabrilla 120 \n Clams 75 \n Flounders 54 \n Grunion 598 \n Halfmoon 4,185 \n Halibut 16,344 \n Kingfish 50,506 \n Lobster 63,248 \n Mackerel 173,222 \n Mixed Fish 5,552 \n Perch 6,807 \n Pompano 8 \n Rock Bass 2,179 \n Rock Fish 97,288 \n Sardines 11,837,080 \n Sculpin 1,979 \n Sea Bass, Black 1,310 \n Sea Bass, White 11,199 \n Shark 1,654 \n Sheepshead 3,897 \n Skate 2,688 \n Skipjack 495,038 \n Smelt 61,592 \n Sole 288 \n Swordfish 405 \n Tuna, Unclassified 1,724 \n Tuna, Blue Fin 7,100 \n Tuna, Yellow Fin 781,403 \n Turtle 21 \n Whitefish 15,699 \n Yellow Tail 29,194 \n Total pounds 13,830,579""","2","""FISH CATCHES IN NOVEMBER AT HIGH MARK""","1","green","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","21","not_reported","not_reported","""The location is unclear because this is just a report of all of the fish that came in the month of November at the local port in San Pedro and Wilmington. There is not information about where these fish were captured.""","1925",6,"medium",NA,1920
-"82","cejournal_19530702","""Coronado Eagle and Journal""",1953-07-02,11,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=CJ19530702.2.114&srpos=47&e=--1846---1973--en--20-CJ-41--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""Coronado’s fishing mayor, Lloyd Harmon, was one of the eight prize winners in San Diego Yellowtail Derby competition last week and one of the top individual performers among derby aspirants as well. \n Mayor Harmon landed in eighth place on the prize list with a 24 pound 4 ouncer, but his prize winning yellow proved the largest of 364 taken June 22. \n Although one day yellowtail catches soared as high as 501 during the week, a 1000-pound leatherback turtle run down and gaffed by a party of anglers aboard the Red Wing was the chief topic of conversation.""","2","""Harmon Takes Fish Derby Spot""","1","leatherback","reported","not_reported","not_reported","1000","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be the same turtle as natcitysn_19530703 (redundant). Location is not specified, but is likely near Coronado/San Diego area, given that this is where the article takes place.""","1953",7,"high",NA,1950
-"83","natcitysn_19530703","""National City Star-News""",1953-07-03,NA,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=NCSN19530703.2.56&srpos=97&e=-------en--20-NCSN-81--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","""A sudden flareup of dirty water slowed up yellowtail fishing at the Coronado Islands last weekend, but it appeared to be clearing rapidly early this week and good fishing for the July 4 weekend appeared to be in store. \n When the slow-down occurred at the Islands, the La Jolla and Point Loma kelp beds quickly took up the slack, and anglers had little difficulty fattening their sacks with barracuda, and calico bass, plus some white sea bass and yellows. \n Among National City anglers who switched to La Jolla and hit the jackpot were V. C. Daniel, who limited out on barracuda, and G. Tweed and L. Tweed. Charles Phillips stuck to the Coronado Islands through thick and thin and was rewarded with a 22 pound 5 ounce yellow. \n Before the dirty water moved in, anglers made daily catches of from 258 to 501 yellows, and veteran rod and reelers expected similar catches once conditions are back to normal. \n Most unusual catch of the week was the taking of a 1000 pound leatherback turtle by anglers aboard the Red Wing last Friday. First take of albacore, 18 of them, was made the same day by Ray Ward and Ben Burch, aboard the Channel Clipper, one hour south of the Coronado Islands.""","2","""'Dirty Water' Forces Anglers to Switch Areas""","1","leatherback","reported","not_reported","not_reported","1000","not_reported","not_reported","""This appears to be reporting the same turtle as cejournal_19530702 (redundant). Location unspecified but potentially around the La Jolla/Coronado Islands area.""","1953",7,"high",NA,1950
-"84","stinson_sdunion_19020720","""San Diego Union and Daily Bee""",1902-07-20,NA,"53","""https://georgehbalazs.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Stinson_1984_BiologyofSeaTurtlesSanDiegoBayCAinNEPacOcean_Thesis_SDSU.pdf""","""Shipping Big Turtles. A 300 pound turtle was shipped to Los Angeles yesterday through Wells Fargo Express by the San Diego Fish Company. A number of these turtles have been sent out of San Diego during the past week or two.""","1","""Historic and Economic Importance of Sea Turtles to San Diego""","1","green","inferred","not_reported","not_reported","300","not_reported","not_reported","""this article in particular refers to one turtle that was shipped, however it also mentions a few other turtles that were caught and shipped elsewhere in the same manner""","1902",5,"medium",NA,1900
-"85","slotribune_18840509","""San Luis Obispo Tribune""",1884-05-09,4,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=SLOTW18840509.2.18&srpos=9&e=-------en--20-SLOTW-1--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","“While at Monterey on Friday last we saw a monster sea turtle that had been caught that day in the bay by some fishermen. The turtle appeared to be fully seven feet in length from nose to end of tail and with four long flippers, like wings, giving it a breadth of about the same. The monster was estimated to weigh 500 or 600 pounds. The captors expressed the intention of selling it for exhibition in Woodwards Garden in San Francisco.”","1","""Monster Turtle""","1","possible_leatherback","inferred","monterey_bay","known","600","7'","not_reported","""Inferred to be a leatherback based on size. This may potentially be the same event/observation as sbmp_18840509 based on location and time""","1884",7,"high",213.36,1880
-"86","sbmp_18840509","""Morning Press""",1884-05-09,3,NA,"""https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=MP18840509.2.12&srpos=33&e=-------en--20-MP-21--txt-txIN-turtle+NOT+story+NOT+florida+NOT+jersey+NOT+bangor+NOT+cuba+NOT+jamaica+NOT+chicago+NOT+snapping+NOT+turn+NOT+turned+NOT+turns+NOT+turning+NOT+deck+NOT+dove+NOT+doves+NOT+house+NOT+pond+NOT+lake+NOT+York+NOT+desert+NOT+race+NOT+school+NOT+neck+NOT+mock+NOT+necks-------""","“Friday while fishing in the bay on Monterey about five miles off shore, one of the boats of F. Nocefore & Co., saw a large turtle floating on the water. They succeeded in catching it with a gaff, and then securely tied it with ropes. Nine men tried to pull it into a boat, but were unable to move it. Three boats were required to tow it to Monterey. It is seven feet seven inches in length, four feet across the back, and has two fins three feet long. It is known as a humpback. —Santa Cruz Surf.”","2","""A Monster Turtle""","1","leatherback","inferred","monterey_bay","known","not_reported","7'7""","""floating on the water""","""The article refers to the species as a humpback—they likely are referring to it as a leatherback based on size. This may be the same event/observation (redundant) as slotribune_18840509 based on location and time. It also further specifies the size to be 4 feet in width and has 3 foot fins""","1884",9,"high",231.14,1880
